> no.\n- 24: 2 even,4 even => no.\n- 25: 2 even,5 odd => equal, 25%2=1 => no.\n- 26: 2 even,6 even => no.\n- 27: 2 even,7 odd => equal, 27%2=1 => no.\n- 28: both even => no.\n- 29: 2 even,9 odd => equal, 29%2=1 => no.\n- 30: 3 odd,0 even => equal, 30%2=0 => yes => count.\n- 31: 3 odd,1 odd => no.\n- 32: 3 odd,2 even => equal, 32%2=0 => yes.\n- 33: both odd => no.\n- 34: 3 odd,4 even => equal, 34%2=0 => yes.\n- 35: both odd => no.\n- 36: 3 odd,6 even => equal, 36%2=0 => yes.\n- 37: both odd => no.\n- 38: 3 odd,8 even => equal, 38%2=0 => yes.\n- 39: both odd => no.\n- 40: 4 even,0 even => no.\n- 41: 4 even,1 odd => equal, 41%2=1 => no.\n- 42: 4 even,2 even => no.\n- 43: 4 even,3 odd => equal, 43%2=1 => no.\n- 44: both even => no.\n- 45: both odd => no.\n- 46: 4 even,6 even => no.\n- 47: 4 even,7 odd => equal, 47%2=1 => no.\n- 48: both even => no.\n- 49: 4 even,9 odd => equal, 49%2=1 => no.\n- 50: 5 odd,0 even => equal, 50%2=0 => yes.\n\nSo beautiful numbers divisible by 2 in [19,50] are: 30,32,34,36,38,50 => 6 numbers. So the expected 6 is correct. So the second assert expecting 14 is likely erroneous. So maybe they intended a different k, maybe 3? Let's test (19,50,3) manually.\n\nCheck numbers that are beautiful (equal ev/odd) and divisible by 3: from above list of beautiful numbers (equal ev/odd) we have: 21,23,25,27,29,30,32,34,36,38,41,43,47,49? Wait many not divisible by 3. Let's list all beautiful numbers (equal ev/odd) between 19 and 50: numbers where digits one even and one odd: those with two digits where one digit is even and the other odd. All two-digit numbers where one digit even, other odd. But need to be within range. Let's enumerate from 19 to 50 inclusive:\n\n19 (odd-odd) no.\n20 (even-even) no.\n21 (even-odd) yes.\n22 (even-even) no.\n23 (even-odd) yes.\n24 (even-even) no.\n25 (even-odd) yes.\n26 (even-even) no.\n27 (even-odd) yes.\n28 (even-even) no.\n29 (even-odd) yes.\n30 (odd-even) yes.\n31 (odd-odd) no.\n32 (odd-even) yes.\n33 (odd-odd) no.\n34