t numbers from 0 to N inclusive with each digit <= limit.\n\nEdge case: p=0 is counted (digits all zero). That's okay.\n\nThus we can implement f(N) via DP.\n\nBut the DP for each call may be expensive if we call many times (like each test case calls count_powerful_integers only once). So it's fine.\n\nPotential large N length up to maybe 19 digits (since finish up to ~5e14? Actually test includes finish up to 5e14 ~ 5*10^14, that's 15 digits. But p_max may have up to maybe 15 digits if s is short. So DP with up to 15-20 digits is trivial.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n1. Parse s: suffix = int(s)\n2. If any digit in s > limit: return 0 (since no such integer can have that suffix because suffix's digits exceed limit)\n3. Compute B = 10^len(s)\n4. Compute p_min = max(0, ceil_div(start - suffix, B)). Ceil division for possibly negative numbers: ceil_div(a,b) = (a + b - 1)//b if a>0 else a // b (since negative division floor in Python is towards negative infinity). Safer to implement: p_min = (start - suffix + B - 1) // B if start > suffix else 0? Let's test: if start <= suffix, then any p >=0 yields x >= suffix >= start? Actually if start <= suffix, then p=0 yields x = suffix which is >= start, so p_min = 0. But p could be negative? Not allowed. So p_min = max(0, (start - suffix + B - 1)//B). But if start-suffix is negative, then integer division (negative + B -1) // B might be negative or zero? Example start=5, suffix=10, start-suffix=-5, B=1000 (len(s)=3). Then ( -5 + 1000 -1 ) // 1000 = 994 // 1000 = 0, so p_min = max(0,0) = 0, correct. So that formula works.\n\nBut careful: if start - suffix is negative but large magnitude (like -10000) and B=1000, then ( -10000 + 1000 - 1 ) // 1000 = (-9001)//1000 = -10? Actually integer division floors toward negative infinity. Let's compute: -9001 // 1000 = -10 (since -10*1000 = -10000 <= -9001). So p_min = max(0, -10) = 0, which is correct because p=0 yields x= suffix >= start? Actually if suffix=10, start = -9990? Wait start is positi