answer.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- We'll use a dict or set of tuples (sum, product, parity). Since we need to iterate over dp to generate new states, using a set is fine.\n\n- For each number x in nums:\n    newdp = set(dp)  # skip\n    # extend from empty\n    new_product = x\n    if new_product <= limit:\n        new_sum = x  # parity=0 (even)\n        new_parity = 1  # next index odd\n        newdp.add((new_sum, new_product, new_parity))\n    # extend from each existing state\n    for s,p,par in dp:\n        new_product = p * x\n        if new_product > limit:\n            continue\n        if par == 0:\n            ns = s + x\n        else:\n            ns = s - x\n        new_par = 1 - par\n        newdp.add((ns, new_product, new_par))\n    dp = newdp\n\nPotential improvement: we need to consider x=0 case: new_product = p * 0 = 0. That's <= limit always (unless limit < 0, but limit >=1). So all states can be extended with product=0. That might generate many states with product=0 for many sum values. But sum unchanged because adding 0 doesn't affect sum. So we may get many duplicates: (s, 0, new_par) for each s. Many sum values possible. But number of sum values is limited. So okay.\n\nNow after DP, we need to find max product among dp where sum == k. But note parity does not matter for final alternating sum (alternating sum defined for subsequence as sum over even positions minus odd positions). The parity of length matters only for sum calculation; final sum is stored. So we just need any parity.\n\nThus:\n\nbest = -1\nfor s,p,par in dp:\n    if s == k and p <= limit:\n        if p > best:\n            best = p\n\nIf best remains -1, return -1.\n\nBut there is a nuance: dp contains only non-empty subsequences. So we are safe.\n\nEdge Cases: Some numbers may be > limit individually; we should still consider them? If x > limit, product = x > limit, can't be in dp. So those numbers cannot be taken as single element subsequence (or as part of any subsequence). That's fine.\n\nBut we must also consider that product may exceed limit due to multiplication, but we skip those states.\n\nNow we need to ensure that dp's size doesn't explode due to product=0 states for each parity for each sum. For x=0, we may generate both parity states for same sum. That's okay.\n\nNow test with given examples.\n\nLet's manually test some:\n\nExample1: nums=[1,2,3], k=2, limit=10.\n\nWe need subsequence with alternating sum = 2 and max product <=10.\n\nLet's enumerate: possible subsequences:\n- [1] sum=1, product=1\n- [2] sum=2, product=2 (valid)\n- [3] sum=3, product=3\n- [1,2] sum=1-2=-1 product=2\n- [1,3] sum=1-3=-2 product=3\n- [2,3] sum=2-3=-1 product=6\n- [1,2,3] sum=1-2+3=2 product=6 (valid)\nThus max product 6 <=10 => answer 6. DP should find that.\n\nNow test: dp initial empty. Process 1:\n- from empty: new_sum=1, product=1, parity=1. dp = {(1,1,1)}.\nProcess 2:\n- from empty: sum=2, product=2, parity=1 -> add.\n- from (1,1,1): parity=1 => odd position, sum = 1 - 2 = -1, product = 2, parity=0. Add (-1,2,0).\ndp after 2: {(1,1,1), (2,2,1), (-1,2,0)}.\nProcess 3:\n- from empty: sum=3, product=3, parity=1.\n- from (1,1,1): parity=1 => sum = 1-3=-2, product=3, parity=0.\n- from (2,2,1): sum = 2-3=-1, product=6, parity=0.\n- from (-1,2,0): parity=0 => sum = -1+3=2, product=6, parity=1.\nThus dp includes (2,6,1). So best sum=2 product=6. Good.\n\nNow test example [0,2,3], k=-5 limit=12. DP will include product=0 states etc. But no sum -5 reachable. So answer -1.\n\nThird example: [2,2,3,3] k=0 limit=9. Let's find best product <=9 sum=0. Potential subsequence [3,3] sum=3-3=0 product=9 => answer 9. DP should find.\n\nNow test single element tests.\n\n- [11] k=11 limit=20 -> product 11, sum 11 => answer 11.\n- [11] k=11 limit=10 -> product 11 > limit, cannot take, answer -1.\n- [0] k=0 limit=10 -> product 0, sum 0 => answer 0. dp from empty: sum=0, product=0? Wait taking 0 from empty: product = 0, sum = 0, parity=1. So dp includes (0,0,1). So