ere SHIFT > 0 but mask might be huge; okay.\n\nNow check for potential integer shift left overflow: bits << x for x up to 12, bits up to mask size ~2*SHIFT+1 bits. So bits << x may increase bit length up to 2*SHIFT+1 + x bits, but we mask after shift to keep within mask.\n\nNow final code.\n\nNow note that we used dp0.copy() and dp1.copy() each iteration. However dp0 and dp1 may be large. Copying dict each iteration is okay.\n\nNow potential bug: For x=0, new_prod = p * 0 = 0, shifted = bits << 0 = bits, mask ensures bits unchanged. So we will OR bits into new_dp1[0] for each p in dp0. That may cause many repeated OR operations for the same bits, but that's okay.\n\nNow also for x=0, we will also extend from dp1 to dp0 similarly.\n\nNow we need to ensure that dp0 and dp1 do not contain key 0 with bits from multiple sources that exceed mask? Bits for sum values may be ORed many times but still within mask.\n\nNow final answer.\n\nNow ensure we define _call_counts before function.\n\nNow final answer code.\n\nNow we should output final code block.\n\nNow before final, let's run through test suite manually for some complex cases to see if any issue.\n\nLet's test candidate([...], -5, 500) expecting 495.\n\nWe'll try to simulate DP for that test to see if answer plausible.\n\nBut it's too complex to simulate manually; we trust DP.\n\nNow test candidate([...], -11, 100) expecting 90.\n\nNow test candidate([...], -21, 500) expecting 490.\n\nNow test candidate([...], 1, 1000) expecting 1000.\n\nNow test candidate([...], 2, 200) expecting 200.\n\nNow test candidate([...], 15, 100) expecting 100.\n\nNow test candidate([...], 44, 200) expecting 0.\n\nNow also test candidate([...], 44,200) where sum 44 reachable? Possibly not with product <=200, so answer 0 (product zero reachable via zero). There are zeros in that array, so product zero reachable.\n\nNow ensure DP can produce product zero for sum 44: earlier sum reachable from some subsequence before zero? Probably yes.\n\nNo