be borderline but okay? However actual reachable states may be far fewer due to constraints. Could be okay for n=150.\n\nBut we need to be careful about performance.\n\nAlternative: Use DP over sum and product only, ignoring parity? Actually parity influences sign of contribution, but we can incorporate parity as part of DP dimension.\n\nThus DP dimension: parity (2) * sum offset (max_range) * product (limit+1). Represent as boolean 3D array maybe using bitset for product dimension. But we can compress product dimension using Python's bitarray? Not needed.\n\nSimplify using set-of-tuples.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef max_product_subsequence(nums, k, limit):\n    max_sum = sum(nums)  # max possible absolute sum\n    offset = max_sum\n    # dp[parity] = dict sum->set(product)\n    dp = [dict(), dict()]  # parity 0,1\n    for x in nums:\n        # new dp states after processing x\n        new_dp = [dict(dp[0]), dict(dp[1])]  # copy existing states for skip case\n        # start new subsequence with just x\n        if x <= limit:\n            parity = 1  # after taking one element (odd length)\n            sum_val = x\n            prod_val = x\n            # insert into new_dp[parity]\n            d = new_dp[parity]\n            if sum_val not in d:\n                d[sum_val] = set()\n            d[sum_val].add(prod_val)\n        # extend existing subsequences\n        for parity in (0,1):\n            for sum_val, prod_set in dp[parity].items():\n                # taking x\n                new_parity = 1 - parity\n                # compute new sum\n                if parity == 0:\n                    new_sum = sum_val + x\n                else:\n                    new_sum = sum_val - x\n                # product\n                for prod in prod_set:\n                    new_prod = prod * x\n                    if new_prod <= limit:\n                        d = new_dp[new_parity]\n                        if new_sum not in d:\n                            d[new_sum] = set()\n    