we just store max product for each sum.\n\nWhen extending, we need to compute new product = old_product * x, if <= limit. Then for new_sum, we compare with existing dp[new_parity][new_sum] and store max.\n\nAlso we need to consider starting new subsequence: we can treat dp[1][x] = max(dp[1].get(x, -inf), x) if x <= limit.\n\nThus dp can be dict mapping sum->max product.\n\nComplexities: At each step, for each parity and each sum entry we compute new product. Number of sum entries maybe up to 3601. So each iteration O(2*3600) ~ 7200 operations. Over 150 steps ~ 1M operations, fine.\n\nThus algorithm: DP with two dicts for parity 0 and 1 storing best product per sum.\n\nImplementation details:\n\nInitialize dp_even = {} (parity 0: length even). dp_odd = {}.\n\nLoop over each x in nums:\n\n- new_even = dp_even.copy()\n- new_odd = dp_odd.copy()\n\n- Starting new subsequence: if x <= limit:\n    new_sum = x (since first index is even)\n    new_parity = 1 (odd length)\n    if new_sum not in new_odd or x > new_odd[new_sum]: new_odd[new_sum] = x\n\n- Extend existing states:\n\nFor each sum_val, prod in dp_even items:\n    # parity even (length%2==0) -> next index is even (since length even, next index is even)\n    # Actually if length%2==0, next element will be at even index (0-indexed) of subsequence, which adds to sum.\n    new_sum = sum_val + x\n    new_prod = prod * x\n    if new_prod <= limit:\n        # new parity becomes odd\n        if new_sum not in new_odd or new_prod > new_odd[new_sum]:\n            new_odd[new_sum] = new_prod\n\nFor each sum_val, prod in dp_odd items:\n    # length%2==1, next index is odd, subtract from sum.\n    new_sum = sum_val - x\n    new_prod = prod * x\n    if new_prod <= limit:\n        # new parity becomes even\n        if new_sum not in new_even or new_prod > new_even[new_sum]:\n            new_even[new_sum] = new_prod\n\nNow after processing all numbers, we need to consider any sum==k in either parity dict. Return max product among dp_even.get(k) and dp_odd.get(k) if present else -1.\n\nEdge Cases:\n\n- Zero product: If x=0, new_prod = prod*0 = 0. So we may store product=0 for new sum. That's allowed.\n\n- Starting subsequence with zero: sum=0 (since 0 added). product=0. That yields sum 0 and product 0. However note that subsequence must be non-empty, and zero product may be best if k=0 and limit >=0 (limit >=1). But we need to consider that product zero might be best if no other product <= limit yields sum k. However they might want maximum product, and zero is allowed. In test case, they have candidate([...], 44, 200) == 0. That indicates there's a subsequence achieving sum 44 with product zero (likely includes a zero). So we must correctly handle zero.\n\n- Starting new subsequence with zero: sum=0, product=0. This should be included.\n\nNow check test cases.\n\nLet's manually test some.\n\nTest 1: nums=[1,2,3], k=2, limit=10. Let's compute manually: subsequence maybe [1,2] => alternating sum: 1 - 2 = -1 not 2. [1,3] => 1 - 3 = -2. [2,3] => 2 - 3 = -1. [1,2,3] => 1 - 2 + 3 = 2 (good). product = 1*2*3=6 <=10. So answer 6. DP should find.\n\nTest 2: [0,2,3], k=-5, limit=12. Need sum -5. Since numbers are non-negative, alternating sum cannot be negative beyond subtracting bigger numbers? Let's see possible subsequence: [2,3] => 2 - 3 = -1. [0,2,3] => 0 - 2 + 3 = 1. [3] => 3. So no -5. So -1.\n\nTest 3: [2,2,3,3], k=0, limit=9. Subsequence maybe [3,3] => 3-3=0 product=9 <=9. So answer 9. DP should get.\n\nTest single element: [11], k=11, limit=20 => product 11. DP: start new subsequence with 11, sum=11, product=11. parity odd. dp_odd[11]=11. Return max 11.\n\n[4], k=4, limit=10 => product 4.\n\n[1], k=1 => product 1.\n\n[0], k=0 => product 0.\n\n[11], k=11, limit=10 => can't include because product > limit? Actually product=11 > limit=10, so no subsequence. Should return -1.\n\nLarge array with all same elements: [12]*150, k=0, limit=5000 => need sum 0. Could use subsequence length 2 with both 1