r using numbers from {0,...,12}. Multiplying by 0 yields 0. Multiplying by 1 yields same product. So product 1 is reachable via subsequence of any number of 1's (if any 1 present). Many numbers are present in random arrays (including 1). So product 1 reachable. Product 2 reachable via [2] or [1,2] etc. Similarly many numbers reachable. The set of reachable products is basically all numbers that can be factored using primes up to 11 (since numbers up to 12 factor into primes 2,3,5,7,11). So many numbers up to 5000 may be reachable. Let's roughly estimate number of reachable products up to 5000. The count of numbers <=5000 with prime factors only among {2,3,5,7,11} (including 0). That's maybe a few hundred? Actually numbers like 13,17,19 etc not reachable. So reachable set is all smooth numbers up to 5000 (5-smooth numbers include 2,3,5 only; with 7 and 11 also). So count maybe around a few hundred. So DP dict size maybe a few hundred per parity. So overall DP operations ~150*400 ~60k, each shift on big ints of size ~3600 bits. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to implement DP carefully.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Compute sum_all = sum(nums).\n\n- offset = sum_all.\n\n- total_range = 2*offset + 1. If offset = 0 (all zeros), total_range = 1. mask = (1 << total_range) - 1.\n\n- Initialize dp = [dict(), dict()] (parity 0 and 1). dp[0] for even length subsequences, dp[1] for odd length.\n\n- For each v in nums:\n\n   - new_dp0 = dp[0].copy()\n   - new_dp1 = dp[1].copy()\n\n   - if v <= limit: start new subsequence (length 1 odd parity):\n\n        prod = v\n        if prod <= limit: (v is <= limit, check)\n            bit_pos = v + offset\n            if bit_pos >=0 and bit_pos < total_range: (always true)\n            bit = 1 << bit_pos\n            # OR into new_dp1\n            if prod in new_dp1: new_dp1[prod] |= bit else new_dp1[prod] = bit\n\n   - For each parity p and dp_par in enumerate(dp):\n\n        For each prod, bits in dp_par.items():\n\n            if v == 0