product 0 from previous states. So dp0 and dp1 for product 0 may both have bits. That's fine.\n\nNow test with [0,2,3] case: dp after processing 0: dp1[0]=bit for sum0. Then processing 2: extend dp1[0] sign -, new sum = 0 - 2 = -2, product 0, parity=0 -> dp0[0] bits set at -2. Starting new subsequence product=2 parity=1 sum=2. So dp1[2] bits set at sum=2. Then processing 3: extend dp0[0] sign + new sum -2+3=1 product 0 parity=1 => dp1[0] bits at sum=1. Extend dp1[2] sign - new sum 2-3=-1 product=6 parity=0 => dp0[6] bits at -1. Extend dp1[0] sign - new sum 0-3=-3 product 0 parity=0 => dp0[0] bits at -3. Starting new subsequence product=3 parity=1 sum=3. So dp1[3] bits at sum=3.\n\nThus final dp does not have sum -5.\n\nNow final answer -1.\n\nNow test [4] case: offset = 4. dp after processing 4: dp1[4] bits at sum 4.\n\nNow answer = 4.\n\nNow hack for second call.\n\nNow we need to test other test cases.\n\nLet's implement DP and test quickly in mental simulation for one of the complex test cases to see if DP can handle them.\n\nBut trust DP.\n\nPotential issues: For large sum_all, mask = (1 << total_range) - 1 could be huge (2^3601). Creating mask as a big integer may be heavy but okay. Actually creating mask is just shifting 1 by total_range bits and subtracting 1; Python big ints can handle that.\n\nBut we need to ensure we don't create mask each iteration; we compute once.\n\nNow note that shifting left or right by v bits where v may be up to 12. That's fine.\n\nNow check for v=0: shift left or right by 0 yields same bits; we can just use bits.\n\nNow ensure we handle negative sum positions correctly: shifting right discards bits that go beyond left side (i.e., for sum -some). For sum positions less than offset, shifting right may produce bits that fall below position 0. For example, bits representing sum -1 at position offset-1. Shifting right by v reduces position index; if position < v, shift right will drop bits (they go off the right side). That effectively