emaining counts of odds and evens. But given the pattern, the parity of each future position is known: it alternates. So at each step, the number of positions left for odds and evens is determined. Let's denote we have positions i+1..n (remaining positions). The parity of position i+1 is opposite of parity of last placed element. So we can compute how many of those positions will be odd vs even. However, the numbers of odds left must exactly equal the number of odd positions left. If not, there are no completions.\n\nThus the count is factorial(remaining_odds) * factorial(remaining_evens) only if the parity of positions left matches the counts. Actually the number of ways to assign the remaining odd numbers to odd positions and even numbers to even positions (according to the pattern) is factorial(remaining_odds) * factorial(remaining_evens). Because we can permute the odds arbitrarily among the odd positions, and evens arbitrarily among the even positions.\n\nThus we can compute the number of completions quickly.\n\nTherefore we can implement a greedy algorithm: iterate positions 1..n, at each step consider all remaining numbers sorted ascending, filter those that satisfy parity requirement (if any). For each candidate x, compute the number of completions if we choose x now. If k <= count, we pick x and continue; else subtract count and continue to next candidate.\n\nWe need to be careful with large factorial values: n up to 100, factorial(50) ~ 3e64 > 1e15. So we need to cap counts at > k (1e15). Since we only need to compare with k (max 1e15), we can compute factorials up to 100 but cap at >1e15 to avoid overflow. Use Python's big ints, but we can also cap.\n\nThus the algorithm is straightforward and O(n^2) per call.\n\nEdge cases: For odd n, only odd-start pattern allowed. So if we start with an even number, then count will be zero (since pattern cannot complete). So we must ensure that we only consider candidates that lead to a feasible pattern.\n\nBetter to implement a function that given the set of remaining numbers (list of odds, evens) and the parity of the next position (odd/even), returns number of completions = factorial(remaining_odds) * factorial(remaining_evens) if the counts match pattern; else 0.\n\nBut we can compute directly: If next position expects odd, we need remaining_odds >= 1; after placing an odd, the parity flips for next position, etc. However, we need to ensure that the number of odd positions left equals remaining_odds, else impossible.\n\nSimplify: At each step, we know the parity of positions for the rest of the permutation. But we can compute if it's possible by checking the parity pattern length. Let's define:\n\nLet total positions left after placing candidate = r = n - pos (where pos is 1-indexed after placing candidate). Let remaining_odds = O, remaining_evens = E.\n\nWe know that the parity of the position pos (the one we just placed) is known. Then the parity of position pos+1 is opposite. So the pattern of parity for positions pos+1..n is alternating starting with opposite of parity_of_last.\n\nThus we can compute number of odd positions among remaining positions as:\n\nIf next_parity = odd (i.e., the position pos+1 expects odd), then number of odd positions left = ceil(r/2). Because if we have r positions starting with odd, the odd positions count is ceil(r/2). Similarly, number of even positions left = floor(r/2).\n\nIf next_parity = even, then number of odd positions left = floor(r/2), even positions left = ceil(r/2).\n\nThus the condition for feasibility is that O == odd_positions_left and E == even_positions_left. Actually O and E must be exactly those counts, because each odd number must occupy an odd position, each even must occupy an even position.\n\nThus completions count = factorial(O) * factorial(E) if O and E match those counts, else 0.\n\nWe can compute factorials precomputed up to 100, but cap at > 10^15 (like INF = 10**18). We can use Python's big ints and compare directly with k (which is <= 1e15). Even if factorial values exceed that, they will be big ints; subtraction is fine.\n\nThus the algorithm will produce correct results for all inputs, not just test cases. Complexity: O(n^2) per call, which is fine for n=100.\n\nBut note: For large n and k up to 10^15, the factorial of O and E can be huge, but we only need to compare to k. We can early exit if factorial > k.\n\nBut computing factorials up to 50! is fine.\n\nThus we can implement the algorithm.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The lexicographic order is standard based on integer values, not parity pattern. So we need to iterate candidates in sorted order.\n\nNow test the sample given: For n=4 (odds:1,3; evens:2,4). n even => both patterns possible: odd-start pattern (odd positions at indices 1,3) and even-start pattern (odd positions at indices 2,4). The total alternating permutations count = 2 * (2! * 2!) = 8. Let's enumerate lexicographically:\n\nAll permutations of 1..4 sorted lexicographically: [1,2,3,4], [1,2,4,3], [1,3,2,4], [1,3,4,2], [1,4,2,3], [1,4,3,2], [2,1,3,4], [2,1,4,3], [2,3,1,4], [2,3,4,1], [2,4,1,3], [2,4,3,1], [3,1,2,4], [3,1,4,2], [3,2,1,4], [3,2,4,1], [3,4,1,2], [3,4,2,1], [4,1,2,3], [4,1,3,2], [4,2,1,3], [4,2,3,1], [4,3,1,2], [4,3,2,1]. Among those, which are alternating? Let's find those that have alternating parity. For n=4, patterns possible: odd-start: positions 1,3 odd; 2,4 even. So odd numbers must be at positions 1 and 3; evens at positions 2 and 4. So permutations: [1,2,3,4], [1,4,3,2], [3,2,1,4], [3,4,1,2]. That's 4 permutations. Even-start: odd numbers at positions 2,4; evens at positions 1,3. So permutations: [2,1,4,3], [2,3,4,1], [4,1,2,3], [4,3,2,1]. That's 4 more. So total 8.\n\nSorted lexicographically: Let's list all 8 sorted:\n\n1. [1,2,3,4]\n2. [1,4,3,2]\n3. [2,1,4,3]\n4. [2,3,4,1]\n5. [3,2,1,4]\n6. [3,4,1,2]\n7. [4,1,2,3]\n8. [4,3,2,1]\n\nThus the 6th permutation is [3,4,1,2] which matches the example: kth_alternating_permutation(4,6) => [3,4,1,2]. Good.\n\nNow for n=3 (odd). odds:1,3; evens:2. Only odd-start pattern (odd positions 1,3). So odd numbers must be at positions 1 and 3; even at position 2. So permutations: [1,2,3] and [3,2,1]. Sorted lexicographically: [1,2,3], [3,2,1]. So k=2 => [3,2,1] matches example.\n\nNow for n=2: odds:1; evens:2. n even => both patterns possible. So permutations: odd-start: [1,2]; even-start: [2,1]. Sorted lexicographically: [1,2], [2,1]. So k=3 => out of range => [].\n\nNow for n=20, k=26336378880001 => expected [] (?), earlier they have test: assert candidate(20,26336378880001) == [] and also earlier they have same n but expected [1] for some other test. Wait they have:\n\n```\nassert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == [1]\n...\nassert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == []\n```\n\nLet's examine. In the earlier test cases (additional test cases) they have:\n\n```\nassert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == [1]\n```\n\nThen later in the large test cases they have:\n\n```\nassert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == []\n```\n\nThus contradictory. Wait first set: \"Additional test cases\" includes:\n\n```\nassert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == [1]\n```\n\nLater \"Larger test cases\" includes:\n\n```\nassert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == []\n```\n\nThus contradictory. Let's re-express the full test suite:\n\n```\ndef check(candidate):\n    # Test cases from the problem\n    assert candidate(4, 6) == [3, 4, 1, 2]\n    assert candidate(3, 2) == [3, 2, 1]\n    assert candidate(2, 3) == []\n    \n    # Additional test cases\n    assert candidate(1, 1) == [1]\n    assert candidate(2, 1) == [1, 2]\n    assert candidate(2, 2) == [2, 1]\n    assert candidate(1, 2) == []\n    assert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == [1]\n    \n    # Larger test cases\n    assert candidate(100, 910773911318901) == [...]\n    assert candidate(100, 987952744058429) == [...]\n    assert candidate(100, 60071615558619) == [...]\n    assert candidate(100, 861099165996912) == [...]\n    assert candidate(100, 42