 might want to cap counts at >1e15 to avoid unnecessary large ints for speed. But it's okay.\n\nNow need to test algorithm with the given test cases. Let's simulate some manually.\n\nCase n=4, k=6. All numbers 1..4. Let's generate all alternating permutations:\n\nOdds: {1,3}, evens: {2,4}. Patterns: start odd => odd-even-odd-even: positions: 1 odd,2 even,3 odd,4 even. The odd numbers can be arranged in any order (2! =2) and evens also (2!). So 2*2 =4 permutations for start odd.\n\nList them lexicographically:\nOdd first, choose odd candidate in sorted order: 1 then 3.\n\nCase first=1: remaining odds {3}, evens {2,4}, next parity even. At position 2, we need even candidate. Candidates in sorted order: 2,4. For each, count completions:\n\n- second=2: remaining odds {3}, evens {4}, next parity odd. total=2, odd_rem=1, even_rem=1, next parity odd => odd_positions_needed = ceil(2/2)=1 => odd_rem=1 matches => count = 1! * 1! =1. So this prefix leads to 1 permutation. So the permutation is [1,2,3,4].\n\n- second=4: similarly leads to [1,4,3,2].\n\nThus with first=1 we have permutations: [1,2,3,4] (k=1), [1,4,3,2] (k=2).\n\nNow first=3: remaining odds {1}, evens {2,4}. At position 2 (even), candidates sorted: 2,4.\n\n- second=2 => prefix [3,2], remaining odds {1}, evens {4}, next parity odd => count=1. Permutation: [3,2,1,4].\n\n- second=4 => prefix [3,4], remaining odds {1}, evens {2}, next parity odd => count=1. Permutation: [3,4,1,2].\n\nThus start odd yields permutations (in order):\n1: [1,2,3,4]\n2: [1,4,3,2]\n3: [3,2,1,4]\n4: [3,4,1,2]\n\nNow start even pattern: even-odd-even-odd. Starting with even candidate sorted: 2,4.\n\nCase first=2: remaining odds {1,3}, evens {4}, next parity odd. At position 2 (odd), candidates sorted: 1,3.\n\n- second=1: prefix [2,1], remaining odds {3}, evens {4}, next parity even => count = 1 (odd_rem=1, even_rem=1, next parity even => odd_positions_needed=floor(2/2)=1, odd_rem=1 ok). Permutation: [2,1,4,3]? Wait we need to fill positions: we have used 2 (even), 1 (odd). Remaining numbers are 3 (odd) and 4 (even). Next parity even => position 3 is even: candidate 4. Then position 4 odd => 3. So permutation: [2,1,4,3].\n\n- second=3: prefix [2,3], remaining odds {1}, evens {4}, next parity even => count=1 => permutation: [2,3,4,1]? Wait after prefix [2,3], remaining odds {1}, evens {4}, next parity even => position 3 even: 4, position 4 odd: 1 => [2,3,4,1].\n\nThus start even with first=2 yields permutations:\n5: [2,1,4,3]\n6: [2,3,4,1]\n\nNow first=4: remaining odds {1,3}, evens {2}, next parity odd.\n\n- second=1: prefix [4,1], remaining odds {3}, evens {2}, next parity even => count=1 => permutation: [4,1,2,3]? Wait after prefix [4,1], remaining odds {3}, evens {2}, next parity even => pos3 even: 2, pos4 odd: 3 => [4,1,2,3].\n\n- second=3: prefix [4,3], remaining odds {1}, evens {2}, next parity even => count=1 => permutation: [4,3,2,1].\n\nThus start even yields permutations:\n7: [4,1,2,3]\n8: [4,3,2,1]\n\nThus total 8 permutations. k=6 => [2,3,4,1]? Wait we enumerated: #5 [2,1,4,3], #6 [2,3,4,1] => matches test: kth_alternating_permutation(4,6) returns [3,4,1,2] according to docstring? Actually docstring says >>> kth_alternating_permutation(4, 6) [3,4,1,2]. That's different. Did we enumerate correctly? Let's check test: In check they assert candidate(4,6) == [3,4,1,2]. Wait that's from original problem's examples, but later tests in check they assert candidate(4,6) == [3,4,1,2]? Let's verify. In the provided check function: first three asserts: candidate(4, 6) == [3, 4, 1, 2]. Yes. So our enumeration gave [2,3,4,1] for #6. So something's off. Let's recalc. Did we generate lexicographic order correctly? Let's list all alternating permutations lexicographically (all permutations of {1,2,3,4} that alternate parity). Let's generate them manually:\n\nAll permutations of 1..4, check parity alternation:\n\nPermutation sorted lexicographically:\n\n1. [1,2,3,4] -> 1 odd,2 even (ok),3 odd (ok),4 even (ok). Alternating: yes. That's valid.\n2. [1,2,4,3] -> 1 odd,2 even,4 even (bad, two evens adjacent). invalid.\n3. [1,3,2,4] -> 1 odd,3 odd (bad). invalid.\n4. [1,3,4,2] -> 1 odd,3 odd (bad). invalid.\n5. [1,4,2,3] -> 1 odd,4 even (ok),2 even (bad). invalid.\n6. [1,4,3,2] -> 1 odd,4 even (ok),3 odd (ok),2 even (ok). valid.\n7. [2,1,3,4] -> 2 even,1 odd (ok),3 odd (bad). invalid.\n8. [2,1,4,3] -> 2 even,1 odd (ok),4 even (ok),3 odd (ok). valid.\n9. [2,3,1,4] -> 2 even,3 odd (ok),1 odd (bad). invalid.\n10. [2,3,4,1] -> 2 even,3 odd (ok),4 even (ok),1 odd (ok). valid.\n11. [2,4,1,3] -> 2 even,4 even (bad).\n12. [2,4,3,1] -> 2 even,4 even (bad).\n13. [3,1,2,4] -> 3 odd,1 odd (bad).\n14. [3,1,4,2] -> 3 odd,1 odd (bad).\n15. [3,2,1,4] -> 3 odd,2 even (ok),1 odd (ok),4 even (ok). valid.\n16. [3,2,4,1] -> 3 odd,2 even (ok),4 even (bad).\n17. [3,4,1,2] -> 3 odd,4 even (ok),1 odd (ok),2 even (ok). valid.\n18. [3,4,2,1] -> 3 odd,4 even (ok),2 even (bad).\n19. [4,1,2,3] -> 4 even,1 odd (ok),2 even (ok),3 odd (ok). valid.\n20. [4,1,3,2] -> 4 even,1 odd (ok),3 odd (bad).\n21. [4,2,1,3] -> 4 even,2 even (bad).\n22. [4,2,3,1] -> 4 even,2 even (bad).\n23. [4,3,1,2] -> 4 even,3 odd (ok),1 odd (bad).\n24. [4,3,2,1] -> 4 even,3 odd (ok),2 even (ok),1 odd (ok). valid.\n\nThus valid permutations in lexicographic order: #1 [1,2,3,4]; #2 [1,4,3,2]; #3 [2,1,4,3]; #4 [2,3,4,1]; #5 [3,2,1,4]; #6 [3,4,1,2]; #7 [4,1,2,3]; #8 [4,3,2,1].\n\nThus the 6th is indeed [3,4,1,2]. Good. So our earlier enumeration gave [2,3,4,1] as #6, but we missed some permutations maybe because we considered only alternating parity patterns starting with odd then even, but lexicographic order of all permutations includes those patterns but the order we generated didn't match lexicographic order across the two starting parity groups. Let's see: In lexicographic order, after [1,4,3,2] (which is start odd group), the next valid permutation is [2,1,4,3] (start even). That matches our enumeration: after start odd group (#1-4), we had start even group #5-8. However, we counted start odd group #5 = [2,1,4,3] (which is start even). Actually we enumerated start odd group gave 4 permutations: [1,2,3,4], [1,4,3,2], [3,2,1,4], [3,4,1,2]. That matches #1, #2, #5, #6 in lexicographic order. Then start even group gave 4 permutations: [2,1,4,3], [2,3,4,1], [4,1,2,3], [4,3,2,1] which correspond to #3, #4, #7, #8. So the overall order interleaves start odd and start even groups. The lexicographic ordering doesn't group by starting parity. It's simply sorted by the first element, then second, etc. So our algorithm which picks the smallest candidate at each step should produce the correct lexicographic ordering automatically. Indeed, at first step we consider candidates 1,2,3,4. For each candidate we compute count of completions. The counts for each candidate reflect the number of alternating permutations with that prefix. So the algorithm will correctly skip appropriate number of permutations for each candidate.\n\nLet's test with n=4, k=6 using the algorithm:\n\n- candidates sorted: 1,2,3,4.\n\nCompute counts:\n\nCandidate 1 (odd): after placing 1, odd_rem = 1 (since odds = {1,3}), even_rem = 2 (evens = {2,4}). Next parity = even. Compute count = f(odd_rem=1, even_rem=2, next_parity=even). total =3. Starting parity even => odd_positions_needed = floor(3/2)=1. odd_rem=1 matches. Count = 1! * 2! =2. So there are 2 permutations starting with 1. Since k=6 >2, subtract 2 => k=4, continue.\n\nCandidate 2 (even): after placing 2, odd_rem = 2, even_rem = 1 (evens left {4}). Next parity odd. Compute count = f(odd_rem=2, even_rem=1, next_parity=odd). total=3. Starting parity odd => odd_positions_needed = ceil(3/2)=2. odd_rem=2 matches. Count = 2! * 1! =2. Since k=4 >2, subtract 2 => k=2.\n\nCandidate 3 (odd): after placing 3, odd_rem = 1 (odd left {1}), even_rem = 2 (evens left {2,4}). Next parity even. Compute count = f(odd_rem=1, even_rem=2, next_parity=even) = same as candidate 1 => 2. Since k=2 <=2, choose candidate 3. Append 3. Now k remains 2 (since we didn't subtract). Actually we need to adjust: When we choose candidate, we keep k as is (still 2) because we want the k-th within that prefix. Now next position: next parity = even. Remaining odds: [1], evens: [2,4]. We need to choose even candidate in sorted order: 2,4.\n\n- candidate 2 (even): after placing 2, odd_rem = 1, even_rem = 1 (evens left {4}). Next parity odd. Compute count = f(odd_rem=1, even_rem=1, next_parity=odd). total=2. Starting parity odd => odd_positions_needed=ceil(2/2)=1. odd_rem=1 matches. Count=1! * 1! =1. Since k=2 >1, subtract 1 => k=1, continue.\n\n- candidate 4 (even): after placing 4, odd_rem=1, even_rem=0. Next parity odd. Compute count = f(odd_rem=1, even_rem=0, next_parity=odd). total=1. Starting parity odd => odd_positions_needed = ceil(1/2)=1. odd_rem=1 matches. Count = 1! * 0! =1. Since k=1 <=1, choose 4. Append 4. Now next parity odd. Remaining odds: [1], evens: [].\n\n- position 3: need odd. Only candidate 1. Append 1. Next parity even.\n\n- position 4: need even. Only candidate left is 2. Append 2.\n\nResult: [3,4,1,2] as expected. So algorithm works.\n\nNow test with n=3, k=2. Let's enumerate: odds {1,3}, evens {2}. n=3 odd => odds=2, evens=1 => only pattern odd-even-odd. So permutations are all interleavings with odds at positions 1 and 3, evens at position 2. Number of permutations = 2! * 1! =2. Lexicographically sorted: first element odd sorted: 1 or 3.\n\n- Starting with 1: remaining odds {3}, evens {2}. At position 2 must be even (2). At position 3 odd (3). So permutation [1,2,3].\n\n- Starting with 3: remaining odds {1}, evens {2}. Permutation [3,2,1].\n\nThus k=2 => [3,2,1] matches test.\n\nNow test n=2, k=1: odds {1}, evens {2}. Alternating permutations: start odd => [1,2]; start even => [2,1]. Lexicographically: [1,2] then [2,1]. So k=1 => [1,2] ok. k=2 => [2,1]. k=3 => empty.\n\nNow test n=20, k=26336378880001 expecting [1] according to docstring? Wait test includes: assert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == [1]? In the problem description docstring they wrote: >>> kth_alternating_permutation(20, 26336378880001) [1] but then later they have additional test cases: assert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == [] ??? Let's check. In the problem docstring, they wrote:\n\n>>> kth_alternating_permutation(20, 26336378880001) [1]\n\nBut in the test cases they have:\n\nassert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == [] (two places: earlier after large tests, they have assert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == [] then later they have again assert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == []). Actually let's locate:\n\nThe test cases block:\n\n```\ndef check(candidate):\n    # Test cases from the problem\n    assert candidate(4, 6) == [3, 4, 1, 2]\n    assert candidate(3, 2) == [3, 2, 1]\n    assert candidate(2, 3) == []\n    \n    # Additional test cases\n    assert candidate(1, 1) == [1]\n    assert candidate(2, 1) == [1, 2]\n    assert candidate(2, 2) == [2, 1]\n    assert candidate(1, 2) == []\n    assert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == []\n    \n    # Larger test cases\n    assert candidate(100, 910773911318901) == [...]\n    ...\n    assert candidate(20, 26336378880001) == []\n    assert candidate(100, 116057028082523) == [...]\n    ...\n```\n\nSo the docstring is contradictory with test: docstring says that call returns [1] but test expects [] for same inputs. Actually the docstring's example may be wrong or there is a mistake. In any case, we must satisfy test expectations.\n\nThus we need to compute whether the total number of alternating permutations for n=20 is less than k=26336378880001. Let's compute total count.\n\nFor n=20 (even), m=10. Number of odds = ceil(20/2) = 10, evens=10. Total alternating permutations = 2 * (10!)*(10!). 10! = 3628800. So (10!)*(10!) = (3.6288e6)^2 = 1.317e13. Multiply by 2 => approx 2.634e13. That's 2.634e13, i.e., 263... something? Let's compute exact: 10! = 3628800. Square = 3628800^2 = 13168189440000? Let's calculate precisely: 3628800 * 3628800 = 13,167,688,960,000? Actually let's compute: 3628800 * 3628800:\n\n- 3628800 * 3,628,800 = 3,628,800 * 3,628,800 = (3.6288e6)^2 = approx 1.317e13. Exactly 3,628,800^2 = ?\n\nLet's compute 3,628,800 * 3,628,800:\n\n3,628,800 * 3,628,800 = (3,628,800 * 3,600,000) + (3,628,800 * 28,800). Actually easier: 3,628,800 = 36288 * 100, so square = (36288^2) * 10,000. 36288^2? 36288 * 36288 = ?\n\nCompute 36288 * 36288: 36288*36000 = 1,306,368,000; 36288*288 = 36288*200 + 36288*80 + 36288*8 = 7,257,600 + 2,903,040 + 290,304 = 10,450,944. Sum = 1,306,368,000 + 10,450,944 = 1,316,818,944. So 36288^2 = 1,316,818,944. Multiply by 10,000 => 13,168,189,440,000. That's the square. So (10!)^2 = 13,168,189,440,000.\n\nMultiply by 2 => total = 26,336,378,880,000.\n\nThus total = 26,336,378,880,000.\n\nk = 26336378880001. That is 26,336,378,880,001. That's exactly total + 1. So indeed there are 26,336,378,880,000 permutations, and k is 1 larger, so answer should be empty list. That matches test expecting [].\n\nThus our algorithm must handle large counts and return [] when k > total.\n\nNow we need to generate permutations for n=100 for various k values. Our algorithm must produce correct permutations for those k values.\n\nGiven that total permutations for n=100 is 2 * (50!)*(50!). That is huge > 1e100. So any k up to 1e15 is far less than total. So our algorithm must correctly navigate the large search space.\n\nThe main challenge: The counts we compute for each prefix are factorial products which can be huge (like 50! * 50! ~ 1e130). That's far beyond 1e15. But we only need to compare with k (max 1e15). So we can cap counts at >k to avoid huge numbers.\n\nBut we can also compute factorials exactly and compare using Python big ints; it's fine.\n\nNow we need to ensure algorithm works for n up to 100 with large k efficiently: At each position we may need to loop over candidates from remaining numbers (max 100). At each step we compute count quickly using factorials, O(1). So total time O(n^2) ~ 10k operations, fine.\n\nPotential pitfalls: Since we need to pick candidates sorted ascending, we need to maintain sorted lists of remaining odds and evens (maybe using list and pop index). At each step, we need to iterate over candidates of the required parity (or both if it's the first step). Actually at each step we need to consider all remaining numbers sorted ascending, but we can restrict to those that satisfy the required parity (since if candidate parity does not match required parity, that prefix cannot be extended to a valid alternating permutation). However, for the first step, both parities allowed.\n\nThus we can maintain two sorted lists: odds_rem, evens_rem. At each step, the set of possible candidates is either odds_rem (if next_parity odd) or evens_rem (if next_parity even). For first step, we need to consider both lists, interleaved in sorted order. That can be done by merging the two sorted lists.\n\nSimplify: At each step, we can generate a list of candidate numbers = sorted(odds_rem + evens_rem) if next_parity is None (first step) else just odds_rem or evens_rem accordingly. Since n is up to 100, sorting each time is fine. Or we can maintain a combined sorted list and filter parity.\n\nSimpler: For each step, if next_parity is None, we consider both lists: we can iterate through numbers from 1 to n, checking if they are still unused. But that's O(n^2). Still fine for n=100.\n\nWe'll maintain a boolean array used[1..n] to indicate if number is used. At each step, we iterate i from 1 to n, if not used[i] and (next_parity is None or parity(i) matches), then consider i as candidate.\n\nBut we also need to compute odd_rem and even_rem quickly: we can keep counts of remaining odds and evens (or compute from used). We'll maintain odd_rem and even_rem counts.\n\nSo algorithm steps:\n\n- Precompute factorials up to n (100) as Python ints.\n\n- Determine total number of alternating permutations for given n: if n is even: total = 2 * fact[n//2] * fact[n//2]; else total = fact[(n+1)//2] * fact[n//2].\n\n- If k > total: return [].\n\n- Initialize used = [False] * (n+1) (1-indexed). odd_rem = ceil(n/2); even_rem = floor(n/2). result = [].\n\n- next_parity = None (meaning any parity allowed for first element). Actually we could treat it as None.\n\n- While len(result) < n:\n\n    - For i in range(1, n+1):\n        - if used[i]: continue.\n        - if next_parity is not None and (i % 2) != next_parity: continue. (Define parity: odd=1, even=0 maybe.)\n        - Determine new_odd_rem = odd_rem - (i%2==1)\n        - new_even_rem = even_rem - (i%2==0)\n        - Determine new_next_parity = 1 if i%2==0 else 0? Actually after placing i, the next required parity is opposite: next_parity = 1 - (i%2) (if using 1 for odd, 0 for even). But we can compute new_next_parity = 1 - (i%2). For first step, next parity becomes opposite of i's parity.\n\n        - Compute count = f(new_odd_rem, new_even_rem, new_next_parity). If count >= k, then choose i: result.append(i), used[i]=True, odd_rem = new_odd_rem, even_rem = new_even_rem, next_parity = new_next_parity, break out of loop to next position.\n        - else: k -= count, continue to next candidate.\n\n    - If loop ends without picking (should not happen if k <= total). But maybe due to bug.\n\n- Return result.\n\nNow implement f:\n\n```\ndef count_permutations(odd_rem, even_rem, next_parity):\n    total = odd_rem + even_rem\n    if total == 0:\n        return 1\n    if next_parity == 1: # odd\n        odd_needed = (total + 1) // 2  # ceil\n    else:\n        odd_needed = total // 2  # floor\n    if odd_rem != odd_needed:\n        return 0\n    # else count = odd_rem! * even_rem!\n    return factorial[odd_rem] * factorial[even_rem]\n```\n\nNote: next_parity can be 0 for even, 1 for odd. For first step we call with next_parity = None, but we only compute count after picking first element.\n\nEdge Cases: For n odd, only one parity pattern is possible: start with odd (since odds > evens). Let's verify: n odd => odds = evens+1. So only starting parity odd yields odd_positions_needed = ceil(total/2) = ceil((odd_rem+even_rem)/2) = (odd_rem+even_rem+1)//2. Starting with odd, odd_needed = ceil(total/2) = (total+1)//2. Since odd_rem = evens+1 = (total+1)//2? Actually total = odd_rem + even_rem = (even+1)+even = 2*even+1 = odd number. Then (total+1)//2 = (2*even+2)//2 = even+1 = odd_rem. So matches. Starting with even would require odd_needed = floor(total/2) = even. But odd_rem = even+1 > even, so count = 0. So our function will correctly return 0 for start even.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nNow we must ensure we compute total permutations correctly for early exit condition. Compute total = count_permutations(odd_count, even_count, None?). Actually we can compute total as:\n\nIf n is even: total = 2 * fact[n//2] * fact[n//2]\nIf n is odd: total = fact[(n+1)//2] * fact[n//2] (only start odd). So we can compute total accordingly.\n\nNow test with n=20, k=26336378880001 (total = 2*10!*10! = 26336378880000). Since k > total, return [].\n\nNow test with n=100 large ks. We need to confirm algorithm returns correct outputs for the test cases.\n\nWe need to verify that for n=100, the algorithm will generate the exact permutations shown in tests for given k values. The tests include many permutations; we need to ensure our algorithm matches.\n\nPotential issues: Factorials for 50! are huge (approx 3e64). Multiplying them yields ~1e129. Python big ints can handle that. However, we need to compute factorials up to 100. 100! is approx 9.33e157, still fine.\n\nNow we need to consider that computing count = factorial[odd_rem] * factorial[even_rem] for each candidate may be large and cause big integer multiplication many times (O(n^2) times). That's okay for n=100 (max 100*100 ~ 10k multiplications). Might be okay.\n\nNow we need to ensure we correctly handle parity mapping: i % 2 == 1 for odd; i % 2 == 0 for even.\n\nNow we need to test with sample test cases manually using code (simulate mentally or write small test). But we can't run code; we need to reason.\n\nLet's test algorithm on some of the given large test cases to ensure they match.\n\nWe need to verify that the algorithm produces exactly the given permutations for n=100 and various ks. Let's manually test a couple of them.\n\nGiven n=100, total permutations huge. The test expects certain permutations that look like initial segment is sorted 1..78, then some pattern. For k = 910773911318901, the expected permutation starts with [1,2,...,78,91,86,...]. So first 78 elements are 1..78 in order. So the algorithm must skip many permutations before arriving at that prefix. That seems plausible.\n\nThus we need to be confident our algorithm yields same lexicographic ordering as defined. We should verify the logic of counting completions: count = odd_rem! * even_rem! if parity pattern matches; else 0. This is correct because for a given parity pattern, we can assign any order of odds to odd positions and evens to even positions. Indeed, the number of completions after a given prefix is independent of which specific odds/even numbers remain, only their counts. Because the set of remaining odds can be arranged arbitrarily into the odd positions, and the set of remaining evens into even positions. There is a one-to-one mapping between permutations of odd numbers and assignments to odd positions. So count = odd_rem! * even_rem! is correct.\n\nThus algorithm is correct.\n\nNow we need to ensure that when next_parity is None (first position), we consider both parity patterns appropriately when counting completions. Our algorithm enumerates candidate i in sorted order; for each i we compute new_odd_rem, new_even_rem, new_next_parity, and then compute count for the remaining suffix using f(new_odd_rem, new_even_rem, new_next_parity). This count will be non-zero only if the parity pattern can be completed given the new parity requirement. That correctly handles both patterns: For even n, both parity start possibilities are allowed (since after picking i, the suffix must be possible). For odd n, only one start parity leads to non-zero count.\n\nThus algorithm will correctly count permutations with each prefix.\n\nPotential subtlety: The total number of permutations with prefix i may be > k but we need to compare k with count. Since k up to 1e15, we may have count huge (like >1e15). In that case we choose i. Good.\n\nNow we must handle the case where k is exactly total (or within). The algorithm will correctly select the last permutation.\n\nNow let's think about potential pitfalls: The ordering of candidates must be based on numeric order, not on parity. Since we loop i from 1 to n, it's numeric order. Good.\n\nNow we need to be careful about the next_parity variable: after placing i, the required parity for next element is opposite of i's parity. So new_next_parity = 1 if i%2==0 else 0? Let's define parity numbers: odd -> 1, even -> 0. So i%2 yields 1 for odd, 0 for even. So opposite parity = 1 - (i%2). So new_next_parity = 1 - (i%2). So if i is odd (i%2=1), new_next_parity = 0 (even). Good.\n\nNow in f we need to interpret next_parity as 0 for even, 1 for odd.\n\nNow implement factorial list: factorial[0] = 1, for i in 1..n: factorial[i] = factorial[i-1] * i.\n\nNow implement total count early check: total = factorial[odd_count] * factorial[even_count] * (2 if odd_count == even_count else 1). Since if odd_count == even_count (n even), there are two possible starting parity patterns. If odd_count != even_count (n odd), only one pattern possible. That matches earlier formula.\n\nNow early exit: if k > total: return [].\n\nNow main loop.\n\nNow we need to ensure we treat k as 1-indexed. Our algorithm uses k as 1-indexed: we compare k > count then subtract; if k <= count we choose. That's correct.\n\nNow after finishing the loop, we have result list length n.\n\nNow we need to return result.\n\nNow test for n=1, k=1: odds=1, evens=0. total = factorial[1]*factorial[0] * 1 = 1. k <= total => proceed. First loop: i=1 candidate odd, odd_rem after removal = 0, even_rem = 0, new_next_parity = even (0). count = f(0,0,0) => total=0 => return 1 (maybe we should treat total==0 as 1). So count = 1. Since k <= count (1 <= 1) => choose i=1. Append. Then loop ends (len result = 1). Return [1]. Good.\n\nn=1, k=2: total=1, k>total => return [].\n\nNow n=2, odds=1, evens=1. total = 2 * 1! * 1! = 2. k=1: algorithm:\n\n- i=1 (odd): new_odd_rem=0, new_even_rem=1, new_next_parity=even (0). count = f(0,1,0). total = 1. Starting parity even => odd_needed = floor(1/2)=0. odd_rem=0 matches, count=0! * 1! = 1. So count=1. Since k=1 <= count, choose i=1. Append 1. Next parity even. Remaining numbers: i=2 (even). Check count for i=2: new_odd_rem=0, new_even_rem=0, new_next_parity=odd (1). count = f(0,0,1) => 1. k still 1, choose i=2. Return [1,2]. Good.\n\nk=2: start loop:\n\n- i=1: count=1 (as above). Since k=2 > count, subtract count => k=1. Continue.\n\n- i=2: candidate even. new_odd_rem=1, new_even_rem=0, new_next_parity=odd (1). count = f(1,0,1). total=1. Starting parity odd => odd_needed = ceil(1/2)=1. odd_rem=1 matches, count=1! * 0! = 1. Since k=1 <= count, choose i=2. Append 2. Next parity odd. Remaining numbers: i=1 (odd). Choose 1. Return [2,1].\n\nk=3 > total=2 => early exit returns [].\n\nThus works.\n\nNow we need to test for n=20, k=26336378880001 => total=26336378880000 => early exit returns [].\n\nNow test for n=20, k=26336378880000 (not in tests) => algorithm should return the last permutation. Let's see if our algorithm would produce correct last permutation. Not needed for test.\n\nNow test for n=100, large ks.\n\nWe need to verify that the algorithm yields the same permutations as expected. Since the test includes many specific permutations, we need to be confident.\n\nPotential pitfalls:\n\n- The factorial numbers may be huge; computing factorial[odd_rem] * factorial[even_rem] may cause huge integers causing performance issues, but Python can handle multiplication of large ints quickly for moderate sizes.\n\n- However, we may need to cap counts to avoid unnecessary large ints. But it's fine.\n\n- We must ensure that we handle the case where total permutations exceed Python's memory? Not likely.\n\nNow we need to double-check the logic for f (count_permutations). It must compute the number of completions given the parity of the next element. Let's test f for some small values.\n\nCase odd_rem=1, even_rem=2, next_parity=even (0). total=3. odd_needed = floor(3/2)=1. odd_rem=1 matches. So count = 1! * 2! = 2. That matches earlier count for prefix after [1] in n=4 case.\n\nCase odd_rem=2, even_rem=1, next_parity=odd (1). total=3. odd_needed = ceil(3/2)=2. odd_rem=2 matches. count = 2! * 1! = 2. That's correct for prefix after [2] in n=4 case.\n\nCase odd_rem=2, even_rem=2, next_parity=odd (1). total=4. odd_needed = ceil(4/2)=2. odd_rem=2 matches. count = 2! * 2! = 4. That's number of completions for prefix [1] when n=4? Actually after picking first odd 1, odd_rem=1, even_rem=2 => count=2. That's fine.\n\nNow for n=4, after picking 1, count for completions is 2 (as earlier). That matches.\n\nNow check for n=3, after picking 1 (odd). odd_rem=1 (since odds initially 2, remove 1 => left {3}), even_rem=1 (evens {2}). next_parity even (0). total=2. odd_needed = floor(2/2)=1. odd_rem=1 matches. count = 1! * 1! = 1. That matches: only one completion after prefix [1] (must be [1,2,3]).\n\nNow after picking 3 (odd) as first element, odd_rem=1 (remaining odds {1}), even_rem=1, next_parity even. Count = 1 as well. So prefix [3] also has 1.\n\nThus algorithm yields correct counts.\n\nNow test for n=5 (odd) to see if algorithm works for odd n.\n\nn=5: odds = {1,3,5}, evens={2,4}. total permutations = odd! * even! = 3! * 2! = 6*2=12. Starting parity must be odd.\n\nLet's list lexicographically:\n\nAll permutations of 1..5 that alternate parity (odd-even-odd-even-odd). So odds at positions 1,3,5; evens at 2,4. So basically we need to assign odds to positions 1,3,5 in any order (3! ways) and evens to positions 2,4 in any order (2! ways) => 12 permutations.\n\nSorted lexicographically: we need to generate them.\n\nOur algorithm will consider first element i from 1..5, but only odd candidates allowed (since next_parity None but after picking i we need to be able to complete). Actually at first step we consider both odd and even candidates, but count for even start will be 0, because after picking even as first element, the parity pattern would be even-odd-even-odd-even, which requires more evens than odds (evens needed = ceil(5/2)=3 but we have only 2 evens) => count=0. So algorithm will skip even candidates because count=0 (k>0?). Actually when count=0, we treat it as no permutations start with that prefix, so we subtract nothing and continue to next candidate. So we effectively consider only odd candidates.\n\nNow for i=1: new odd_rem=2, even_rem=2, new_next_parity even. total=4, odd_needed = floor(4/2)=2, odd_rem=2 => count = 2! * 2! = 4. So 4 permutations start with 1.\n\ni=2: even start count = f(new odd_rem=3, new_even_rem=1, next_parity odd). total=4. odd_needed = ceil(4/2)=2, but odd_rem=3 => mismatch => count=0. So skip.\n\ni=3: odd start, new odd_rem=2, even_rem=2, count=4.\n\ni=4: count=0.\n\ni=5: odd start, count=4.\n\nThus total = 12.\n\nNow k=some value will be chosen accordingly.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nNow for n=100, odd_rem and even_rem will be 50 each initially.\n\nNow we need to consider the case where k may be > total. Already early exit.\n\nNow we must implement the function in Python with type hints and docstring as given.\n\nNow we must ensure code passes the tests exactly.\n\nPotential hidden tests may include n up to 100, k up to 10^15, maybe also k=0 (though spec says k>=1). Not needed.\n\nPotential hidden tests may test performance or edge cases like n=0 (but spec says n >= 1). So fine.\n\nNow we need to write code.\n\nOne nuance: The total count for n=100 is huge, but we only need to store factorials up to 50 maybe. However we need factorial up to 100 for odd_rem and even_rem values maybe up to 50 each (since max odd_rem or even_rem = 50). But we may also need factorial for odd_rem up to 51 maybe if n odd. So we can compute factorial up to n.\n\nNow we need to compute factorial[0..n] using Python's int.\n\nNow we also need to handle big k values; we will treat k as Python int.\n\nNow algorithm details:\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n```\ndef kth_alternating_permutation(n: int, k: int) -> List[int]:\n    # Precompute factorials up to n\n    factorial = [1] * (n+1)\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        factorial[i] = factorial[i-1] * i\n\n    # Count of odds and evens\n    odd_total = (n + 1) // 2\n    even_total = n // 2\n\n    # total number of alternating permutations\n    total = factorial[odd_total] * factorial[even_total]\n    if odd_total == even_total:\n        total *= 2\n\n    if k > total:\n        return []\n\n    used = [False] * (n + 1)  # 1-indexed\n    odd_rem = odd_total\n    even_rem = even_total\n    result = []\n    next_parity = None  # None means any parity allowed for first element\n\n    # Helper function to count completions\n    def count_completions(o_rem, e_rem, next_par):\n        if o_rem == 0 and e_rem == 0:\n            return 1\n        total_rem = o_rem + e_rem\n        if next_par == 1:  # odd required next\n            odd_needed = (total_rem + 1) // 2\n        else:  # even required next (next_par == 0)\n            odd_needed = total_rem // 2\n        if o_rem != odd_needed:\n            return 0\n        # else count = o_rem! * e_rem!\n        return factorial[o_rem] * factorial[e_rem]\n\n    # Main loop\n    while len(result) < n:\n        # iterate candidates in numeric order\n        for i in range(1, n + 1):\n            if used[i]:\n                continue\n            # parity requirement\n            if next_parity is not None:\n                if (i % 2) != next_parity:\n                    continue\n            # compute new remaining counts after picking i\n            o_rem = odd_rem - (1 if i % 2 == 1 else 0)\n            e_rem = even_rem - (1 if i % 2 == 0 else 0)\n            # determine parity required for next position\n            new_next = 1 - (i % 2)  # 1 for odd, 0 for even\n            # count completions after choosing i\n            cnt = count_completions(o_rem, e_rem, new_next) if (len(result) + 1) < n else 1  # if this is the last element, count is 1\n            # Actually count_completions works for zero remaining elements, returning 1.\n            # So we can always call it.\n            cnt = count_completions(o_rem, e_rem, new_next)\n            if k > cnt:\n                k -= cnt\n                continue\n            # choose i\n            result.append(i)\n            used[i] = True\n            odd_rem = o_rem\n            even_rem = e_rem\n            next_parity = new_next\n            break\n        else:\n            # No candidate found (should not happen)\n            break\n\n    return result\n```\n\nBut we need to be careful about the base case: After picking the last element (when len(result) == n-1 before picking), after picking i, o_rem + e_rem = 0, count_completions(0,0,new_next) returns 1 (as per function). So we can just call count_completions; it will return 1.\n\nNow note: The count_completions function expects next_par to be either 0 or 1. For the case where total_rem = 0, we can just return 1 directly. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to think about parity mapping: i%2 yields 0 for even, 1 for odd. So new_next = 1 - (i%2). This yields 1 for even i? Wait: i%2 = 0 for even, so new_next = 1 - 0 = 1 (odd). That matches: after even, next must be odd. After odd, i%2=1, new_next = 0 (even). Good.\n\nNow count_completions: for next_par = 1 (odd), odd_needed = ceil(total_rem/2). For next_par = 0 (even), odd_needed = floor(total_rem/2). Implementation: if next_par == 1: odd_needed = (total_rem + 1) // 2 else: odd_needed = total_rem // 2.\n\nNow if odd_rem != odd_needed: return 0 else return factorial[odd_rem] * factorial[even_rem].\n\nNow for total_rem = 0, we need to handle separately: odd_needed = 0 either way, odd_rem=0, even_rem=0, count = factorial[0]*factorial[0] = 1, works.\n\nThus we can just compute with formula without special case.\n\nNow we must ensure that when total_rem > 0 but odd_rem != odd_needed, we return 0.\n\nNow we also need to handle the scenario where next_par is None (first step). But we are not calling count_completions with next_par=None; we compute for each candidate i directly, where new_next is known. So it's fine.\n\nNow we need to handle k as int potentially large, but Python ints fine.\n\nNow we need to ensure that we compute total correctly: total = factorial[odd_total] * factorial[even_total] * (2 if odd_total == even_total else 1). For n even, odd_total = even_total => multiply by 2. For n odd, odd_total > even_total => no multiply.\n\nNow we need to ensure we use Python's integer multiplication for factorial values; that yields huge ints.\n\nNow we need to test with the given test cases mentally for some large ones.\n\nBut we need to ensure the algorithm's count_completions matches the actual number of completions for given prefix when next_par parity is given.\n\nWe need to double-check that after picking first element i, the parity constraint for the next position is opposite of i's parity. However, what about the parity constraint for the remaining positions beyond that? The pattern is determined by the next parity and the counts. The count_completions function only uses the next parity and counts, not the actual pattern of parity positions beyond the next. But given that we require alternating parity, the pattern is fully determined by the parity of the next element: it will be alternating after that. So the number of completions for suffix given next parity and counts is indeed factorials if the counts match the required pattern.\n\nThus count_completions is correct.\n\nNow we need to ensure that after picking i at position pos, we compute new_next = 1 - (i%2) correctly for the next position. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to check that we update next_parity to new_next for the next iteration.\n\nNow we need to verify that we compute count for each candidate correctly. For each candidate i, we compute o_rem, e_rem after removal. Then we compute count_completions(o_rem, e_rem, new_next). That's the number of completions for the suffix. If k > count, we skip this candidate and subtract count; else we select it.\n\nThus algorithm yields k-th lexicographic alternating permutation.\n\nNow we need to test with some of the given large test cases to ensure we produce expected output.\n\nGiven the complexity, we can attempt to simulate some steps for n=100, k=910773911318901 to see if output matches expected prefix [1..78,91,...]. But that's too many steps to simulate manually. Instead we can reason that algorithm is correct, and thus the outputs will match.\n\nPotential issues: The lexicographic order of permutations for n=100 may not be exactly as the test expects if we misinterpret parity requirement. Let's double-check the definition: \"Alternating permutation is a permutation where no two adjacent elements are both odd or both even.\" That's exactly parity alternates, but not necessarily strict alternation pattern? Wait, \"no two adjacent elements are both odd or both even\" means that any two adjacent numbers must have opposite parity. That is exactly alternating parity. So we have the parity alternating condition. So our algorithm matches definition.\n\nThus for n=100, the permutations must have alternating parity, but there might be two possible patterns: odd-even-odd-even... or even-odd-even-odd... as we considered.\n\nNow lexicographic order: All permutations sorted by numeric order, not by parity pattern.\n\nThus our algorithm enumerates them correctly.\n\nNow we need to confirm that we treat parity as only odd/even. That's fine.\n\nNow the only potential subtlety: For n even, the number of odds = number of evens. So both patterns are possible. Our count_completions function uses next_par (the parity required for next element) to compute odd_needed = ceil(total/2) if next_par odd else floor(total/2). This effectively ensures that the pattern of parity alternates. For start parity odd, odd_needed = ceil(total/2). For start parity even, odd_needed = floor(total/2). So count_completions returns factorials when pattern matches counts. That is correct.\n\nNow test for n=4 after picking first element 2 (even). new_next = odd (1). odd_rem=2 (odds left {1,3}), even_rem=1 (evens left {4}). total=3. odd_needed for next_par odd = ceil(3/2)=2. odd_rem=2 matches, count = 2! * 1! = 2. That matches earlier.\n\nNow test after picking prefix [2,1] (even then odd). After second step, odd_rem=1 (remaining odd {3}), even_rem=1 (remaining even {4}), new_next = even (0). total=2. odd_needed for next_par even = floor(2/2)=1. odd_rem=1 matches, count=1! * 1! = 1. So only one completion: [2,1,4,3]. Good.\n\nNow test after picking prefix [2,3] (even, odd). odd_rem=1 (remaining odd {1}), even_rem=1 (remaining even {4}), new_next = even. same count=1. Completion: [2,3,4,1]. Good.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nNow we need to consider performance for n=100 and many ks. Each iteration loops over up to n candidates (100). So total operations approx n^2 = 10k. For each candidate we compute count_completions: compute total_rem, odd_needed, compare odd_rem, compute factorial multiplication (maybe large). That's fine.\n\nNow check for n=100 and k close to 1e15. Count_completions may produce huge counts (like 50! * 50! ~ 1e129). That's far bigger than k, so we will select the smallest candidate at each step until we find a prefix where count < k (skip). Actually the algorithm will often find that the first candidate has count >> k, so it will select the first candidate. This means for many ks the result may start with 1. Indeed the test expected first 78 numbers are 1..78 for many ks. This is plausible: Since the number of completions after prefix [1,2,3,...] is huge, the k-th permutation may be early lexicographically.\n\nThus algorithm will produce prefix that matches.\n\nNow we need to ensure we handle k as 1-indexed: Starting with k=1 selects first permutation. Our algorithm subtracts count only if k > count; if k <= count, we select candidate. That's correct.\n\nNow we need to test with large ks like 910773911318901. Let's see if the algorithm will produce the expected prefix. Hard to simulate manually, but likely correct.\n\nPotential bug: The count_completions function may return huge ints that cause performance slowdown due to repeated multiplication. To reduce overhead, we could cache factorial values and maybe use multiplication but it's okay.\n\nPotential bug: The algorithm may overflow recursion? No recursion.\n\nPotential bug: The algorithm uses used list to check if number used, which is O(1). The loops for each position iterate from 1..n (100) each time, which is fine.\n\nPotential bug: When n is large, the total number of permutations is huge, but we may need to compute total at start for early exit. For n=100, total = factorial[50]*factorial[50] * 2. That multiplication yields huge number, but Python can handle it. However we might want to avoid computing it if we only need to compare with k. But it's okay.\n\nNow edge case: If k is exactly total, algorithm should return last permutation. Let's test with n=4, total=8, k=8. According to our enumeration, last permutation is [4,3,2,1]. Let's simulate algorithm:\n\nk=8.\n\nFirst step: i=1 count=2, k>2 => k=6.\n\ni=2 count=2, k>2 => k=4.\n\ni=3 count=2, k>2 => k=2.\n\ni=4 count=2, k <=2 => select i=4. Now result=[4], next parity odd, odd_rem=2, even_rem=1, k=2.\n\nSecond step: candidates: i=1 (odd) count? after picking 