tarting odd). So prefix [1..78] corresponds to start parity odd (since 1 is odd). After prefix 1..78, we have used odds up to 77 (odd numbers up to 77) and evens up to 78 (evens up to 78). So odds remaining: odd numbers from 79 to 99 (since odds up to 100 are 1..99 odd numbers). Let's list odd numbers: 1,3,...,99 => there are 50 odds. Up to 77 used, that's 39 odds (since odd numbers: 1,3,...,77: count = (77+1)//2 = 39). So remaining odds = 50-39 = 11 odds: 79,81,83,85,87,89,91,93,95,97,99.\n\nSimilarly evens used up to 78: evens 2,4,...,78 => count = 78/2 = 39 evens used. Total evens = 50 (2..100). So remaining evens = 11 evens: 80,82,84,86,88,90,92,94,96,98,100.\n\nNow after prefix 1..78, we have 22 positions left (positions 79..100). The required parity for position 79: Since start parity odd, positions alternate. Position 79 is odd (since start odd, odd positions odd). So required parity at position 79 is odd. So the remaining suffix must be a permutation of the remaining odds and evens alternating, starting with odd at pos79. So we need to generate the k' -th permutation of the remaining numbers with this pattern.\n\nThus the lexicographic ordering of suffix permutations will be based on the remaining numbers sorted.\n\nNow the expected output suffix for the given k is [91,86,81,94,89,84,79,98,83,82,85,90,87,80,99,92,97,100,93,88,95,96]. Let's check if this matches lexicographic ordering for some rank.\n\nNow compute number of permutations for suffix: O_rem=11, E_rem=11 => total suffix permutations = 11! * 11! = huge (approx (39916800)^2 ~ 1.6e15). So there are many suffix permutations.\n\nThus k' = k - offset where offset is number of permutations with prefix less than [1..78].\n\nOur algorithm will compute idx for each position accordingly.\n\nThus likely matches.\n\nThus algorithm should produce correct outputs.\n\nNow we need to test that for n=100 and some k values, the algorithm yields correct permutation as per test.\n\nGiven the complexity, it's plausibl