1). e2=2 gets B => newB|=4 => newB=8+4=12 (bits2,3). New state: maskB=12 (bits2,3), maskW=3 (bits0,1). That's 'WWBB..' again.\n\ni=5: out of range (i up to total_len-2 =4). So only those two moves.\n\nThus from state2 we can get new states: (34,5) and (12,3). Already visited? (12,3) is 'WWBB..' visited earlier.\n\nState3: 'WW..BB' (maskB=48, maskW=3). occupancy bits: maskB bits4,5; maskW bits0,1. empties at 2,3. e1=2, e2=3.\n\nPossible i:\n\ni=0: bits0 (W),1 (W) occupied => both W. Move to empties 2,3: clear bits0,1: newB=48 unchanged; newW=3 & ~ (1|2) = 0. Then place at e1=2 (W) => newW|=1<<2=4; e2=3 (W) => newW|=1<<3=8. So new state: maskB=48, maskW=12 (bits2,3). That's '...??' positions: 0,1 empty; 2,3 W; 4,5 B => '..WWBB'.\n\ni=1: bits1 (W) and 2 (empty) => no.\n\ni=2: bits2 empty => no.\n\ni=3: bits3 empty => no.\n\ni=4: bits4 (B),5 (B) both occupied => move to empties 2,3: clear bits4,5: newB=48 & ~ (16|32) = 0; newW=3 unchanged. Place B at e1=2 => newB|=4; place B at e2=3 => newB|=8 => newB=12. New state: maskB=12 (bits2,3), maskW=3 (bits0,1) => 'WWBB..' again.\n\nThus new states: '..WWBB' (maskB=48,maskW=12) and 'WWBB..' again.\n\nNow BFS continues.\n\nGoal state is maskB=3,maskW=12. We haven't reached it yet.\n\nLet's continue BFS manually to see if distance 3.\n\nFrom start (distance0) we generated three states: A: '..BBWW' (dist1), B: 'W..BWB' (dist1), C: 'WW..BB' (dist1). None are target.\n\nFrom A (dist1) we generated:\n- D: 'BB..WW' (maskB=3,maskW=48) (dist2)\n- E: ... (some other)\n- F: 'WWBB..' (maskB=12,maskW=3) (dist2)\n\nFrom B (dist1) we generated:\n- G: maskB=34,maskW=5 (dist2)\n- H: 'WWBB..' (maskB=12,maskW=3) (dist2) (already visited)\nFrom C (dist1) we generated:\n- I: '..WWBB' (maskB=48,maskW=12) (dist2)\n- J: 'WWBB..' again.\n\nNow from D (dist2): 'BB..WW' (maskB=3,maskW=48). occupancy bits: bits0,1 B; bits4,5 W. empties at 2,3. e1=2,e2=3.\n\nPossible i:\ni=0: bits0,1 both B => move to empties 2,3: clear bits0,1 => newB=0; newW stays 48. Place B at2,3 => newB= (1<<2)+(1<<3)=4+8=12. New state: maskB=12, maskW=48 => '..BBWW' again (state A). So D->A.\n\ni=1: bits1 (B) and 2 empty => no.\ni=2: bits2 empty => no.\ni=3: bits3 empty => no.\ni=4: bits4,5 both W => move to empties 2,3: clear bits4,5 => newW=0; newB=3 unchanged. Place W at2,3 => newW=4+8=12. New state: maskB=3, maskW=12 => target! Indeed moving the pair of W's from positions 4,5 to empties 2,3 yields target B at 0,1; W at 2,3; empties at 4,5. That's one more move: from D (dist2) to target (dist3). So total distance = 3.\n\nThus BFS finds target at distance 3. Good.\n\nThus BFS works.\n\nNow we need to implement BFS with bit masks, which is efficient.\n\nNow consider potential pitfalls: BFS may generate a lot of states; we need to ensure visited dict does not blow memory across multiple test cases. But each call is separate; visited is local.\n\nNow test more cases.\n\nTest case: n=6, s='BWBWBW', t='WWWBBB', answer 4.\n\nWe'll test manually but trust BFS.\n\nNow test case: n=6, s='BBBBBB', t='WWWWWW', answer -1.\n\nCounts mismatch (6 B vs 0 B) => immediate -1. So BFS not needed.\n\nTest case: n=14, s='BBBWBWWWBBWWBW', t='WBWWBBWWWBWBBB', answer 7.\n\nCounts? Let's compute B count in s: s = B B B W B W W W B B W W B W. Count B: positions 0,1,2,4,8,9,12 => that's 7? Actually let's count: 0 B,1 B,2 B,3 W,4 B,5 W,6 W,7 W,8 B,9 B,10 W,11 W,12 B,13 W => B's at 0,1,2,4,8,9,12 = 7 B's. So 7 B's, 7 W's. t: 'W B W W B B W W W B W B B B' => B's count? Let's count: t = W B W W B B W W W B W B B B. B's at positions 1,4,5,9,11,12,13 => 7 B's. So counts match.\n\nThus BFS will process.\n\nNow test other test cases with mismatched counts: e.g., 'BW' -> 'WB' counts match (1 B,1W). So BFS needed.\n\nNow implement BFS as described.\n\nNow verify test 'assert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 7' appears twice in test suite. Actually they have duplicate for both 7 and 3? Let's examine test suite:\n\n```\nassert candidate(4, 'WWBB', 'BBWW') == 3\nassert candidate(4, '