1). e2=2 gets B => newB|=4 => newB=8+4=12 (bits2,3). New state: maskB=12 (bits2,3), maskW=3 (bits0,1). That's 'WWBB..' again.\n\ni=5: out of range (i up to total_len-2 =4). So only those two moves.\n\nThus from state2 we can get new states: (34,5) and (12,3). Already visited? (12,3) is 'WWBB..' visited earlier.\n\nState3: 'WW..BB' (maskB=48, maskW=3). occupancy bits: maskB bits4,5; maskW bits0,1. empties at 2,3. e1=2, e2=3.\n\nPossible i:\n\ni=0: bits0 (W),1 (W) occupied => both W. Move to empties 2,3: clear bits0,1: newB=48 unchanged; newW=3 & ~ (1|2) = 0. Then place at e1=2 (W) => newW|=1<<2=4; e2=3 (W) => newW|=1<<3=8. So new state: maskB=48, maskW=12 (bits2,3). That's '...??' positions: 0,1 empty; 2,3 W; 4,5 B => '..WWBB'.\n\ni=1: bits1 (W) and 2 (empty) => no.\n\ni=2: bits2 empty => no.\n\ni=3: bits3 empty => no.\n\ni=4: bits4 (B),5 (B) both occupied => move to empties 2,3: clear bits4,5: newB=48 & ~ (16|32) = 0; newW=3 unchanged. Place B at e1=2 => newB|=4; place B at e2=3 => newB|=8 => newB=12. New state: maskB=12 (bits2,3), maskW=3 (bits0,1) => 'WWBB..' again.\n\nThus new states: '..WWBB' (maskB=48,maskW=12) and 'WWBB..' again.\n\nNow BFS continues.\n\nGoal state is maskB=3,maskW=12. We haven't reached it yet.\n\nLet's continue BFS manually to see if distance 3.\n\nFrom start (distance0) we generated three states: A: '..BBWW' (dist1), B: 'W..BWB' (dist1), C: 'WW..BB' (dist1). None are target.\n\nFrom A (dist1) we generated:\n- D: 'BB..WW' (maskB=3,maskW=48) (dist2)\n- E: ... (some other)\n- F: 'WWBB..' (maskB=12,maskW=3) (dist2)\n\nFrom B (dist1) we generated:\n- G: maskB=34,maskW=5 (dist2)\n- H: 'WWBB..' (maskB=12,maskW=3) (dist2) (already visited)\nFrom C (dist1) we generated:\n- I: '..WWBB' (maskB=48,maskW=12) (dist2)\n- J: 'WWBB..' again.\n\nNow from D (dist2): 'BB..WW' (maskB=3,maskW=48). occupancy bits: bits0,1 B; bits4,5 W. empties at 2,3. e1=2,e2=3.\n\nPossible i:\ni=0: bits0,1 both B => move to empties 2,3: clear bits0,1 => newB=0; newW stays 48. Place B at2,3 => newB= (1<<2)+(1<<3)=4+8=12. New state: maskB=12, maskW=48 => '..BBWW' again (state A). So D->A.\n\ni=1: bits1 (B) and 2 empty => no.\ni=2: bits2 empty => no.\ni=3: bits3 empty => no.\ni=4: bits4,5 both W => move to empties 2,3: clear bits4,5 => newW=0; newB=3 unchanged. Place W at2,3 => newW=4+8=12. New state: maskB=3, maskW=12 => target! Indeed moving the pair of W's from positions 4,5 to empties 2,3 yields target B at 0,1; W at 2,3; empties at 4,5. That's one more move: from D (dist2) to target (dist3). So total distance = 3.\n\nThus BFS finds target at distance 3. Good.\n\nThus BFS works.\n\nNow we need to implement BFS with bit masks, which is efficient.\n\nNow consider potential pitfalls: BFS may generate a lot of states; we need to ensure visited dict does not blow memory across multiple test cases. But each call is separate; visited is local.\n\nNow test more cases.\n\nTest case: n=6, s='BWBWBW', t='WWWBBB', answer 4.\n\nWe'll test manually but trust BFS.\n\nNow test case: n=6, s='BBBBBB', t='WWWWWW', answer -1.\n\nCounts mismatch (6 B vs 0 B) => immediate -1. So BFS not needed.\n\nTest case: n=14, s='BBBWBWWWBBWWBW', t='WBWWBBWWWBWBBB', answer 7.\n\nCounts? Let's compute B count in s: s = B B B W B W W W B B W W B W. Count B: positions 0,1,2,4,8,9,12 => that's 7? Actually let's count: 0 B,1 B,2 B,3 W,4 B,5 W,6 W,7 W,8 B,9 B,10 W,11 W,12 B,13 W => B's at 0,1,2,4,8,9,12 = 7 B's. So 7 B's, 7 W's. t: 'W B W W B B W W W B W B B B' => B's count? Let's count: t = W B W W B B W W W B W B B B. B's at positions 1,4,5,9,11,12,13 => 7 B's. So counts match.\n\nThus BFS will process.\n\nNow test other test cases with mismatched counts: e.g., 'BW' -> 'WB' counts match (1 B,1W). So BFS needed.\n\nNow implement BFS as described.\n\nNow verify test 'assert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 7' appears twice in test suite. Actually they have duplicate for both 7 and 3? Let's examine test suite:\n\n```\nassert candidate(4, 'WWBB', 'BBWW') == 3\nassert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 7\n...\nassert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 3\n```\n\nWait there is a duplicate:\n\nFirst they have:\n\n```\nassert candidate(4, 'WWBB', 'BBWW') == 3\nassert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 7\n...\nassert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 3\n```\n\nThe last one is a duplicate of second but expects 3, contradictory. Let's double-check test list:\n\n```\nassert candidate(4, 'WWBB', 'BBWW') == 3\nassert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 7\n...\nassert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 3\n```\n\nThe last line says assert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 3. That's contradictory because earlier same input expects 7. Let's re-express: The test suite includes both:\n\n- assert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 7\n- later assert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 3\n\nActually they have both: earlier they had \"assert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 7\" after many test cases. Then later they have \"assert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 3\". Let's list all lines:\n\n```\nassert candidate(6, 'BWBWBW', 'WWWBBB') == 4\nassert candidate(6, 'BBBBBB', 'WWWWWW') == -1\nassert candidate(14, 'BBBWBWWWBBWWBW', 'WBWWBBWWWBWBBB') == 7\n\nassert candidate(10, 'BBBBBWBWWW', 'BBWWWWWBBB') == -1\nassert candidate(14, 'WBWBWBWBWBWBWB', 'WWWWWWWBBBBBBB') == 8\nassert candidate(14, 'BWBBBWBBWBBWBB', 'WWWWBBBBBBBBBB') == 8\nassert candidate(14, 'BWBBBBBBBBBBBB', 'WBBBBBBBBBBBBB') == 4\nassert candidate(4, 'WWBB', 'BBWW') == 3\nassert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 7\nassert candidate(12, 'WBWWWWBWBBBB', 'BBBBBBWWWWWW') == 8\nassert candidate(3, 'BBW', 'BBW') == 0\nassert candidate(14, 'BWBWWWBBWBWWBW', 'WWWBBBWWBWBWWB') == 9\nassert candidate(3, 'BBW', 'WBB') == -1\nassert candidate(2, 'BW', 'WB') == -1\nassert candidate(2, 'BW', 'BW') == 0\nassert candidate(12, 'WBWBWBWBBBBB', 'BBBBBBBBWWWW') == 8\nassert candidate(14, 'WWWWWWWWWWWWWW', 'WWWWWWWWWWWWWW') == 0\nassert candidate(14, 'BBWBWBBBBBBBBB', 'WWBBBBBBBBBBBB') == 5\nassert candidate(9, 'WBWBWBWWW', 'WWWWWWBBB') == 6\nassert candidate(4, 'WWBB', 'WBWB') == -1\nassert candidate(14, 'WWWWBWWBWWBWWW', 'BBBWWWWWWWWWWW') == 7\nassert candidate(14, 'WBWBBBWWBWBBWB', 'BBBWWWBBWBWBBW') == 9\nassert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 3\nassert candidate(14, 'BWBWBWBWBWBWBW', 'BWBWBWBWBWBWBW') == 0\n```\n\nYes, there is contradictory: earlier they said 7, later they say 3. That cannot both be true. Let's check the test list more carefully: maybe there is a mistake: earlier they had \"assert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 7\" but later they have \"assert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 3\". Actually the later line is \"assert candidate(4, 'BBWW', 'WWBB') == 3\". Let's verify that it's the same input: 'BBWW' to 'WWBB'. Indeed same. So contradictory test expectations.\n\nThus we need to see which one is correct according to the problem. Let's think manually: n=4, s='BBWW', t='WWBB'. We earlier manually found a sequence of length 7? Actually we earlier manually found a sequence of length 7? Let's compute manually:\n\nStart: 'BBWW..' (positions 0,1 B;2,3 W; empties 4,5). Goal: 'WWBB..' (positions 0,1 W;2,3 B; empties 4,5). The earlier BFS we just traced for 'WWBB' to 'BBWW' gave distance 3. That was from 'WWBB' to 'BBWW' (swap colors). That was distance 3 (as we found). The reverse might be distance 3 also? Since operations are reversible, the distance from A to B equals distance from B to A. So distance from 'BBWW' to 'WWBB' should also be 3. Indeed, we found a sequence of 3 moves from 'WWBB' to 'BBWW': i=0 (move WW to empties), i=2 (move BB to empties), i=4 (move WW back). That transformed 'WWBB..' to 'BBWW..' in 3 moves. The reverse sequence from 'BBWW..' to 'WWBB..' would be the same moves in reverse order: move BB to empties (positions 0,1 to empties 4,5) => state '..WWBB'; then move WW from 2,3 to empties 0,1 => 'WW..BB'; then move BB from 4,5 to empties 2,3 => 'WWBB..'. That's 3 moves. So distance is 3, not 7. So test expecting 7 is likely wrong. But the test suite includes both contradictory expectations. Which one will be used? The test harness will run all asserts; if any fails, the solution fails. So we must produce an implementation that satisfies all asserts. Since they conflict, perhaps one of them is not actually executed? Or maybe they have two separate lines but one is overwritten or something? Let's examine test suite ordering: The duplicate appears near the end, after other tests. But both are present; if we run them sequentially, the second will assert candidate(4,'BBWW','WWBB') == 3, which contradicts earlier expectation of 7; one of them must be wrong. Possibly the earlier expectation of 7 is a mistake, but the later one is correct. Or maybe we misinterpret the problem: Maybe empties not required to be at positions n+1,n+2 at the end? Let's re-evaluate.\n\nIf empties can be anywhere at final, then distance from 'BBWW' to 'WWBB' might be larger because we may need to return empties to original positions? But we always require empties at n+1,n+2 to match target? Let's consider scenario where empties can be anywhere; but the target is just the first n cells matching t, empties can be anywhere. In that case, we could end with empties at other positions as long as first n match. However, our BFS targets empties at original positions. That might cause distance differences. Let's explore.\n\nIf empties can be anywhere, then from 'BBWW' to 'WWBB', we could achieve target in 3 moves as we found, but empties end up at positions 4,5 (original empties). Actually after the sequence we described, final empties are at 4,5, so empties at original positions. So it's consistent. So distance 3.\n\nWhy would distance be 7? Possibly if empties must be at some specific positions other than original? Or maybe the operation definition is different: maybe the two empty cells are always the same two cells (positions n+1,n+2) and you cannot move empties around; you always move the selected pair of stones to the two empty cells (i.e., you must move them to the fixed empty 