urn 0\n    if s.count('B') != t.count('B') or s.count('W') != t.count('W'):\n        return -1\n\n    start = s + '..'\n    target = t + '..'\n\n    from collections import deque\n\n    dq = deque([start])\n    dist = {start:0}\n    while dq:\n        cur = dq.popleft()\n        d = dist[cur]\n        if cur == target:\n            return d\n        # find empties\n        e1 = cur.find('.')\n        e2 = cur.find('.', e1+1)\n        # generate moves\n        # For each i from 0 to n+1-1 (i.e., len(cur)-2)\n        L = len(cur)\n        for i in range(L-1):\n            if cur[i] != '.' and cur[i+1] != '.':\n                # generate new state\n                # We can build as list\n                lst = list(cur)\n                lst[e1] = cur[i]\n                lst[e2] = cur[i+1]\n                lst[i] = '.'\n                lst[i+1] = '.'\n                new_state = ''.join(lst)\n                if new_state not in dist:\n                    dist[new_state] = d+1\n                    dq.append(new_state)\n    return -1\n\nNow we must check correctness for testcases that have large n=14 and answer up to maybe 9. BFS will search up to depth 9; number of states within depth 9 might be moderate.\n\nPotential memory: visited may include many states up to maybe tens of thousands.\n\nEdge Cases: BFS may find target but not minimal? BFS ensures minimal because each edge weight=1.\n\nNow we need to embed in final answer. Also include import.\n\nPotential improvements: Use bitmask representation to speed up. But not needed.\n\nTesting quickly on sample: Let's do quick mental test for n=4, s='WWBB', t='BBWW' expecting 3. BFS should find.\n\nLet's simulate manually:\n\nInitial: positions: 0 W,1 W,2 B,3 B,4 .,5 .\nGoal: B B W W . .\n\nWe need to see if reachable in 3 moves.\n\nPossible moves:\n\nStep 1: Choose adjacent stones at positions 2,3 (B,B) -> move to empties 4,5 => empties become at 2,3, stones B,B at 4,5. New state: W W . . B B\n\nStep 2: Choose adjacent stones