b at position 5 is symmetric to 1? The axis is line through point 5, which is same as line through point 1? No, positions opposite? Actually points are equally spaced, axis through point 5 is at angle 5*(360/6)=300°, same as 5/6 circle. The reflection axis is rotated relative to Alice's axis. So maybe parity matters.\n\nBut test (6,5) not given.\n\nIf n even and k is even but not n/2? (6,2) true (even k not n/2). (20,4) false (n even, k even not n/2). (14,1) true (k odd). (12,7) false (n even, k odd). So parity alone not enough.\n\nMaybe condition depends on gcd(n,k) or gcd(n,2k) and parity of something.\n\nLet's compute gcd(n,k) for each.\n\nI'll compute some.\n\n(6,2): gcd=2 => True.\n(6,3): gcd=3 => False.\n(6,1): gcd=1 => True.\n(200000,100000): gcd=100000 => False.\n(15,3): gcd=3 => False.\n(13,5): gcd=1 => True.\n(7,6): gcd=1 => maybe True? (contradiction). (7,6) is gcd=1. We'll see later.\n(12,7): gcd=1 => False. So gcd=1 can be false.\n\n(14,1): gcd=1 => True.\n(15,5): gcd=5 => False.\n(19,18): gcd=1 => True.\n(27,24): gcd=3 => False.\n(23,15): gcd=1 => True.\n(26,2): gcd=2 => True.\n(20,4): gcd=4 => False.\n(15,7): gcd=1 => True.\n(7,1): gcd=1 => True.\n(18,5): gcd=1 => True.\n(21,17): gcd=1 => True.\n(28,24): gcd=4 => False.\n(24,5): gcd=1 => False.\n(4,2): gcd=2 => False.\n(9,3): gcd=3 => False.\n(9,1): gcd=1 => True.\n(27,6): gcd=3 => False.\n(25,22): gcd=1 => True.\n(19,6): gcd=1 => True.\n(24,3): gcd=3 => False.\n(15,14): gcd=1 => True.\n(21,4): gcd=1 => True.\n(21,15): gcd=3 => False.\n(21,20): gcd=1 => True.\n(3,2): gcd=1 => True.\n(17,3): gcd=1 => True.\n(23,5): gcd=1 => True.\n(1038,484): gcd? 1038=2*3*173, 484=4*121=2^2*11^2. gcd = 2? Actually 1038 even, 1038/2=519; 484/2=242; gcd maybe 2? Let's compute: 1038 factors: 2 * 3 * 173. 484 = 2^2 * 11^2 = 4 *121 = 2^2 *11^2. So common factor = 2. So gcd=2 => True. So gcd=2 can be true.\n(1139,729): 1139 is prime? 1139 =? 1139/7 ~ 162.7; not divisible by 3 (1+1+3+9=14), not 5, maybe 1139= 17*67? 17*67=1139 indeed! So 1139=17*67. 729=3^6. gcd=1 => True.\n(432,377): gcd=1? 432 factors 2^4*3^3. 377=13*29. gcd=1 => False. So gcd=1 can be false.\n(1642,829): 1642=2*821, 829 is prime. gcd=1 => True.\n(791,277): 791 = 7*113? Actually 7*113=791. 277 prime. gcd=1 => True.\n(1745,1009): 1745=5*349, 1009 prime. gcd=1 => True.\n(904,16): gcd? 904=8*113=2^3*113, 16=2^4. gcd=8? Actually 2^3=8, 113 not dividing 16. So gcd=8 => False.\n(736,544): gcd? 736=2^5*23 (32*23=736). 544=2^5*17 (32*17=544). So gcd=32 => False.\n(1944,591): 1944=2^3*3^5 (8*243=1944). 591=3*197. gcd=3 => False.\n(1227,961): 1227=3*409, 961=31^2. gcd=1 => True.\n(152289,139407): compute gcd? Both large. Let's compute difference: 152289-139407=12882. gcd(139407,12882). 139407 mod 12882 =? 12882*10=128820, remainder 106? Actually 139407-128820=106? Wait compute: 12882*10 = 128820, remainder = 106? 139407-128820=106? Let's compute precisely: 139407 - 128820 = 10,587. Actually 128820 is less than 139407, difference = 10,587. Then 12882 mod 10587 = 12882-10587=2295. 10587 mod 2295 = 10587 - 2295*4 = 10587 - 9180 = 1407. 2295 mod 1407 = 2295-1407=888. 1407 mod 888 = 1407-888=519. 888 mod 519 = 369? Actually 519*1=519, remainder 369. 519 mod 369 = 150. 369 mod 150 = 69. 150 mod 69 = 12. 69 mod 12 = 9. 12 mod 9 = 3. 9 mod 3 = 0. So gcd=3. So gcd=3 => False.\n\n(187388,144729): compute gcd? 187388 even, 144729 odd. gcd maybe 1? Let's compute gcd(187388,144729). Use Euclid: 187388 mod 144729 = 42659. 144729 mod 42659 = 144729 - 42659*3 = 144729 - 127977 = 16752. 42659 mod 16752 = 42659 - 16752*2 = 42659 - 33504 = 9155. 16752 mod 9155 = 7597? Actually 9155*1=9155, remainder 7597. 9155 mod 7597 = 1558. 7597 mod 1558 = 7597 - 1558*4 = 7597 - 6232 = 1365. 1558 mod 1365 = 193. 1365 mod 193 = 1365 - 193*7 = 1365 - 1351 = 14. 193 mod 14 = 193 - 14*13 = 193 - 182 = 11. 14 mod 11 = 3. 11 mod 3 = 2. 3 mod 2 = 1. So gcd=1. So gcd=1 => True? Actually answer is False. So gcd=1 can be false.\n\n(167199,10041): gcd? 10041 = 3*3347 (since 3347*3=10041). 167199 mod 10041 =? 10041*16=160656, remainder 6543. 10041 mod 6543 = 10041-6543=3498. 6543 mod 3498 = 6543-3498=3045. 3498 mod 3045 = 453. 3045 mod 453 = 3045 - 453*6 = 3045 - 2718 = 327. 453 mod 327 = 126. 327 mod 126 = 75. 126 mod 75 = 51. 75 mod 51 = 24. 51 mod 24 = 3. 24 mod 3 = 0. So gcd=3. So gcd=3 => False.\n\n(179879,103564): gcd? Let's compute. 179879 mod 103564 = 762... Actually 103564*1=103564, remainder 762... compute: 179879-103564=762... Wait 179879-103564 = 762... let's compute: 179,879 - 103,564 = 76,315. So remainder = 76,315. Next 103,564 mod 76,315 = 27,249. 76,315 mod 27,249 = 21,817? Actually 27,249*2=54,498, remainder 21,817. 27,249 mod 21,817 = 5,432. 21,817 mod 5,432 = 21,817 - 5,432*4=21,817 - 21,728 = 89. 5,432 mod 89 = 5,432 - 89*61=5,432 - 5,429 = 3. 89 mod 3 = 2 (since 3*29=87, remainder2). 3 mod 2 =1. So gcd=1. So gcd=1 => True.\n\n(197276,31462): gcd? 197276 mod 31462 =? 31462*6=188,772, remainder 8,504. 31462 mod 8504 = 31462 - 8504*3 = 31462 - 25,512 = 5,950. 8504 mod 5950 = 2554. 5950 mod 2554 = 842? Actually 2554*2=5108, remainder 842. 2554 mod 842 = 2554 -842*3=2554-2526=28. 842 mod 28 = 842 - 28*30 = 842 - 840 = 2. 28 mod 2 = 0 => gcd=2. So gcd=2 => False.\n\n(176462,90857): gcd? 176462 mod 90857 = 855... compute: 90857*1=90857, remainder = 176462 - 90857 = 85605. 90857 mod 85605 = 5252. 85605 mod 5252 = 85605 - 5252*16=85605 - 84,032 = 1,573? Actually 5252*16 = 84,032, remainder = 1,573. 5252 mod 1573 = 5252 - 1573*3 = 5252 - 4719 = 533. 1573 mod 533 = 1573 - 533*2 = 1573 - 1066 = 507. 533 mod 507 = 26. 507 mod 26 = 507 - 26*19 = 507 - 494 = 13. 26 mod 13 = 0 => gcd=13. So gcd=13 => False.\n\n(182506,13410): gcd? 182506 mod 13410 =? 13410*13=174330, remainder 8,176. 13410 mod 8176 = 5,234. 8176 mod 5234 = 2,942. 5234 mod 2942 = 2,292? Actually 2942*1=2942, remainder 2,292. 2942 mod 2292 = 650. 2292 mod 650 = 342? Actually 650*3=1950, remainder 342. 650 mod 342 = 308? Actually 342*1=342 remainder 308. 342 mod 308 = 34. 308 mod 34 = 2? Actually 34*9=306 remainder 2. 34 mod 2 = 0 => gcd=2. So gcd=2 => True.\n\n(151355,71579): gcd? 151355 mod 71579 = 79? Actually 71579*2=143158, remainder 8,197. 71579 mod 8197 = 71579 - 8197*8=71579 - 65576 = 6003. 8197 mod 6003 = 2194. 6003 mod 2194 = 6003 - 2194*2=6003 - 4388 = 1615. 2194 mod 1615 = 579. 1615 mod 579 = 1615 - 579*2=1615 -1158 = 457. 579 mod 457 = 122. 457 mod 122 = 457 - 122*3 = 457 - 366 = 91. 122 mod 91 = 31. 91 mod 31 = 28? Actually 31*2=62 remainder 29. Wait compute: 91-62=29. 31 mod 29 = 2. 29 mod 2 = 1. So gcd=1 => True.\n\n(198493,8985): gcd? 198493 mod 8985 =? 8985*22=197670, remainder 823. 8985 mod 823 = 8985 - 823*10=8985 -8230 =755. 823 mod 755 = 68. 755 mod 68 = 755 -68*11=755 -748=7. 68 mod 7 = 68 - 7*9=68 -63=5. 7 mod 5 =2. 5 mod 2=1. So gcd=1 => True.\n\n(166640,152569): gcd? difference = 14071. Compute gcd(152569,14071). 152569 mod 14071 =? 14071*10=140710, remainder 11859. 14071 mod 11859 = 2212. 11859 mod 2212 = 11859 - 2212*5=11859 -11060=799. 2212 mod 799 = 2212 -799*2=2212 -1598 = 614. 799 mod 614 =185. 614 mod 185 = 614-185*3=614-555=59. 185 mod 59 = 185-59*3=185-177=8. 59 mod 8 = 3 (8*7=56 remainder 3). 8 mod 3 = 2. 3 mod 2 =1. So gcd=1 => False.\n\nThus no simple gcd pattern.\n\nMaybe condition depends on parity of n/gcd(n,k) or something.\n\nWe can try to derive known solution: This problem appears known on Codeforces or similar. Perhaps it's \"Circle Coloring\" problem: The answer is True iff n is not divisible by 2 * gcd(n,k) or something.\n\nLet's think more about the process.\n\nWe need to be able to eventually color all points. Since they cooperatively maximize colored points, they can decide which points to color.\n\nOne approach: Represent the set S after each move as invariant under either f_A or f_B. So S alternates between being invariant under f_A (after Alice's move) and f_B (after Bob's move). Starting with empty set S0 = {}. The first move is Alice's. After Alice's move, S1 must be invariant under f_A. She can color any point that satisfies symmetry: either a fixed point (0 or maybe n/2) or a point whose reflection is already black (none initially). So first move, Alice can only color a fixed point of f_A. Fixed points of f_A are points i such that -i ≡ i (mod n) => 2i ≡ 0 mod n => i ≡ 0 (mod n/2). So there are either 1 or 2 fixed points: i=0 always, and also i=n/2 if n even. So Alice's first move can be point 0, or if n even also point n/2. But Alice is at point 0, but can she color any point? The rule says they can color any white point, with constraint that after coloring, the pattern must be symmetric w.r.t their line. So they can color any point that yields symmetry. For Alice's first move, she can color point 0 (fixed). Also she could color point n/2 (if n even) because that is also fixed under reflection through 0? Let's check: reflection about axis through point 0 maps point n/2 to -n/2 mod n = n - n/2 = n/2 (since n/2 = -n/2 mod n). So yes, n/2 is also fixed point. So Alice can start by coloring point 0 or n/2.\n\nAfter Alice colors a fixed point, S1 = {fixed}. That set is symmetric under f_A.\n\nNow Bob's turn: his axis is through point k. He must add a point such that S2 = S1 ∪ {x} is invariant under f_B. Since S1 may not be invariant under f_B, we need to add x such that the set becomes invariant under f_B.\n\nThus we need to find x such that S1 ∪ {x} = f_B(S1 ∪ {x}). That is, for each point in S1, its reflection under f_B must be in set. Since S1 may have points not symmetric under f_B, we need to add appropriate points to pair them.\n\nThus the process is akin to building a set that alternates invariance under the two reflections. At each step, we can add one point (and potentially rely on existing points to satisfy symmetry for the other point). So we need to be able to gradually fill all points.\n\nOne approach: This is possible iff the group generated by f_A and f_B acts transitively and the process can be done by adding one point at a time respecting constraints.\n\nI recall a known result: For two reflections generating a dihedral group, you can generate the whole group via alternating multiplication of reflections. The group generated by f_A and f_B is dihedral; the elements are identity, f_A, f_B, r, r^2, ... where r = f_B ∘ f_A = rotation by 2k.\n\nThus we can represent each point as a group element applied to a base point? For any i, we can map from 0 via rotations: i = r^{t}(0). Since r(i) = i+2k, we can generate positions reachable by multiples of 2k from 0. The orbit of 0 under rotation by 2k is all points i such that i ≡ t*2k mod n. This orbit size = n / gcd(n,2k). So if gcd(n,2k) = 1, the orbit is all points. If gcd > 1, we can only reach a subset.\n\nBut we also have ability to reflect, not just rotate. Starting from 0, we can get to any point via composition of reflections and rotations. Actually the dihedral group can map any point to any other point because it's transitive on vertices of regular n-gon: the dihedral group D_n (order 2n) includes rotations and reflections. However the reflections we have are not arbitrary: only two specific reflections, not all n reflections. The group generated by two reflections is indeed the dihedral group D_m where m is the order of the rotation r = f_B ∘ f_A (which is rotation by 2k). This dihedral group acts on n points but its action may have multiple orbits: each orbit is of size m (maybe). Actually the dihedral group D_m acts on the vertices of a regular m-gon. But we have n points; the action of D_m on Z_n is via rotation by 2k and reflection about 0 (f_A). The orbit of a point under this group is the set {i + t*2k mod n, -i + t*2k mod n}. So the orbit size is either m (if i not fixed) or m/2 if i is symmetric? Actually the orbit size is m unless i is invariant under the reflection after some shift.\n\nThus the set of points can be partitioned into orbits of size equal to the order of rotation r (denoted m = n/gcd(n,2k)). If m = n (gcd=1) then whole set is one orbit, else there are gcd(n,2k) orbits.\n\nThus if gcd(n,2k) > 1, you cannot map between orbits using allowed transformations (reflections and rotations). So maybe you cannot color points from different orbits because the symmetry constraints restrict you to stay within an orbit? Let's examine.\n\nIf we start with S = {}. Alice colors 0. That is in orbit O0 = {0, 2k, 4k, ...} maybe. Actually 0 is fixed under rotations? Rotating 0 by 2k yields 2k (mod n), so 0 is not fixed unless 2k ≡ 0 mod n (i.e., n divides 2k). So orbit of 0 includes multiples of 2k. So if gcd(n,2k) > 1, the orbit is a proper subset. But maybe we can later add points from other orbits by using the other reflection? Let's see.\n\nAfter Alice's first move, S = {0} (or {n/2}). Then Bob must add a point to make S symmetric under f_B. Since S currently contains 0, we need to have its reflection under f_B also present. Reflection of 0 under f_B is f_B(0) = 2k - 0 = 2k (mod n). So Bob must add point 2k (or maybe if 0 is also fixed under f_B? That would be if 0 = k mod n or 0 = k + n/2 mod n, i.e., k = 0 or k = n/2, but k is between 1 and n-1). So Bob must add 2k (mod n). That satisfies symmetry because now S = {0,2k}, and f_B maps 0↔2k, so invariance holds.\n\nThus after Bob's move, S = {0,2k}. This set is also invariant under f_A? Not necessarily, but not needed until Alice's turn.\n\nNow Alice's turn again. She must add a point to S such that S ∪ {x} is invariant under f_A. Currently S = {0,2k}. Reflection under f_A maps 0↔0, 2k↔ -2k = n-2k. So to be invariant under f_A, we need to also have n-2k in the set. Currently we don't have it. So Alice can add x = n-2k, which is the reflection of 2k under f_A. So S becomes {0,2k,n-2k}. This is symmetric under f_A (0 fixed, 2k↔n-2k). Good.\n\nNow Bob's turn: set S = {0,2k,n-2k}. Need to make it symmetric under f_B. Let's see f_B maps 0↔2k, as before, and also maps n-2k ↔ ? Compute f_B(n-2k) = 2k - (n-2k) = 4k - n. mod n = (4k mod n) - n? Actually mod n, 4k - n ≡ 4k (mod n). So f_B maps n-2k ↔ 4k (mod n). So we need to add point 4k to pair with n-2k. Also we need to ensure that after adding, the set is invariant: we have already 0↔2k pair, and we need to add 4k to have its reflection  n-2k present, which we already have. So after Bob adds 4k, S becomes {0,2k,n-2k,4k}. This set is invariant under f_B: pairs (0↔2k), (n-2k↔4k). Good.\n\nNow Alice's turn: need to pair 4k's reflection: f_A(4k) = -4k = n-4k. Also need to reflect n-2k? Already paired with 2k? Actually under f_A, 2k ↔ n-2k (already both present). So need to add n-4k. So S adds n-4k. Then S = {0,2k,n-2k,4k,n-4k}. Now invariant under f_A.\n\nContinuing, this pattern will generate points: at each Bob turn, we add 2t*k (for t=1,2,...). At each Alice turn, we add n - 2t*k. So after t rounds, we will have points {0, 2k, n-2k, 4k, n-4k, ... up to 2t*k, n-2t*k}. This will continue until we fill the orbit of size m = n/gcd(n,2k) maybe.\n\nThus the process will generate points in the orbit generated by multiples of 2k and their reflections.\n\nThus the condition for being able to color all points is that the orbit generated by multiples of 2k (and reflections) covers all points. That is, the order of rotation r = n/gcd(n,2k) must be n, i.e., gcd(n,2k) = 1? However we saw (6,2) has gcd(6,4)=2 => order = 6/2=3. Yet we can fill all points? Let's test with process.\n\nn=6, k=2. Then 2k = 4 mod6. Multiples of 4 mod6: 0,4,2,? Actually 0*4=0, 1*4=4, 2*4=8 mod6=2, 3*4=12 mod6=0 repeats after 3 steps. So orbit size = 3. So points reachable from 0 by rotation are {0,4,2}. These are three points out of six. The other three points are {1,3,5}. Are those reachable? Perhaps they belong to another orbit. Starting from 0, we only get {0,2,4}. However we might also be able to start with Alice choosing n/2 = 3 as first point (if n even) rather than 0. Let's see.\n\nIf Alice first colors point 3 (n/2) instead of 0. Then S1 = {3}. Bob's reflection of 3 under f_B: f_B(3) = 2k - 3 = 4 - 3 = 1 mod6. So Bob must add 1. Then S2 = {3,1}. Then Alice's reflection of 1 under f_A: -1 = 5. She adds 5. S3 = {3,1,5}. Bob's reflection of 5 under f_B: 2k -5 = 4 -5 = -1 =5 mod6? Wait compute: 4-5 = -1 mod6 = 5. So 5 is fixed under f_B? Actually check: f_B(5) = 2*2 -5 =4-5 = -1 ≡ 5 (mod6). So 5 is a fixed point of f_B (since 2k=4). Indeed because 5 satisfies i = 2k - i => 2i = 2k => i = k =2? Wait solve i = 4 - i => 2i = 4 => i =2 mod6? Actually 2*5 =10 mod6=4 => 10 mod6 =4, not 4? Let's solve: i = 2k - i mod n => 2i ≡ 2k (mod n). For n=6, 2k=4. So 2i ≡4 mod6 => i ≡2 mod3. Solutions: i=2,5? Let's check i=2: 2*2=4 mod6 => OK. i=5: 2*5=10 mod6 =4 => OK. So both 2 and 5 are fixed points of f_B. Indeed f_B(2) = 4-2=2, f_B(5) =4-5 = -1 mod6 =5. So both 2 and 5 are fixed. So after adding 5, S3 = {3,1,5} is symmetric under f_A? Let's check f_A reflection: -3 = 3 (since n/2 = 3 fixed). -1 =5, -5=1. So yes symmetric. Good.\n\nNow Bob's turn: need invariance under f_B. S3 = {3,1,5}. Let's compute f_B mapping: f_B(3) = 4-3 =1 (present), f_B(1) = 4-1 =3 (present), f_B(5) =5 (fixed). So S3 is already invariant under f_B! So Bob can skip? Actually he must color a point, but can he color a point that maintains invariance? He could color a fixed point of f_B, i.e., 2 (or 5 but 5 already black). So he can color 2. After adding 2, S4 = {3,1,5,2}. Is S4 invariant under f_B? Check f_B(2) =2 (fixed), others fine. So yes.\n\nNow Alice's turn: need invariance under f_A. S4 includes 2 and 5 which are a pair under f_A: -2 = 4 (mod6) not present. Wait -2 = n-2 = 4, not present. So S4 is not invariant under f_A. To fix, Alice can add point 4 (the reflection of 2). So S5 = {3,1,5,2,4}. Now invariance under f_A: check pairs: 3 fixed, 1↔5, 2↔4. Good.\n\nNow Bob's turn: need invariance under f_B. Let's see f_B mapping: f_B(3) = 1, present; f_B(1) = 3, present; f_B(5) =5; f_B(2) =2; f_B(4) =? f_B(4) = 4-4=0? Actually f_B(4) = 2k -4 =4-4=0. 0 not present. So S5 is not invariant under f_B; need to add 0. So Bob adds 0. S6 = all six points {0,1,2,3,4,5}. After Bob's move, set invariant under f_B? Check: f_B(0) =4 (present), f_B(4) =0 (present). So yes. After that, all points are colored; game ends. So indeed (6,2) works.\n\nThus process can succeed even when gcd(n,2k) > 1, because we can start with a different fixed point (n/2) and then generate the other orbit.\n\nThus condition likely depends on parity of n and parity of k? Let's explore.\n\nObservation: For n even, there are two fixed points of f_A: 0 and n/2. For f_B, there are also possibly two fixed points: k and (k + n/2) mod n (if n even). So we have choices for initial point.\n\nIf n is odd, each reflection has only one fixed point (the point of the player). For Alice, only 0. For Bob, only k.\n\nThus for n odd, the process may be forced to start with 0 (since Alice's only fixed point is 0). After that, Bob must add 2k, etc. The orbit generated is multiples of 2k. Since n odd, 2 is invertible mod n, so multiples of 2k generate same orbit as multiples of k? Actually gcd(n,2k) = gcd(n,k) because n odd, gcd(n,2) = 1. So orbit size = n / gcd(n,k). So if gcd(n,k) = 1, the orbit is full, else not.\n\nThus for n odd, condition likely: gcd(n,k) = 1 for success. Let's test.\n\nTest odd n cases:\n\n- (15,3) n=15 odd, k=3, gcd(15,3)=3>1, answer False. Good.\n- (13,5) n=13 odd, gcd=1 => True. Good.\n- (15,5) n=15 odd, gcd(15,5)=5 >1 => False. Good.\n- (19,18) n=19 odd, gcd(19,18)=1 => True. Good.\n- (27,24) n=27 odd, gcd(27,24)=3 => False. Good.\n- (23,15) n=23 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n- (20,4) n even, ignore.\n- (15,7) n=15 odd, gcd=1 => True. Good.\n- (9,3) n=9 odd, gcd=3 => False. Good.\n- (9,1) gcd=1 => True.\n- (27,6) n=27 odd, gcd(27,6)=3 => False.\n- (25,22) n=25 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n- (19,6) n=19 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n- (15,14) n=15 odd, gcd(15,14)=1 => True.\n- (21,17) n=21 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n- (21,15) n=21 odd, gcd=3 => False.\n- (21,4) n=21 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n- (21,20) n=21 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n- (3,1) n=3 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n- (3,2) n=3 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n- (5,2) n=5 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n- (7,1) n=7 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n- (7,6) n=7 odd, gcd=1 => True (both True and False contradictory). So likely True.\n\n- (13,5) repeated True.\n\n- (23,5) n=23 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n\n- (17,3) n=17 odd, gcd=1 => True.\n\n- (19,18) True.\n\n- (23,15) True.\n\n- (19,6) True.\n\n- (21,17) True.\n\n- (21,4) True.\n\n- (21,20) True.\n\n- (27,24) false.\n\n- (27,6) false.\n\n- (27,??) there is (27,24) false.\n\n- (27,6) false.\n\n- (27,??) (27,24) false.\n\n- (27,??) (27,6) false.\n\n- (27,??) (27,??) (27,24) false.\n\n- (27,??) (27,??) ok.\n\nThus for odd n, condition appears to be gcd(n,k) == 1.\n\nNow test odd n with gcd=1 but answer false? Let's see if any odd n case with gcd=1 is false.\n\nList odd n cases with gcd=1:\n\n- (13,5) True.\n- (19,18) True.\n- (23,15) True.\n- (15,7) True.\n- (9,1) True.\n- (25,22) True.\n- (19,6) True.\n- (15,14) True.\n- (21,17) True.\n- (21,4) True.\n- (21,20) True.\n- (3,1) True.\n- (3,2) True.\n- (5,2) True.\n- (7,1) True.\n- (13,5) duplicate.\n- (23,5) True.\n- (17,3) True.\n- (13,5) True.\n- (23,5) True.\n- (13,5) already.\n\nAll true. So pattern holds.\n\nNow test odd n with gcd > 1:\n\n- (15,3) false.\n- (15,5) false.\n- (27,24) gcd(27,24)=3 false.\n- (27,6) gcd=3 false.\n- (9,3) false.\n- (21,15) gcd=3 false.\n- (15,3) false.\n- (9,3) false.\n- (27,24) false.\n- (27,6) false.\n- (21,15) false.\n- (15,3) false.\n- (15,5) false.\n- (27,??) etc.\n\nAll false.\n\nThus for odd n, answer = (gcd(n,k) == 1). Good.\n\nNow for even n, condition is more complex.\n\nEven n examples:\n\n- (6,2) True, gcd(6,2)=2 >1. (6,1) True gcd=1. (6,3) False gcd=3>1. (6,5) unknown.\n\n- (4,2) False (n even, k=n/2). gcd=2 >1.\n\n- (200000,100000) n even, k=n/2, answer false.\n\n- (200000,1) false (k=1, gcd=1). Interesting: n=200000 even, k=1 => gcd=1 but answer false. So for even n, gcd=1 is not sufficient.\n\n- (200000,100000) false (k=n/2). So not sufficient.\n\n- (14,1) True (gcd=1). So why (200000,1) false? Something else.\n\n- (12,7) False (n even, k=7, gcd(12,7)=1). So false.\n\n- (14,1) true.\n\n- (12,7) false.\n\n- (14,1) true.\n\n- (18,5) true (gcd(18,5)=1).\n\n- (26,2) true (gcd=2). (n=26 even, k=2, gcd=2). True.\n\n- (20,4) false (gcd=4). (n even, k=4). false.\n\n- (28,24) false (gcd=4). false.\n\n- (24,5) false (gcd=1). false.\n\n- (9,1) true (odd case). Not relevant.\n\n- (24,3) false (gcd=3). false.\n\n- (14,1) true (gcd=1). true.\n\n- (12,7) false (gcd=1). false.\n\n- (12,7) appears twice false.\n\n- (4,2) false (gcd=2). false.\n\n- (2,1) true (n=2 even, k=1). gcd=1 true.\n\n- (6,2) true (gcd=2). true.\n\n- (6,3) false (gcd=3). false.\n\n- (6,1) true (gcd=1). true.\n\n- (200000,1) false (gcd=1). So something else.\n\n- (100000?) Not needed.\n\n- (14,1) true.\n\n- (20,4) false.\n\n- (20,??) not given.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,3) false.\n\n- (24,5) duplicate false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\nOk.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\nNow (24,5) false despite gcd=1.\n\n- (24,5) false also maybe due to something else.\n\n- (24,5) appears again later but same result.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\n- (24,5) false.\n\nThus pattern: For even n, sometimes gcd=1 yields false (like (12,7), (24,5), (200000,1)). But (14,1) true, (18,5) true, (26,2) true, (6,1) true, (6,2) true, (2,1) true.\n\nThus need further condition.\n\nMaybe the condition is that k is odd? Let's see:\n\n- n=6 even, k=2 (even) -> true.\n- n=6, k=1 (odd) -> true.\n- n=6, k=3 (odd) -> false. So odd k not guarantee.\n\nCheck n=12 even, k=7 (odd) -> false. n=14, k=1 (odd) -> true. n=18, k=5 (odd) -> true. n=20, k=4 (even) -> false. n=28, k=24 (even) -> false. n=24, k=5 (odd) -> false. n=24, k=3 (odd) -> false. n=12,7 false. So parity not enough.\n\nMaybe condition is that k and n/2 share parity? Not sure.\n\nLet's think about the process for even n. We have two possible fixed points for Alice: 0 and n/2. She can start at either.\n\nIf she starts at 0, then Bob must add 2k. Then Alice must add n-2k, etc. This process generates points from the orbit of multiples of 2k, as earlier. If she starts at n/2, then Bob must add f_B(n/2) = 2k - n/2 = 2k - n/2 mod n. Let's compute: n/2 = n/2. So 2k - n/2 mod n = 2k - n/2 (if that is non-negative) else plus n. So we get a different starting point.\n\nThus we can generate two orbits: one from 0 and one from n/2.\n\nIf the orbits together cover all points, then we can color all points.\n\nThus condition: The set generated by multiples of 2k (starting from 0) plus the set generated by multiples of 2k plus n/2? Actually start from n/2, then Bob adds point f_B(n/2) = 2k - n/2. Then Alice adds its reflection etc. So effectively we generate points of form n/2 + t*2k and also n - (n/2 + t*2k) = n/2 - t*2k? Actually let's derive.\n\nLet's denote r(i) = i + 2k (mod n). Starting from 0 we get orbit O0 = {0, 2k, 4k, ..., (m-1)*2k} where m = n / gcd(n,2k). Starting from n/2 we get orbit Ohalf = {n/2, n/2 + 2k, n/2 + 4k, ...} (mod n). Since n even, adding multiples of 2k cycles modulo n; the orbit size is same m = n / gcd(n,2k). However O0 and Ohalf might be the same or disjoint, depending on whether n/2 is in O0. If n/2 is in O0, then the two orbits coincide; otherwise they are distinct and together they form all points (since there are n points, each orbit size m, and we have two orbits => 2*m = n => m = n/2 => gcd(n,2k) = 2). So if gcd(n,2k) = 2, then m = n/2, and O0 and Ohalf are disjoint, covering all points.\n\nIf gcd(n,2k) > 2, then m < n/2, and even with both orbits we may not cover all points (since 2*m = 2*(n/gcd) = 2n/gcd < n if gcd>2). So can't cover all points.\n\nIf gcd(n,2k) = 1, then m = n, O0 alone covers all points; Ohalf also same as O0 (since n/2 is in O0 because gcd=1 => rotation by 2k generates all residues, includes n/2). So O0 = entire set; can succeed with Alice starting at 0. So why (12,7) false? Compute n=12 even, k=7, 2k = 14 mod12 = 2. gcd(12,2) = 2. So gcd(n,2k)=2. Then m = n/2 = 6. So O0 has size 6; Ohalf also size 6, but they may be disjoint? O0 = {0,2,4,6,8,10}. Ohalf = {6,8,10,0,2,4}? Actually n/2 = 6, adding multiples of 2 gives {6,8,10,0,2,4}. That is same set as O0. So O0 and Ohalf are not disjoint; they coincide. So the union is just 6 points, not all 12. Indeed we can't cover all points. So (12,7) false matches this.\n\nThus condition: We need that O0 ∪ Ohalf = all points. That holds if either:\n\n- gcd(n,2k) = 1 (then O0 = all points, success)\n- OR gcd(n,2k) = 2 (then O0 size = n/2, and Ohalf is disjoint from O0, covering the other half, success) provided Ohalf indeed is disjoint from O0 (which holds if n/2 is NOT in O0). Since O0 size n/2, if O0 includes n/2, then Ohalf = O0. But O0 includes n/2 iff n/2 is reachable from 0 by multiples of 2k, i.e., there exists t such that t*2k ≡ n/2 (mod n). That is 2k*t ≡ n/2 (mod n). Multiply both sides by 2: 4k*t ≡ n (mod 2n?) Might be easier: Since n even, dividing equation by gcd(2k,n) which is 2 (assuming gcd=2) we can test.\n\nLet d = gcd(n,2k). If d=2, then we can reduce mod n/d = n/2. The equation t*(2k/d) ≡ (n/2)/d? Actually n/2 = (n/d)*(d/2). Since d=2, n/d = n/2. So we want t*(2k/2) ≡ n/2 (mod n). Equivalent to t*k ≡ n/2 (mod n). Since k is odd? Not necessarily. Let's examine.\n\nIf d=2, then 2k = 2 * k' where k' = k (mod n/2?). Actually gcd(n,2k) = 2 => n even, and k may be odd? Let's see: n even, 2k always even, gcd includes factor 2; any extra factor from k and n/2.\n\nIf n has a factor 2 and maybe other common factors with k.\n\nThus if d=2, O0 = {0, 2k, 4k, ...} modulo n. Since step size is 2k, which is 2*(k) mod n. Since gcd(2k,n)=2, the step size modulo n cycles through n/2 distinct residues: those residues that are even multiples? Actually they will be the set of residues that are multiples of 2 (i.e., even numbers) maybe? Let's see with n=12, k=7, 2k=2, O0 = {0,2,4,6,8,10} = all even numbers. So O0 = all even points. Ohalf = {6,8,10,0,2,4} = also even numbers. So both are same because n/2=6 is already even. So O0 includes n/2 because n/2 is even.\n\nNow consider n=6, k=2: n even, 2k=4, gcd(6,4)=2 => d=2. O0 = {0,4,2} = {0,2,4}. That's all even numbers (0,2,4). Ohalf = {3,1,5}? Let's compute n/2=3. Ohalf = {3, (3+4)=7 mod6=1, (3+8)=11 mod6=5}. So Ohalf = {3,1,5} = all odd numbers. So O0 and Ohalf are disjoint and together cover all points. Indeed n/2=3 is odd, not in O0.\n\nThus condition for success when d=2: O0 must not contain n/2. That is, n/2 must be odd with respect to O0? Actually O0 includes residues reachable from 0 by steps of 2k. Since O0 = { t*2k mod n : t=0..(m-1) }, where m=n/2. Since 2k is even, O0 will consist of residues that are multiples of gcd(2k,n)=2, i.e., all even residues. Indeed if n even, any multiple of 2 is even. So O0 = set of even residues (if gcd=2). Since 0 is even, n/2 may be even or odd depending on n/2 parity. If n/2 is even, then n/2 belongs to O0 (since it's even). If n/2 is odd, then n/2 is not in O0. So we need n/2 odd, i.e., n/2 is odd => n/2 % 2 = 1 => n divisible by 2 but not by 4 (i.e., n ≡ 2 mod 4). For n divisible by 4, n/2 is even, so O0 includes n/2, and Ohalf = O0, failing to cover odd residues. Indeed examples:\n\n- n=6 (6 ≡ 2 mod 4) => success (true). Indeed n/2=3 odd.\n\n- n=10 (10 ≡ 2 mod 4) would likely be true for some k with gcd=2? Let's test with maybe k=3 (2k=6, gcd(10,6)=2). Would we expect true? Possibly.\n\n- n=12 (12 ≡ 0 mod4) => n/2=6 even, O0 includes n/2, Ohalf same as O0, fails, false as seen.\n\n- n=14 (14 ≡ 2 mod4). For (14,1) we have 2k=2, gcd(14,2)=2. O0 = even residues {0,2,4,6,8,10,12}. n/2=7 odd, not in O0. So Ohalf = {7,9,11,13,1,3,5} = odd residues, disjoint, covers all points. So (14,1) true. Good.\n\n- n=18 (18 ≡ 2 mod4). 2k for k=5 is 10, gcd(18,10)=2. O0 = multiples of 10 mod18: 0,10,2,12,4,14,6,16,8,? Actually step size 10 mod18. Since gcd=2, O0 size=9 (n/2). O0 likely includes all even residues? Let's see: multiples of 10: 0,10, (20 mod18=2), (30 mod18=12), (40 mod18=4), (50 mod18=14), (60 mod18=6), (70 mod18=16), (80 mod18=8), (90 mod18=0) stops. So O0 = {0,2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16} = all even residues. n/2=9 odd, not in O0, so Ohalf = odd residues => true.\n\nThus condition for even n appears to be:\n\nLet d = gcd(n, 2k). If d == 1: success (since O0 = all points).\n\nIf d == 2: success iff n/2 is odd (i.e., n % 4 == 2). Because O0 = all even residues, Ohalf = odd residues (if n/2 odd). If n%4==0, O0 includes n/2 even, Ohalf = O0, fails.\n\nIf d > 2: failure (since O0 size < n/2, cannot cover all points even with Ohalf). Let's test against examples:\n\n- (6,2): n=6, 2k=4, gcd=2, n%4=2 => success True. matches.\n\n- (6,1): 2k=2, gcd(6,2)=2, n%4=2 => True. matches.\n\n- (6,3): 2k=6, gcd(6,6)=6 >2 => failure. matches false.\n\n- (12,7): 2k=14 mod12=2, gcd(12,2)=2, n%4=0 => failure. matches false.\n\n- (14,1): 2k=2, gcd=2, n%4=2 => true.\n\n- (18,5): 2k=10, gcd(18,10)=2, n%4=2 => true.\n\n- (20,4): 2k=8, gcd(20,8)=4 >2 => false. matches.\n\n- (28,24): 2k=48 mod28=20, gcd(28,20)=4 >2 => false. matches.\n\n- (24,5): 2k=10, gcd(24,10)=2, n%4=0 => false. matches.\n\n- (24,3): 2k=6, gcd(24,6)=6 >2 => false. matches.\n\n- (24,5) false (as above). Good.\n\n- (200000,1): n=200000 even, n%4? 200000 % 4 = 0 (since 200000 divisible by 4). 2k = 2, gcd(200000,2) = 2, d=2, but n%4=0 => failure. Indeed answer false. Good.\n\n- (200000,100000): 2k = 200000 mod200000 = 0, gcd(n,0) = n = 200000 >2 => false.\n\n- (2,1): n=2, n%4=2 (2%4=2). d = gcd(2,2)=2. d=2, n%4=2 => true. matches.\n\n- (4,2): n=4, n%4=0, d = gcd(4,4)=4 >2 => false (also d>2). matches false.\n\n- (8,? not given). Let's test (8,1): n=8, n%4=0, 2k=2, d=gcd(8,2)=2, d=2 but n%4=0 => false predicted. Let's see if we can manually think. Possibly false.\n\n- (8,3): 2k=6, gcd(8,6)=2, n%4=0 => false predicted. Might be false.\n\n- (8,2): 2k=4, gcd(8,4)=4 >2 => false.\n\n- (10,1): n%4=2, d=gcd(10,2)=2 => true predicted. Not given but could be true.\n\n- (10,3): 2k=6, gcd(10,6)=2 => true.\n\n- (10,5): 2k=10, gcd=10 >2 => false.\n\n- (10,4): 2k=8, gcd=2 => true.\n\nNow test against more given examples: (26,2) n=26, n%4=2 (since 26%4=2). 2k=4, gcd(26,4)=2 => true predicted. Indeed answer true.\n\n- (26,? other) not given.\n\n- (26,? ) maybe.\n\n- (26,13) would be k=13 = n/2 => d=gcd(26,26)=26 >2 false. Likely false.\n\n- (26,5): 2k=10, gcd(26,10)=2, n%4=2 => true. Not given.\n\n- (30,? etc). Not needed.\n\nNow test against some large examples.\n\nLarge ones:\n\n- (1038, 484): n=1038 even? 1038 %2 = 0 (since 1038 even). n%4 = 1038%4 = 2? 4*259 = 1036, remainder 2 => n%4=2. Compute 2k = 2*484 = 968. Compute gcd(1038,968). Let's compute gcd(1038,968): 1038-968=70, gcd(968,70) -> 70*13=910, remainder 58, gcd(70,58)=12? Actually 70 mod 58 =12, 58 mod12=10, 12 mod10=2, 10 mod2=0 => gcd=2. So d=2, n%4=2 => success True (given True). Good.\n\n- (1139, 729): n=1139 odd => use odd rule: gcd(1139,729)=? 1139 prime factors 17*67. 729 = 3^6. gcd=1 => True. matches.\n\n- (432,377): n=432 even, n%4? 432%4=0. 2k = 754? Actually 2*377=754. Compute gcd(432,754). Since 754 >432, gcd(432,754) = gcd(432,754-432=322). gcd(432,322) = gcd(322,110) = gcd(110,102) = gcd(102,8) = gcd(8,6) = gcd(6,2) = 2. So d=2, n%4=0 => predicted false. Indeed answer false.\n\n- (1642,829): n even? 1642%2=0, n%4=2? 4*410=1640, remainder 2 => n%4=2. Compute 2k = 2*829 = 1658. gcd(1642,1658) = gcd(1642,1658-1642=16) = gcd(1642,16). 1642 mod16 = 1642 - 16*102 = 1642 - 1632 = 10. gcd(16,10)=2. So d=2, n%4=2 => true. Answer True.\n\n- (791,277): n odd, gcd(791,277)=? 791 mod277 = 791 - 277*2 = 791 - 554 = 237. 277 mod237 = 40. 237 mod40 = 37. 40 mod37 = 3. 37 mod3 = 1. So gcd=1 => True. matches.\n\n- (1745,1009): n odd, gcd? 1745 mod 1009 = 736. 1009 mod 736 = 273. 736 mod 273 = 190? Actually 273*2=546, remainder 190. 273 mod 190 = 83. 190 mod 83 = 24. 83 mod 24 = 11. 24 mod 11 = 2. 11 mod2=1. So gcd=1 => True. matches.\n\n- (904,16): n even, n%4=0? 904%4=0 (since 4*226=904). Compute 2k=32, gcd(904,32) = gcd(904 mod32 =? 32*28=896, remainder 8, so gcd(32,8)=8. d=8 >2 => false. matches false.\n\n- (736,544): n even, n%4=0 (736%4=0). 2k=1088 mod736 = 352? Actually compute 2*544 = 1088. 1088 mod736 = 352. gcd(736,352) =? 736 mod352 = 32, gcd(352,32)=32. d=32>2 => false.\n\n- (1944,591): n even, n%4=0? 1944%4=0 (since 4*486=1944). 2k=1182. gcd(1944,1182) = gcd(1944-1182=762,1182) = gcd(762,1182) = gcd(762,420) = gcd(420,342) = gcd(342,78) = gcd(78,342%78=342-78*4=342-312=30) = gcd(78,30)=gcd(30,18)=gcd(18,12)=gcd(12,6)=6. So d=6 >2 => false. matches false.\n\n- (1227,961): n odd, gcd=1 => true. matches.\n\n- (152289,139407): n odd? 152289 is odd (since last digit 9). gcd? We computed earlier = 3. So >1 => false. matches.\n\n- (187388,144729): n even? 187388 even. n%4? 4*46847=187388, remainder 0 => n%4=0. Compute 2k = 289458? Actually 2*144729 = 289458. Compute gcd(187388,289458) = gcd(187388,289458-187388=102070). Then gcd(187388,102070). 187388-102070=852? Actually 187388-102070=852? Let's compute: 187388 - 102070 = 85,318. Then gcd(102070,85,318). 102070-85,318=16,752. gcd(85,318,16,752). 85,318 mod 16,752: 16,752*5=83,760, remainder 1,558. gcd(16,752,1,558). 16,752 mod 1,558: 1,558*10=15,580, remainder 1,172. 1,558 mod 1,172 = 386. 1,172 mod 386 = 0? Actually 386*3=1158, remainder 14. 386 mod 14 = 8? Actually 14*27=378, remainder 8. 14 mod 8=6. 8 mod 6=2. 6 mod 2=0 => gcd=2. d=2, n%4=0 => false predicted. matches false.\n\n- (167199,10041): n odd (since 167199 odd). gcd=3 => false.\n\n- (179879,103564): n odd? 179879 odd. gcd=1 => true. matches.\n\n- (197276,31462): n even, n%4? 197276%4=0 (since 4*49319=197276). Compute 2k=62924. gcd(197276,62924) =? Let's compute: 197276 mod 62924 = 197276 - 62924*3 = 197276 - 188772 = 8,504. gcd(62924,8504). 62924 mod 8504 = 62924 - 8504*7 = 62924 - 59528 = 3,396. 8504 mod 3396 = 8504 - 3396*2 = 8504 - 6792 = 1,712. 3396 mod 1712 = 3396 - 1712*1 = 1,684? Wait 1712*1=1712, remainder = 1684. 1712 mod 1684 = 28. 1684 mod 28 = 1684 - 28*60=1684 - 1680 = 4. 28 mod 4 = 0 => gcd=4? Actually final gcd=4. So d=4 >2 => false. matches false.\n\n- (176462,90857): n even, n%4? 176462%4=2? Let's compute: 176462/2=88231, it's even? Actually 176462 mod 4: 4*44115 = 176460, remainder 2 => n%4=2. Compute 2k=181714. mod n: 181714-176462=5,252. So 2k mod n = 5252. Compute gcd(176462,5252). 176462 mod 5252 =? 5252*33=173,316, remainder 3,146. Then gcd(5252,3146). 5252 mod 3146 = 2106? Actually 3146*1=3146 remainder 2106. 3146 mod 2106 = 1040. 2106 mod 1040 = 26? Actually 1040*2=2080 remainder 26. 1040 mod 26 = 0 => gcd=26. d=26 >2 => false. matches false.\n\n- (182506,13410): n even, n%4? 182506%4=2 (since 4*45626=182504 remainder2). Compute 2k=26820. gcd(182506,26820) =? Compute 182506 mod 26820: 26820*6=160920, remainder 21586. 26820 mod 21586 = 5234. 21586 mod 5234 = 21586 - 5234*4 = 21586 - 20936 = 650. 5234 mod 650 = 5234 - 650*8 = 5234 - 5200 = 34. 650 mod 34 = 650 - 34*19 = 650 - 646 = 4. 34 mod 4 = 2. 4 mod 2 = 0 => gcd=2. d=2, n%4=2 => true. matches true.\n\n- (151355,71579): n odd (151355 odd), gcd=1 => true.\n\n- (198493,8985): n odd? 198493 odd (ends with 3). gcd(198493,8985) = 1? earlier we computed gcd=1. So true.\n\n- (166640,152569): n even, n%4? 166640%4=0. Compute 2k = 305138? Actually 2*152569=305138. mod n: 305138 - 166640 = 138498. 138498 - 166640? Actually that's bigger than n? Wait we need to reduce mod 166640: 305138 - 166640 = 138498. That's less than 166640, so remainder = 138498. Compute gcd(166640,138498) =? 166640 mod 138498 = 28142. 138498 mod 28142 = 138498 - 28142*4 = 138498 -112568 = 25930? Wait 28142*4=112568, remainder = 25930. 28142 mod 25930 = 2212. 25930 mod 2212 = 25930 - 2212*11=25930-24332=1598. 2212 mod 1598 = 614. 1598 mod 614 = 1598 - 614*2=1598-1228=370. 614 mod 370 = 244? Actually 370*1=370 remainder 244. 370 mod 244 = 126. 244 mod 126 = 118? Actually 126*1=126 remainder 118. 126 mod 118 = 8. 118 mod 8 = 6? Actually 8*14=112 remainder 6. 8 mod 6 = 2. 6 mod 2 = 0 => gcd=2. d=2, n%4=0 => false. matches false.\n\n- (2,1) true (n%4=2, d=2). matches.\n\n- (3,1) odd case, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (3,2) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (4,2) even, n%4=0, d=4 >2 false.\n\n- (5,2) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (6,1) even n%4=2, d=2 true.\n\n- (6,2) even n%4=2, d=2 true.\n\n- (6,3) even n%4=2, d=6>2 false.\n\n- (200000,100000) n%4=0 (since 200000%4=0), d=200000>2 false.\n\n- (200000,1) n%4=0, d=2 false.\n\nThus the rule appears consistent with all test cases.\n\nNow we must also consider n odd case: answer true iff gcd(n,k) == 1 (since gcd(n,2k) = gcd(n,k) when n odd). Let's verify for all odd n testcases:\n\n- (21,4) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (21,15) odd, gcd=3 false.\n\n- (21,20) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (21,17) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (15,14) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (15,7) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (15,5) odd, gcd=5 false.\n\n- (15,3) odd, gcd=3 false.\n\n- (15,3) already false.\n\n- (13,5) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (13,5) duplicate.\n\n- (23,15) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (23,5) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (19,18) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (19,6) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (19,2?) not given.\n\n- (27,24) odd, gcd=3 false.\n\n- (27,6) odd, gcd=3 false.\n\n- (27,6) false.\n\n- (27,24) false.\n\n- (25,22) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (23,15) true.\n\n- (23,5) true.\n\n- (17,3) true.\n\n- (19,18) true.\n\n- (21,4) true.\n\n- (21,17) true.\n\n- (21,20) true.\n\n- (21,15) false.\n\n- (21,??) others.\n\n- (9,3) odd, gcd=3 false.\n\n- (9,1) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (7,6) odd, gcd=1 => true, but there is contradictory false. Likely true.\n\n- (7,1) odd, gcd=1 true.\n\n- (7,6) appears again false. Might be mistake.\n\n- (7,6) also appears earlier as True. We'll assume true is correct.\n\n- (3,2) true.\n\n- (3,1) true.\n\nThus rule holds.\n\nNow check odd n with gcd>1 but maybe still true? Let's see if any odd n with gcd>1 but answer true. None in test list.\n\nNow check odd n with gcd=1 but answer false? None.\n\nThus rule for odd n is indeed gcd(n,k) == 1.\n\nNow for even n, rule: let d = gcd(n, 2k). If d == 1: success. If d == 2: success iff n % 4 == 2 (i.e., n/2 odd). Else: false.\n\nBut wait, what about n even, d == 1? That implies gcd(n,2k)=1 => n odd? Actually if n is even, then 2 divides n, so gcd(n,2k) is at least 2 because 2 divides both n and 2k. So d cannot be 1 for even n. Indeed, for n even, gcd(n,2k) >= 2 always. So d==1 never occurs for even n. So only possible d values are >=2.\n\nThus for even n, success condition reduces to:\n\n- d = gcd(n, 2k) == 2 and n % 4 == 2.\n\nAlternatively, equivalently: gcd(n, k) = 1? Let's see. For even n, if d=2, then gcd(n,k) = 1? Not necessarily: Example (6,2) n=6, k=2, gcd(6,2)=2 >1. But d = gcd(6, 2*2=4) = 2. So gcd(n,k) can be >1. So condition is not gcd(n,k)=1.\n\nThus condition: n even, let g = gcd(n, 2k). If g == 2 and n/2 is odd (i.e., n % 4 == 2), then True; else False.\n\nBut we must also consider case where n even and k = n/2 (Bob opposite). In that case, 2k ≡ 0 mod n, so g = n. Then false (unless n=2 maybe). Indeed (2,1) is n=2, k=1 = n/2, g = 2 (since 2k = 2 mod 2 = 0, gcd(2,0)=2). n%4=2, g=2 => condition says true. Indeed (2,1) true. So rule works for n=2.\n\nThus final rule:\n\nif n % 2 == 1: return gcd(n, k) == 1.\n\nelse (n even):\n    g = math.gcd(n, 2*k)\n    return g == 2 and (n // 2) % 2 == 1\n\nSimplify (n // 2) % 2 == 1 is same as n % 4 == 2.\n\nThus return (math.gcd(n, 2*k) == 2 and n % 4 == 2).\n\nNow we need to verify for all testcases including even n with gcd(n,2k) >2 and n%4==2 maybe? Example (6,3): n=6, 2k=6, gcd=6 >2 => false. Good.\n\n(12,7): n=12, 2k=2, gcd=2, n%4=0 => false. Good.\n\n(20,4): n=20, 2k=8, gcd=4 >2 => false. Good.\n\n(24,5): n=24, 2k=10, gcd=2, n%4=0 => false. Good.\n\n(24,3): n=24, 2k=6, gcd=6 >2 => false. Good.\n\n(4,2): n=4, 2k=4, gcd=4 >2 => false. Good.\n\n(200000,1): n%4=0, gcd=2 => false. Good.\n\n(200000,100000): n%4=0, gcd=200000 >2 => false.\n\n(26,2): n%4=2, 2k=4, gcd=2 => true.\n\n(14,1): n%4=2, gcd=2 => true.\n\n(6,2): n%4=2, gcd=2 => true.\n\n(6,1): n%4=2, gcd=2 => true.\n\n(2,1): n%4=2, gcd=2 => true.\n\n(10,1): not in tests but would be true.\n\nNow check (8,1) predicted false. Not in tests.\n\nNow check (8,3) predicted false.\n\nCheck (8,2) false.\n\nCheck (8,5) maybe? 2k=10 mod8=2, gcd=2, n%4=0 => false. So false.\n\nNow check (8,3) 2k=6, gcd(8,6)=2, n%4=0 => false.\n\nThus pattern holds.\n\nNow check odd n case with k = n/2 (when n odd, n/2 not integer). Not needed.\n\nNow verify that no odd n case with gcd(n,k) = 1 but n%4==2 and d==2? That doesn't apply.\n\nThus final implementation: compute gcd(n,k) for odd n, else compute gcd(n, 2*k) and check condition.\n\nEdge cases: n=1? Not allowed as per constraints (n>=2). So fine.\n\nNow we need to implement can_color_all_points with given signature.\n\nWe must ensure using Python's math.gcd. Also handle large numbers up to 2*10^5? Actually constraints up to 2*10^5 per problem statement but test includes up to 200k and many large numbers up to 200k? Actually there are some large numbers up to ~200k (200000). In tests there are some numbers like 152289, 187388, etc. So fine.\n\nNow implement:\n\ndef can_color_all_points(n: int, k: int) -> bool:\n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        return math.gcd(n, k) == 1\n    else:\n        return math.gcd(n, 2*k) == 2 and n % 4 == 2\n\nCheck for n=2: n%4=2, gcd(2,2*k) = gcd(2,2) =2, condition true => True.\n\nCheck n=2, k=0? Not allowed because k>=1. So fine.\n\nNow test with all given testcases.\n\nLet's simulate mentally each.\n\nWe'll go through each test case to confirm.\n\nTestcases:\n\n1. (6,2): n even, n%4=2, gcd(6,4)=2 => True.\n\n2. (6,3): n%4=2, gcd(6,6)=6 !=2 => False.\n\n3. (6,1): gcd(6,2)=2, n%4=2 => True.\n\n4. (200000,100000): n even, n%4=0, condition false => False.\n\n5. (15,3): odd, gcd(15,3)=3 !=1 => False.\n\n6. (13,5): odd, gcd=1 => True.\n\n7. (