rther\n                        break\n            cost[i][j] = best\n\n    # ----- DP: dp[pos][parts] = minimal cost for prefix s[:pos] using `parts` substrings -----\n    dp = [[INF] * (k + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    dp[0][0] = 0\n    for pos in range(n):\n        for parts in range(k):\n            cur = dp[pos][parts]\n            if cur == INF:\n                continue\n            # remaining substrings after we add one more\n            remaining = k - (parts + 1)\n            min_remaining_len = 2 * remaining\n            # next cut must be at least two characters ahead\n            for nxt in range(pos + 2, n + 1):\n                if (n - nxt) < min_remaining_len:\n                    continue\n                new_val = cur + cost[pos][nxt - 1]\n                if new_val < dp[nxt][parts + 1]:\n                    dp[nxt][parts + 1] = new_val\n\n    answer = dp[n][k]\n    return answer if answer != INF else -1\n```", "thinking": "Given that the user asks for a complete implementation of the function that passes the tests. So I need to produce code that will pass the tests.\n\nWe need to solve the problem: given string s length up to 200, partition into k substrings to minimize total changes required to make each substring a semi-palindrome.\n\nWe need to compute for any substring s[i:j] (i inclusive, j exclusive) the minimal number of changes needed to make it a semi-palindrome.\n\nWe need to define semi-palindrome. The definition: length >=2. There exists a positive integer d such that 1 <= d < len, len % d == 0, and taking indices that have the same modulo by d forms a palindrome.\n\nInterpretation: For a substring t of length L >=2, there is some divisor d of L (excluding L itself) such that when we group characters by their index modulo d (i.e., positions p where p % d = r for each r from 0 to d-1), each group forms a palindrome string. That is, for each r, the sequence t[r], t[r+d], t[r+2d], ... t[r+(m-1)d] (where m = L/d) should be a palindrome. Equivalently, t[i] should equal t[L - d - i]?? Wait need to think.\n\nWe can define constraints: For a given d, for any offset r (0 <= r < d), and any index p in 0..(L/d-1), the character at position r + p*d should equal the character at position r + (L/d - 1 - p)*d. That is, within each modulo class, the sequence is symmetric.\n\nThus the condition for t to be a semi-palindrome is existence of d dividing L, d < L, such that for all i, t[i] == t[L-1 - (i % d) - d * floor(i / d)? Not exactly. Let's formalize.\n\nLet L be length, d a divisor (proper divisor). Let m = L / d. For each offset r in [0, d-1], we have a subsequence of length m: positions r, r+d, r+2d, ..., r+(m-1)d. This subsequence must be a palindrome: for each p in [0, m-1], character at position r + p*d must equal character at position r + (m-1-p)*d.\n\nHence condition: For any i, let r = i % d, p = i // d. Then the counterpart is r + (m-1-p)*d = (m-1-p)*d + r. So i's counterpart j = (m-1-p)*d + r = (m-1 - i//d)*d + (i % d). So condition: t[i] == t[j] for all i.\n\nThus each pair (i, j) must match.\n\nIf d is a divisor, then this condition yields constraints across the string.\n\nNow, we need to compute minimal changes to transform t into a semi-palindrome: we can choose any d that works. For each d, we can compute the minimal changes needed to satisfy the palindrome constraints across each modulo class. Since each modulo class is independent, the minimal changes for that class is: For each pair of positions (i, j) in the same class where i < j, we need to make them equal. The optimal way is to change to the majority character among the positions in that pair (or possibly more than two if m is odd, the middle position doesn't need a pair). Actually within each modulo class, the subsequence of length m must be a palindrome. So we can treat each palindrome requirement across positions. For each class, we have positions at indices r + p*d for p = 0..m-1. For each p, there is counterpart at p' = m-1-p. So pairs (p, p') where p < p' need to have same char. If m is odd, the middle position p = (m-1)/2 is self-matching, no constraint.\n\nThus the minimal changes for that class is sum over pairs of minimal changes to make them equal: for each pair (i, j), we need to change either i or j (or both) to a common char. The minimal changes is 1 if chars differ, 0 if same (since we can change one to the other). But we could also change both to a third char but that would be 2 changes, not optimal. So minimal changes per pair is 0 if same else 1.\n\nThus total changes for that d is number of mismatched pairs across all classes.\n\nBut is there any more flexible option? Could we change both characters to something else that reduces mismatches elsewhere? The pairs are independent; each position appears in exactly one pair (except possible middle ones). So you just need to make each pair equal; you can choose any character (maybe the majority across the pair) but each pair has only two positions (or maybe more if you consider that each class could have more than 2 positions if m>2). Actually each class of length m > 2 yields multiple pairs: (p0,p_{m-1}), (p1,p_{m-2}), etc. For each pair, you have two characters. So indeed each pair is independent.\n\nThus for given d, minimal changes = number of pairs (i,j) such that s[i]!=s[j], across all classes. Equivalent to compute for each pair of positions i and j that are counterpart under modulo d and i<j count mismatches.\n\nWe can compute quickly for substring t.\n\nNow the overall problem: Partition s into k substrings to minimize sum of minimal changes. Classic DP: dp[i][p] = minimal changes for first i characters (prefix length i) partitioned into p substrings. dp[0][0] = 0. For i>0, dp[i][p] = min_{j < i} dp[j][p-1] + cost[j][i-1], where cost[a][b] is minimal changes for substring s[a:b+1] (inclusive). Note that k <= len(s)/2, but k can be up to 100 for length up to 200. DP O(n^2 * k). n <=200, k <=100, O(200^2 *100 = 4 million) fine.