uence of length m must be a palindrome. So we can treat each palindrome requirement across positions. For each class, we have positions at indices r + p*d for p = 0..m-1. For each p, there is counterpart at p' = m-1-p. So pairs (p, p') where p < p' need to have same char. If m is odd, the middle position p = (m-1)/2 is self-matching, no constraint.\n\nThus the minimal changes for that class is sum over pairs of minimal changes to make them equal: for each pair (i, j), we need to change either i or j (or both) to a common char. The minimal changes is 1 if chars differ, 0 if same (since we can change one to the other). But we could also change both to a third char but that would be 2 changes, not optimal. So minimal changes per pair is 0 if same else 1.\n\nThus total changes for that d is number of mismatched pairs across all classes.\n\nBut is there any more flexible option? Could we change both characters to something else that reduces mismatches elsewhere? The pairs are independent; each position appears in exactly one pair (except possible middle ones). So you just need to make each pair equal; you can choose any character (maybe the majority across the pair) but each pair has only two positions (or maybe more if you consider that each class could have more than 2 positions if m>2). Actually each class of length m > 2 yields multiple pairs: (p0,p_{m-1}), (p1,p_{m-2}), etc. For each pair, you have two characters. So indeed each pair is independent.\n\nThus for given d, minimal changes = number of pairs (i,j) such that s[i]!=s[j], across all classes. Equivalent to compute for each pair of positions i and j that are counterpart under modulo d and i<j count mismatches.\n\nWe can compute quickly for substring t.\n\nNow the overall problem: Partition s into k substrings to minimize sum of minimal changes. Classic DP: dp[i][p] = minimal changes for first i characters (prefix length i) partitioned into p substrings. dp[0][0] = 0. For i>0, dp[i][p] = min_{j < i} dp[j][p-1]