\n\nNow we must output only the code and not extra commentary.\n\nThus final answer will include import statements, constants, global variable, function definition.\n\nNow let's consider any potential issues with type hints: The function signature includes type hint from typing import List, but we can include that.\n\nNow we need to consider that they may also import this function from a module. But it's fine.\n\nNow we need to ensure we have correct indent.\n\nNow final answer: Provide the code.\n\nNow let's think about any potential pitfalls:\n\n- The DP may be off by one because we used FULL_MASK = (1 << 11) - 1, but we should ensure that shift and mask are correct. For v up to 10, shifting mask bits up to 10 yields bits up to 20, but we mask down to 10 bits. So correct.\n\n- For mask << v, if mask has bits up to 10 and v>0, shift may produce bits beyond 10; we mask them out.\n\n- For v >10 (stay), we treat as unchanged.\n\n- For v values where (mask << v) & FULL_MASK yields 0 (no new bits), new_mask = mask | 0 = mask. So contributions from those v are also adding prob * inv_a to dp2[mask], same as stay. That's okay.\n\nNow think about potential overflow of list indexing: new_mask may be up to 2047, okay.\n\nNow consider using Python's built-in pow for modular inverse. That's fine.\n\nNow think about global variable naming: _call_counts defined at module level.\n\nNow ensure that we reference global variable inside function.\n\nNow we need to ensure that we don't accidentally modify dp across calls. We define dp inside function, so it's fresh each call.\n\nNow implement final code.\n\nNow we need to test the function manually on some test cases.\n\nGiven the constraints, we can test a few:\n\nTest (4, [1,2,3,4]):\n\nWe'll try to compute DP manually using code, but trust.\n\nTest (4, [1,7,2,9]):\n\nWe trust DP.\n\nNow test (100, [1000000]*100). That's large DP with many dice. It should compute in time.\n\nNow test (100, [441826,...]) large. DP should compute.\n\nN