306. Good.\n\n- n=1, a1=1000000. Probability that die value is exactly 10, since that's only way to get subset sum 10 (including die). So probability = 1/1000000. Compute inv(1000000) mod MOD. Need to compute. They expect 616898040. Let's check: inv(10^6) mod 998244353 = 10^6^{-1} mod p. Since p is prime, inv(10^6) = pow(10^6, p-2, p). Should be 616898040. We'll trust.\n\n- n=10, dice faces all 1: each die always shows value 1. So the vector is always all ones. Subset sum can be any integer from 0 to 10 (by picking any number of dice). So sum 10 is reachable (by picking all 10 dice). So probability = 1 (since only one possible assignment). So numerator = denominator = 1, probability = 1 => output 1. Indeed test expects 1.\n\n- n=100, dice faces all 1: always values 1. Subset sum 10 reachable if there are at least 10 dice; we have 100 dice, so always reachable. So probability =1. So output 1.\n\n- n=9, dice faces all 1: only 9 dice, each value 1. Subset sum max =9, cannot reach 10. So probability=0. They test that.\n\n- n=4, faces [1,2,3,4] (small). We can compute DP.\n\nThus DP approach seems correct.\n\nNow compute DP efficiently. Represent masks as integer bitmask of length 11 (0..10). Initially mask = 1 << 0 = 1. For each die, we compute new DP array of length 2048 zeroed. For each mask with DP value >0, we need to compute transitions for v in [1..small] and for identity case (big_count). For each v, compute new_mask = mask | ((mask << v) & ((1 << 11)-1)). Explanation: the reachable sums after including v: for each sum s in mask (bit s set), we can add v to get sum s+v (if <=10). So we shift mask left by v bits, mask << v, then mask OR with that (plus original mask). Actually the reachable set after processing new die: you can either not include this die (keep same reachable sums), or include it (add its value). So new_mask = mask | (mask << v). But we need to cap bits beyond 10 (i.e., mask << v may have bits beyond position 10). So we mask with (1<<11)-1.\n\nNow