on and exclusion of each die in DP. Our DP transition already includes both possibilities: we consider not including (by mask unchanged) for each v? Actually we treat each value v, and transition new_mask = mask | (mask << v). That includes both not including (mask) and including (mask << v). So the identity case is automatically included (since v >10 yields mask unchanged). However, we also need to consider the possibility of not including a die when v <=10: but that is already accounted because new_mask = mask | (mask << v) includes mask (original reachable sums). So for each v, the DP transition includes both possibilities. However, for values >10, new_mask = mask | (mask << v) = mask (since shift bits are out of range). So it's also correct.\n\nThus we don't need a separate \"big identity\" case for not including, because the transition already includes not including. But we need to account for the number of assignments for each value v. For each v, we add DP[i-1][mask] to DP[i][new_mask]. So for v >10, new_mask = mask, and we would add DP[i-1][mask] for each such v. This is equivalent to DP[i-1][mask] * big. So the identity case is accounted as we are doing.\n\nThus DP described is correct.\n\nNow we need to test on sample cases. Let's manually compute for some.\n\nCase: n=4, dice_faces=[1,7,2,9] expected output 942786334.\n\nWe can test after implementation.\n\nPotential pitfalls: Must ensure dp initialization correct: dp[1] = 1 means mask with only sum 0 reachable. But mask 1 << 0 = 1, correct. But dp array index is mask integer (0..2047). So dp[1] = 1.\n\nNow, after processing dice, we sum dp[mask] where bit 10 set. Could also just compute total numerator = sum_{mask} dp[mask] if (mask >> 10) & 1 else 0.\n\nCheck for n=0? Not needed.\n\nNow compute denominator = product of a_i % MOD.\n\nNow final answer = numerator * inv(denominator) % MOD.\n\nPotential overflow: Python ints fine.\n\nNow verify for test where candidate(1,[10]) expected 299473306. DP will com