..10: For each v, new_mask = old_mask | (old_mask << v). Since old_mask=1 (bits: 0). Shift yields 1 << v (bit v). So new_mask = (1 | (1<<v)). So after processing each v, dp2[mask] accumulates dp[1] * inv_f.\n\nBut we have multiple v leading to distinct masks: mask = 1 + (1<<v). For each v, dp2[mask] = inv_f.\n\nThus after processing die, dp[mask] for each mask (bits {0, v}) is inv_f. So the probability that reachable set includes sum 10 is sum over masks with bit 10 set = dp[mask where v=10] = inv_f = 299473306. That's correct.\n\nNow case: n=1, f=9. max_val=9. No v=10. So no mask with bit 10 set. Answer = 0.\n\nCase: n=1, f=1000000. max_val=10. For v=1..10 each probability inv_f. So probability that sum 10 reachable is probability that die rolls 10 = inv_f = 1/1000000 mod M. Let's compute inv_f for 1000000 mod M. Let's compute: pow(1000000, M-2, M) gives some value; expected answer 616898040. Check: 1000000 * 616898040 mod M =? Let's compute: 616898040 * 1000000 = 616898040000000? mod M? Not sure. But test expects 616898040. So DP will produce that.\n\nNow case: n=4, dice faces [1, 2, 3, 4].\n\nWe can compute DP manually maybe.\n\nBut DP approach should compute correct answer.\n\nNow consider case: all dice faces = 1 (i.e., each die always shows value 1). Then each die always contributes 1 if included. So reachable sums after N dice are exactly {0,1,...,N}. So if N>=10, sum 10 is reachable with probability 1 (since deterministic). Indeed test expects candidate(10, [1]*10) == 1, candidate(100, [1]*100) == 1.\n\nOur DP with f_i=1: inv_f = 1 (since 1's inverse is 1). max_val = 1. p_big = 0. For each die, transition for v=1: new_mask = old_mask | (old_mask << 1). Since we always shift by 1, reachable sums become all sums up to number of dice. After N dice, mask bits 0..N set. So bit 10 set if N>=10 => dp sum = 1 (since dp[mask] = 1 for unique mask). Indeed DP should produce answer 1.\n\nNow case: n=9, all dice faces = 1 (nine dice). Then reachable sums are 0..9, so bit