_mask = (1 << 11) - 1\n    for f in dice_faces:\n        inv_f = pow(f, M - 2, M)\n        max_val = 10 if f >= 10 else f\n        p_big = ((f - max_val) % M) * inv_f % M\n        new_dp = [0] * (1 << 11)\n        # identity transition for p_big\n        if p_big != 0:\n            for mask in range(1 << 11):\n                if dp[mask]:\n                    new_dp[mask] = (new_dp[mask] + dp[mask] * p_big) % M\n        # transitions for each v in 1..max_val\n        for v in range(1, max_val + 1):\n            shift = v\n            for mask in range(1 << 11):\n                if dp[mask]:\n                    new_mask = mask | ((mask << shift) & full_mask)\n                    new_dp[new_mask] = (new_dp[new_mask] + dp[mask] * inv_f) % M\n        dp = new_dp\n\n    ans = 0\n    target_bit = 1 << 10\n    for mask in range(1 << 11):\n        if mask & target_bit:\n            ans = (ans + dp[mask]) % M\n    return ans\n\nBut careful: we need to handle dp[mask] maybe many zeros; we can iterate over masks with non-zero dp to improve speed but not needed.\n\nBut we need to treat p_big as identity transition. However we must also consider that for v values, we also need to include identity (i.e., not including the die) as part of reachable set. But our DP transition for each v already includes old_mask unchanged because we OR with shifted bits. Actually new_mask = old_mask | (old_mask << v). That includes old_mask itself (since OR includes old_mask). So identity transition for each v also includes not including the die. But the identity transition for v>10 is just old_mask unchanged (shift bits truncated). So p_big corresponds to probability of v>10, which yields new_mask = old_mask (since shift bits beyond 10 are discarded). So we handle p_big accordingly.\n\nNow note: we might double count identity contributions: For each v (<=10), we have new_mask = old_mask | (old_mask << v). This includes old_mask unchanged; but we still multiply by probability of that v (1/f). So identity part contributed from each v (since not including die) is accounted for within each v's transition. So it's fine. The p_big identity also adds identity probability.\n\nThus DP sum of probabilities remains 1.\n\nNow verify DP for n=1, f=10:\n\ndp[mask] start: dp[1]=1.\n\nf=10: inv_f = inv10. max_val=10, p_big = (10-10)*inv_f = 0.\n\nLoop v=1..10:\n\nFor v=1: new_mask = 1 | (1 << 1) = 1 | 2 = 3. new_dp[3] += 1*inv_f.\n\nv=2: new_mask = 1 | (1 << 2) = 5. new_dp[5] += inv_f.\n\n...\n\nv=10: new_mask = 1 | (1 << 10) = (1 + 1024) = 1025. new_dp[1025] += inv_f.\n\nThus new_dp[1025] = inv_f, answer = sum masks with bit 10 set = new_dp[1025] = inv_f. Good.\n\nNow test for n=10, dice faces all 1:\n\nEach die f=1: inv_f = 1, max_val=1, p_big=0.\n\ndp start mask=1 (bit0). For each die, transition v=1: new_mask = mask | (mask << 1). This DP is deterministic: after k dice, dp[mask] is 1 for mask = (1 << (k+1))-1 (bits 0..k). So after 10 dice, mask bits 0..10 set, probability 1. Answer = sum dp[mask] with bit10 set = dp[mask] = 1.\n\nNow for n=9, all 1: after 9 dice, mask bits 0..9 set, no bit10, answer=0.\n\nThus DP works.\n\nNow we must implement global _call_counter.\n\nPotential concurrency issues not relevant.\n\nNow test for (1, [1000000]) expected 616898040. Let's compute DP: inv_f = pow(1000000, M-2, M). p_big = (1000000-10)*inv_f mod M = 999990 * inv_f mod M. But we need only probability that sum 10 reachable. Only value 10 yields reachable sum 10. So answer = inv_f. Indeed DP will produce inv_f. So answer matches.\n\nNow test for (100, [1000000] * 100) expected 868119802. We must compute DP for many dice with f=1e6 each. Since each die has small probability 1/1e6 of being <=10 values; we need DP for many dice, but DP state size 2048. So should compute within time.\n\nNow test for (4, [1,7,2,9]) expected 942786334. We trust DP.\n\nNow test for (7, [1,10,100,...]) expected 996117877. DP should compute.\n\nNow large test case with many random faces expects 385328118