Could prune.\n\nAlternatively, we can generate a Latin square of order n using a simple method: For each row i, column j: assign symbol (i + j) mod n (if we had n symbols). But we have only 3 symbols, not n. But we can assign the symbols in a cyclic pattern for n=3 (complete Latin square). For n>3, we need to have blanks. Maybe we can assign each row i: place A in column (i), B in column (i+1 mod n), C in column (i+2 mod n). That ensures each column gets each symbol exactly once if n=3. For n>3, this assignment will produce each column having each symbol exactly once? Let's test n=4: row0: A at col0, B at col1, C at col2; row1: A at col1, B at col2, C at col3; row2: A at col2, B at col3, C at col0; row3: A at col3, B at col0, C at col1. Let's count per column: col0: A from row0, C from row2, B from row3 -> A, B, C each appear exactly once. Indeed each column gets each symbol exactly once for any n, as long as we assign A at col i, B at col i+1, C at col i+2 (mod n). Because for each column j, which rows have A in column j? row i where i=j mod n => exactly one row. B appears where i+1=j => i=j-1, exactly one row. C appears where i+2=j => i=j-2, exactly one row. So each column gets exactly one of each symbol. Great! This works for any n >= 3.\n\nThus we have a deterministic construction for placements. Now we must respect leftmost non-'.' char in each row must equal R[i]; topmost non-'.' char in each column must equal C[j].\n\nGiven our deterministic placement, each row i has letters at columns i (A), i+1 (B), i+2 (C). Sorted by column index, leftmost is at column i (A). So leftmost char in each row is always A. That would mean R must be all 'A's. But test cases include R not all As, e.g., R='ABC' for n=3, R='AABAC', etc. So we need to be able to shift which symbol appears leftmost in each row.\n\nWe can permute the mapping of symbols to positions within each row. For each row, we need to assign a permutation of columns for A,B,C such that each column gets each symbol