invB[pB[i]] = i\n                    invC[pC[i]] = i\n\n                for j in range(n):\n                    rowA, rowB, rowC = invA[j], invB[j], invC[j]\n                    # smallest row in this column\n                    min_row = rowA\n                    sym = 'A'\n                    if rowB < min_row:\n                        min_row = rowB\n                        sym = 'B'\n                    if rowC < min_row:\n                        min_row = rowC\n                        sym = 'C'\n                    if sym != c[j]:\n                        ok = False\n                        break\n                if not ok:\n                    continue\n\n                # ------------------------------------------------------------\n                # All constraints satisfied – build the grid.\n                grid = [['.' for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]\n                for i in range(n):\n                    grid[i][pA[i]] = 'A'\n                    grid[i][pB[i]] = 'B'\n                    grid[i][pC[i]] = 'C'\n                return [''.join(row) for row in grid]\n\n    # No placement works.\n    return None\n```\n\nThe implementation follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above\nand passes the supplied test suite.", "thinking": "Thus, to pass the tests, I need to implement solve_grid in a way that passes all the test cases provided. The constraints are small (n from 3 to 5). I can brute-force search for a solution: fill an n×n grid with characters A, B, C and '.' such that each row and column contains exactly one A, one B, one C and other cells are '.' (i.e., n - 3 cells per row/col are '.'). Additionally, the leftmost non-'.' character in row i must be exactly r[i]; the topmost non-'.' character in column i must be exactly c[i].\n\nWe need to consider that \"leftmost character written in the i-th row matches the i-th character of R\" means that the first non-'.' in each row (i.e., smallest column index j where grid[i][j] != '.') must be r[i]