is row 0, column i. So C[i] = grid[0][i].\n\nThus constraints: grid[i][0] = R[i]; grid[0][j] = C[j].\n\nThis is different from leftmost non-empty. Let's examine the example: solve_grid(3, 'ABC', 'ABC') returns ['ABC','BCA','CAB']. Indeed grid[0][0] = 'A' = R[0]; grid[1][0] = 'B' = R[1]; grid[2][0] = 'C' = R[2]; also grid[0][0] = 'A' = C[0]; grid[0][1] = 'B' = C[1]; grid[0][2] = 'C' = C[2]. So indeed they match first row and first column.\n\nThus constraints are that the first column equals R, and the first row equals C. Not leftmost non-empty.\n\nThus problem reduces to constructing an n x n grid with exactly one A, B, C per row and per column, with first column predetermined as R and first row predetermined as C.\n\nEmpty cells '.' allowed elsewhere.\n\nThus we need to fill the remaining (n-1)*(n-1) cells with letters '.' or letters such that each row and column contains exactly one A, one B, one C. The predetermined cells must also satisfy these constraints: each row i already has one letter at column0 (R[i]), each column j already has one letter at row0 (C[j]). However note that the cell (0,0) is both row0 col0; it must match both R[0] and C[0]; thus we must have R[0] == C[0] else impossible. In example both are 'A'.\n\nThus constraints: For all i, R[i] must be one of 'A','B','C'; similarly for C[j]. The grid must be such that each row contains exactly one each of 'A','B','C', and each column contains exactly one each. Since each row has predetermined leftmost column value, we need to fill each row's remaining columns to have the other two letters (the missing ones). Similarly each column has predetermined topmost row value, we need to fill other rows to have the other two letters.\n\nThus this is a Latin square of order n with 3 symbols, but only 3 symbols used per row/col, leaving other cells empty. Actually each row must contain exactly one of each of three symbols, no more. So each row has exactly 3 non-'.' cells: those are the positions of A, B, C. Since column0 is fixed as R[i] which may be any of the three symbols, each row still needs the other two symbols placed somewhere among columns 1..n-1. Similarly each column j already has C[j] in row0, so each column needs the other two symbols placed somewhere among rows 1..n-1.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: given a set of rows and columns each with a pre-assigned symbol at a fixed position (0, i) for rows and (0, j) for columns (including overlapping (0,0)), fill the rest with placements of the remaining symbols such that each row has exactly one A, B, C, each column has exactly one A, B, C.\n\nThus we need to assign positions for each symbol in each row beyond column0, and each column beyond row0, ensuring consistency.\n\nThis is similar to constructing a Latin square with predetermined first row and first column.\n\nGiven n is 3 to 5, we can brute force search for placements.\n\nWe can treat each symbol separately: For each symbol X (A, B, C), we need a permutation mapping rows to columns where X appears. For symbol X, we have predetermined placements: for each row i, if R[i] == X, then the column for X in row i is fixed as col=0. For each column j, if C[j] == X, then the row for X in column j is fixed as row=0. For the overlapping cell (0,0), if both constraints apply, they must be consistent (i.e., R[0]==C[0]==X). For other rows and columns, we need to fill exactly one X per row and per column.\n\nThus we need to find permutations pi_X for each symbol X that satisfy:\n\n- For each row i, pi_X[i] is column where X appears.\n- For each column j, inv_pi_X[j] is row where X appears.\n\nGiven predetermined placements for some rows/columns: for row i where R[i]==X, we must have pi_X[i] = 0. For column j where C[j]==X, we must have inv_pi_X[j] = 0, i.e., pi_X[0] = j. If both apply to same row i=0 and column j=0, then both constraints require pi_X[0] = 0 and pi_X[0] = j, so j must be 0 and X must match both R[0] and C[0].\n\nWe need to find permutations that satisfy these constraints.\n\nAlso we must ensure that for each row i, the three columns pi_A[i], pi_B[i], pi_C[i] are distinct (since each cell can hold only one symbol). Similarly each column j must have distinct rows for each symbol (but that's automatically satisfied if pi_X are permutations, but we must ensure that for each column j, the rows assigned for A, B, C are distinct; but since each column gets exactly one of each symbol, they will be distinct because each symbol appears only once per column, but could they be same row? Wait, column j could have A at row i, B at row i (same row), but that would be two symbols in same cell (i,j). But that can't happen because each symbol's placement is a separate cell; we need to ensure that for each cell (i,j), at most one symbol is placed. So we need to ensure that for any pair of symbols X, Y, we don't have pi_X[i] == pi_Y[i] (same column for same row) and also for any column j, we don't have inv_pi_X[j] == inv_pi_Y[j] (same row for same column). Actually the condition pi_X[i] == pi_Y[i] ensures that row i would have two symbols in same column, which is invalid. Similarly inv_pi_X[j] == inv_pi_Y[j] ensures column j would have two symbols in same row, which is same as above. So it's enough to enforce that for each row i, pi_A[i], pi_B[i], pi_C[i] are all distinct.\n\nThus overall constraints: permutations pi_A, pi_B, pi_C, each of size n, with constraints:\n\n- For each i, pi_A[i], pi_B[i], pi_C[i] distinct.\n- For each i, if R[i] == 'A', then pi_A[i] == 0; similarly for 'B' and 'C'.\n- For each j, if C[j] == 'A', then pi_A[0] == j; similarly for B, C.\n- Additional consistency: If R[0] != C[0], then impossible.\n\nThat's it.\n\nThus we need to find three permutations satisfying these constraints.\n\nGiven n <=5, we can brute force: generate all permutations for each symbol, filter by constraints.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n```\nfrom itertools import permutations\n\ndef solve_grid(n, r, c):\n    # Validate that r[0] == c[0] else no solution\n    if r[0] != c[0]:\n        return None\n\n    # Generate all permutations of range(n)\n    cols = list(range(n))\n    perms = list(permutations(cols))\n\n    # Pre-filter permutations by row constraints: For each perm in perms, for each i,\n    # if r[i] == X, then perm[i] must be 0.\n    # Pre-filter column constraints: for each perm, if c[j] == X, then perm[0] must be j.\n\n    # Actually for each symbol X, we need to pre-filter perms for that symbol.\n```\n\nWe'll create a dictionary mapping symbol to list of candidate perms.\n\n```\nsymbol_to_perms = {}\nfor X in 'ABC':\n    cand = []\n    for perm in perms:\n        ok = True\n        # row constraints\n        for i,ch in enumerate(r):\n            if ch == X:\n                if perm[i] != 0:\n                    ok = False\n                    break\n        # column constraints\n        for j,ch in enumerate(c):\n            if ch == X:\n                if perm[0] != j:\n                    ok = False\n                    break\n        # also ensure that perm[0] equals something consistent for both row0 and col0 constraints\n        # Actually row0 constraint also includes r[0]==X -> perm[0]==0, which is same as column0 constraint if c[0]==X.\n        # We'll just check both.\n        if ok:\n            cand.append(perm)\n    symbol_to_perms[X] = cand\n```\n\nNow we need to choose a triple (permA, permB, permC) from respective lists such that for each row i, the three perm values are distinct.\n\nWe can iterate nested loops. The lists may be small.\n\nFor each candidate triple, check distinctness per row.\n\nIf found, construct grid:\n\nInitialize grid with '.'.\n\nSet for each row i:\ngrid[i][permA[i]] = 'A'\ngrid[i][permB[i]] = 'B'\ngrid[i][permC[i]] = 'C'\n\nBut need to check that we didn't assign a cell twice (distinctness ensures not). Also ensure that the predetermined cells match: grid[i][0] = r[i]; grid[0][j] = c[j]; but our assignments will already set those because of constraints. However we should double-check.\n\nAfter constructing, we can return as list of strings.\n\nIf none found, return None.\n\nTest for candidate(3, 'AAA', 'BBB'): r = AAA, c = BBB. Let's see if our algorithm can find a solution.\n\nFirst, check r[0] = 'A', c[0] = 'B', they differ -> algorithm returns None. But test expects not None. So maybe the constraint is not that the top-left cell must match both; maybe they allow inconsistency? Let's examine the problem statement again: \"The leftmost character written in the i-th row matches the i-th character of R. The topmost character written in the i-th column matches the i-th character of C.\" For i=0, leftmost character in row0 is at column0 (the leftmost column). The topmost character in column0 is at row0 (the topmost row). So indeed both refer to the same cell (0,0). So they must match. But test case suggests they may not enforce that? Let's check test: they have candidate(3, 'AAA', 'BBB') is not None. So r[0]='A', c[0]='B', they differ. So either the problem statement is interpreted differently (maybe leftmost means column 0, topmost means row 0, but they might consider those constraints separate for each row/column but they don't conflict because they refer to different cells? Actually they refer to different cells: \"leftmost character written in the i-th row\" is the leftmost character in row i, which is at column index of the leftmost non '.' cell, not necessarily column0. And \"topmost character written in the i-th column\" is the topmost non '.' cell in column i, which could be at row index >0. So they could be separate cells. That would allow r[0] and c[0] to be different because the leftmost character in row0 might be at column some index >0, and topmost character in column0 might be at row some index >0. So they aren't necessarily the same cell. So our earlier interpretation of \"first column = R\" and \"first row = C\" is too restrictive. The actual constraints are about leftmost non-empty cell per row, and topmost non-empty per column. That's what we originally thought. However we realized that 'AAA', 'BBB' case seemed impossible under that interpretation. But test expects solution exists. Let's re-evaluate.\n\nWe need to analyze thoroughly. We have constraints:\n\n- Each row has exactly one A, B, C placed somewhere in the row, rest '.'.\n- In each row i, among the columns where we placed letters (i.e., the three positions of A, B, C), the leftmost (minimum column index) of those letters must be r[i].\n- In each column j, among the rows where we placed letters (i.e., the three positions of A, B, C in that column), the topmost (minimum row index) must be c[j].\n\nGiven that each row has exactly three letters placed (A, B, C) at three distinct columns. The leftmost among those three must be a specific letter. So each row i has a designated symbol that appears in the smallest column among its three positions. That symbol is r[i].\n\nSimilarly, each column j has a designated symbol that appears in the smallest row among its three positions. That symbol is c[j].\n\nGiven that there are exactly three letters per row and per column, but there could be empty columns/rows before the leftmost/topmost.\n\nNow, is there a solution for n=3, r='AAA', c='BBB'? Let's try to construct manually.\n\nWe have n=3. Each row must contain A, B, C. Each column must contain A, B, C.\n\nWe need row leftmost letters: each row leftmost is A. So in each row, the column of A must be less than columns of B and C. So for each row i, a_i < b_i and a_i < c_i.\n\nWe need column topmost letters: each column topmost is B. So for each column j, the row of B must be less than rows of A and C. So for each column j, b_row[j] < a_row[j] and b_row[j] < c_row[j].\n\nNow we have to find permutations a_i, b_i, c_i (columns where each appears) and their inverses.\n\nGiven n=3, columns are 0,1,2. Let's attempt to assign a_i for each row.\n\nWe need a_i distinct across rows (since each column gets exactly one A). So a_i is a permutation of {0,1,2}.\n\nSimilarly b_i is a permutation of {0,1,2}.\n\nc_i is a permutation of {0,1,2}.\n\nConstraints:\n\n- For each row i: a_i < b_i and a_i < c_i.\n\nSince each row's a_i is the smallest among the three distinct numbers a_i, b_i, c_i, and we have three distinct numbers (0,1,2), the only way a_i is smallest is a_i = 0 (since it's the only number smaller than others). But a_i can be 0 for each row? No, a_i must be distinct across rows, so only one row can have a_i=0. So no solution? Wait, we could have a_i=0 for one row, a_i=1 for another row, a_i=2 for another row, but then for row where a_i=1, we need b_i and c_i >1, but they must be distinct and among {0,2}? Actually we need b_i and c_i distinct from a_i and each other. If a_i=1, the only numbers >1 are 2. We need two numbers >1 distinct, but we only have one number 2 >1. So impossible. Similarly for a_i=2, there are no numbers >2. So indeed each row's a_i must be 0. But only one row can have column 0 for A. So impossible. Therefore no solution under these constraints.\n\nThus test case candidate(3, 'AAA', 'BBB') being not None suggests they interpret constraints differently. Let's re-express: maybe the leftmost character written in each row is the character at column 0 (the leftmost column), regardless of empty cells. That means each row's column 0 must be r[i]. Similarly, topmost character in each column is the character at row 0 (the topmost row), i.e., grid[0][j] = c[j]. That would be like first column = R and first row = C. Then r[0] and c[0] are independent; they can be different because they refer to different cells: (0,0) would be both grid[0][0] = r[0] (since column 0) and grid[0][0] = c[0] (since row 0). So they must be equal. So the test case still fails because r[0] != c[0]. So they must be allowing grid[0][0] to have both values? That can't be. So perhaps they allow the leftmost character to be placed anywhere in the row (including column0) but they don't require the topmost character to be placed anywhere else, but they can be placed in different cells. That is, the leftmost character in row i can be at any column, but the topmost character in column j can be at any row, not necessarily the same cell as the leftmost of row i. So r[0] and c[0] refer to different cells: the leftmost character in row 0 may be at column something >0; the topmost character in column 0 may be at row something >0. So they don't have to coincide. Therefore r[0] and c[0] can be different. The only requirement is that for each row, the leftmost non-empty cell (i.e., smallest column index among the three letters) contains r[i]; for each column, the topmost non-empty cell (i.e., smallest row index among the three letters) contains c[j].\n\nThus we revert to original interpretation. Then why does 'AAA','BBB' have a solution? Let's try to see if there is any arrangement for n=3 that satisfies leftmost per row = A and topmost per column = B.\n\nWe argued earlier that it's impossible because each row's leftmost must be A, which forces A's column to be the smallest among the three columns for that row. Since there are only three columns, for row with A at column 0, B and C at columns 1 and 2, that's fine. For row with A at column 1, B and C must be at columns >1, i.e., only column 2 is >1, but need two distinct columns >1, impossible. So A cannot be at column >0. So each row's A must be at column 0. Thus A's column assignments are all 0, which violates column constraint (each column must have exactly one A). So indeed no solution.\n\nThus test case seems contradictory. Let's double-check test case: they assert candidate(3, 'AAA', 'BBB') is not None. That could be a mistake in the test. But we must make it pass. So maybe they have a different interpretation where each row and column must contain exactly one of each letter, but they can also have '.' anywhere, and maybe they allow rows/columns to have extra letters? No, the problem states exactly one of each per row/col.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the leftmost character written in the i-th row refers to the leftmost column of the entire grid (column 0) and the topmost character in the i-th column refers to the topmost row (row 0), but they treat them separately, not requiring consistency at (0,0). However, they might treat them as independent constraints, but still the grid must have exactly one of each symbol per row and per column. If r[0] != c[0], then the cell (0,0) would need to be both R[0] and C[0]; conflict. So they'd have to place R[0] at (0,0) to satisfy leftmost for row0, and C[0] at (0,0) to satisfy topmost for col0; cannot both. So impossible. So they must be allowing leftmost character to be at column 0 but not requiring topmost for column 0 to be at row 0, maybe they can be placed at any row above? No, topmost means smallest row index among the placed letters. If we have a letter in column 0 at row 0, that's the topmost. So to have topmost = B in column 0, we need B at row0, column0. Then leftmost in row0 would also be B (since B at column0). So row0 leftmost would be B, not A. So if r[0]=A, c[0]=B, no solution.\n\nThus test case seems contradictory to problem as described.\n\nLet's double-check test code:\n\n```\nassert candidate(3, 'AAA', 'BBB') is not None\n```\n\nYes.\n\nMaybe they allow the leftmost character in row i to be at column i (i.e., the diagonal)? No, they said leftmost character written in the i-th row matches the i-th character of R. Possibly they interpret \"leftmost character written in the i-th row\" as the character in the first column (col 0) irrespective of emptiness. Similarly \"topmost character written in the i-th column\" as the character in the first row (row 0). Then they would be independent constraints for each row and column, but they'd overlap at (0,0). However they might not enforce that r[0] must equal c[0] because they might treat those constraints as separate: leftmost character in row0 = R[0] at grid[0][0]; topmost character in column0 = C[0] at grid[0][0] as well, but they might think you can set grid[0][0] to a value that matches both? But if they are different, then constraint can't be satisfied. So they must have test cases where they match. In test case candidate(3,'AAA','BBB'), r[0]='A', c[0]='B' differ, so they'd be contradictory. So perhaps they didn't consider this and they think there is a solution. Maybe they think you can place the leftmost character in row i not necessarily at column 0 but at some column leftmost in that row, but you can also place topmost character in column i at some row, not necessarily row0, and they can be independent, thus r[0] and c[0] refer to possibly different cells: leftmost for row0 could be at column 1 (if column0 is '.'), topmost for column0 could be at row 1 (if row0 is '.'). So they can be independent. In that case, r[0] and c[0] are not required to be equal. So it's possible.\n\nThus we need to search for solutions where leftmost per row is r[i] but not necessarily at column 0; there may be '.' before that. Similarly topmost per column is c[j] but not necessarily at row 0; there may be '.' above that.\n\nThus we need to find placements of A, B, C in each row and column, respecting that for each row i, the leftmost placed symbol (i.e., the smallest column index among the three symbols placed in that row) is r[i]; and for each column j, the topmost placed symbol (i.e., the smallest row index among the three symbols placed in that column) is c[j].\n\nGiven that each row has exactly three letters placed (A, B, C). The leftmost among them is whichever column has the minimum index. So r[i] must be the symbol placed in the column with minimum index for that row.\n\nThus for each row i, we need to assign positions for A, B, C (distinct columns). Let’s define pi_X[i] as the column of X in row i. Then the leftmost among pi_A[i], pi_B[i], pi_C[i] is min_col = min(pi_A[i], pi_B[i], pi_C[i]). The symbol at that column must be r[i]; i.e., the symbol X such that pi_X[i] == min_col must satisfy X == r[i].\n\nSimilarly for each column j, define sigma_X[j] as the row where X appears in column j (i.e., sigma_X[j] = inv_pi_X[j]). The topmost among sigma_A[j], sigma_B[j], sigma_C[j] is min_row = min(sigma_A[j], sigma_B[j], sigma_C[j]); the symbol at that row must be c[j]; i.e., X such that sigma_X[j] == min_row must satisfy X == c[j].\n\nThus constraints: For each row i, the minimal column among pi_A[i], pi_B[i], pi_C[i] corresponds to symbol r[i]; For each column j, the minimal row among inv_pi_A[j], inv_pi_B[j], inv_pi_C[j] corresponds to symbol c[j].\n\nNow, we need to find permutations pi_A, pi_B, pi_C that satisfy these constraints.\n\nGiven n <=5, we can brute force all permutations for each symbol, checking constraints. That's manageable. For n=5, each symbol has 120 permutations; total combos 1.7 million; check constraints for each; still okay.\n\nWe need to also ensure per row distinctness, which is automatically satisfied if pi_X[i] are distinct across X for each i; we need to check that.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Generate all permutations for each symbol: perms = list(permutations(range(n))).\n- For each perm_A in perms:\n  - For each perm_B in perms:\n    - For each perm_C in perms:\n      - For each i in 0..n-1:\n        - if perm_A[i] == perm_B[i] or perm_A[i] == perm_C[i] or perm_B[i] == perm_C[i]: continue (invalid).\n        - Determine leftmost symbol: min_col = min(perm_A[i], perm_B[i], perm_C[i]).\n          - If min_col == perm_A[i] and r[i] != 'A': invalid.\n          - If min_col == perm_B[i] and r[i] != 'B': invalid.\n          - If min_col == perm_C[i] and r[i] != 'C': invalid.\n      - For each j in 0..n-1:\n        - Determine rows: row_A = inv_perm_A[j] (i.e., index i where perm_A[i]==j). We can compute inverse mapping for each perm.\n        - row_B = inv_perm_B[j], row_C = inv_perm_C[j].\n        - min_row = min(row_A, row_B, row_C).\n          - If min_row == row_A and c[j] != 'A': invalid.\n          - If min_row == row_B and c[j] != 'B': invalid.\n          - If min_row == row_C and c[j] != 'C': invalid.\n      - If all constraints satisfied, construct grid and return.\n\nWe also need to ensure that the leftmost symbol is unique; but given three distinct columns, the minimum column is unique, unless two symbols share the same column (which we disallow). So fine.\n\nSimilarly for topmost.\n\nThus we can implement solve_grid as above.\n\nNow check if candidate(3,'AAA','BBB') yields a solution.\n\nWe need to find permutations for A,B,C that satisfy constraints:\n\n- For each row i: leftmost symbol is A.\n\nThus for each row i, the column of A must be the smallest among the three columns. That means for each i, perm_A[i] < perm_B[i] and perm_A[i] < perm_C[i].\n\n- For each column j: topmost symbol is B.\n\nThus for each column j, row of B must be the smallest among the three rows. So inv_perm_B[j] < inv_perm_A[j] and inv_perm_B[j] < inv_perm_C[j].\n\nNow we need to find permutations satisfying both. Let's try to find one manually for n=3.\n\nDefine perm_A = (a0,a1,a2), perm_B = (b0,b1,b2), perm_C = (c0,c1,c2).\n\nConstraints:\n\nRow constraints:\n\n- For i=0: a0 < b0, a0 < c0.\n- i=1: a1 < b1, a1 < c1.\n- i=2: a2 < b2, a2 < c2.\n\nColumn constraints: For each column j, define rows:\n\n- For column 0: row_B0 = inv_B[0] = index i where b_i = 0. Similarly row_A0 = inv_A[0] = i where a_i=0, row_C0 = inv_C[0] = i where c_i=0. Must have row_B0 < row_A0 and row_B0 < row_C0.\n\n- For column 1: row_B1 < row_A1 and row_B1 < row_C1.\n\n- For column 2: row_B2 < row_A2 and row_B2 < row_C2.\n\nThus B must be placed in each column at a row that is above (i.e., smaller index) than the rows of A and C in that column.\n\nThus B's permutation must be \"above\" A and C permutations in each column.\n\nThis is similar to a partial order. Let's try to find permutations.\n\nWe need permutations of {0,1,2}.\n\nLet’s start with A's permutation. Since each row's A must be the smallest column in that row, we need a_i to be the minimum among {a_i,b_i,c_i}. So we need to place A in each row at column that is smaller than B and C's columns.\n\nGiven columns are 0,1,2, the only way to have a_i be strictly smaller than both b_i and c_i is to have a_i = 0, and b_i,c_i be 1 and 2 (order doesn't matter). For row where a_i=1, we need b_i,c_i >1, but only 2 is >1; need two distinct >1, impossible. So a_i must be 0 for all rows. But that violates column uniqueness for A (cannot have three rows with column 0). So no solution. This suggests candidate(3,'AAA','BBB') should be None. Yet test expects not None. Something is off.\n\nMaybe the constraint about leftmost character per row is not \"strictly smallest column among the three symbols\", but \"the leftmost character written in the i-th row matches the i-th character of R\", but maybe they allow the leftmost character to be any of the three symbols, but also there can be empty cells '.' before it, but they also allow other letters placed to the left as long as they are '.'? Actually leftmost means first non '.' from left. So it must be the smallest column index among the three symbols. So same.\n\nThus it seems impossible. So either test is wrong or problem statement is slightly different. Let's examine other test cases to see if they align with the \"first column/row\" interpretation.\n\nTest: result = candidate(3, 'ABC', 'ABC'), they expect a solution. Under first column/row interpretation, R = ['A','B','C'] as first column; C = ['A','B','C'] as first row; we need to fill grid accordingly. The example solution 'ABC','BCA','CAB' matches that: first column: A,B,C; first row: A,B,C. That matches both constraints. So both interpretations produce a solution.\n\nTest: result = candidate(5, 'AABAC', 'AABCA') is not None.\n\nLet's check if first column/row interpretation works: n=5, R[0]=A, R[1]=A, R[2]=B, R[3]=A, R[4]=C. First column must be those letters. C = 'AABCA' as first row: grid[0]=A A B C A.\n\nBut we need e