eed to compute sum of weights for each subset; compute sum % k; track minimal remainder. Since w < k, sum can exceed Python int but Python's big ints handle it.\n\nPotential optimization: precompute edges list as (u-1, v-1, w). Use union-find.\n\nEdge count m maybe up to 28, choose n-1 edges: at most 1.1M combos. For each combination we need to verify that it forms a spanning tree: i.e., number of edges = n-1 and graph is connected and acyclic. We can test with union-find: initially each node separate; for each edge, if find(u) == find(v) => cycle -> invalid; else union. After processing all edges, check if number of unions made = n-1 (i.e., no cycles) and final number of components = 1. If any cycle, skip.\n\nSince edges count is small, we can also early break on cycle detection.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\ndef min_spanning_tree_mod_k(n, m, k, edges):\n    edges0 = [(u-1, v-1, w) for u,v,w in edges]\n    best = None\n    from itertools import combinations\n    for combo in combinations(range(m), n-1):\n        parent = list(range(n))\n        rank = [0]*n\n        def find(x):\n            while parent[x]!=x:\n                parent[x]=parent[parent[x]]\n                x=parent[x]\n            return x\n        def union(x,y):\n            rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n            if rx == ry:\n                return False\n            if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n                parent[rx] = ry\n            elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n                parent[ry] = rx\n            else:\n                parent[ry] = rx\n                rank[rx] += 1\n            return True\n        total = 0\n        ok = True\n        for idx in combo:\n            u,v,w = edges0[idx]\n            if not union(u,v):\n                ok=False\n                break\n            total += w\n        if ok:\n            # check connectivity: after processing n-1 edges with no cycles, we must have exactly n-1 unions -> connected\n            # Actually if there were no cycles, it's a tree if all nodes are connected, which we can check if all find() same.\n            root = find(0)\n            for i in range(1,n):\n                if find(i)!=root:\n                    ok=False\n                    break\n        if ok:\n            rem = total % k\n            if best is None or rem < best:\n                best = rem\n                if best == 0:\n                    # can't get better than 0, break early? but we cannot break because we might not find any 0 combos earlier.\n                    # but if we found 0, we can return 0\n                    return 0\n    return best if best is not None else 0\n\nEdge Cases: Graph is connected, so at least one spanning tree exists.\n\nTesting: Provided test cases.\n\nPotential issue: For n=2, combos of size 1 (n-1). We'll compute union and check connectivity; will be fine.\n\nThe last test case in the list includes an assert that candidate returns 99999999999999, but earlier they had the same call that returned 81703739369768? Wait there is contradictory test:\n\nassert candidate(2, 1, 249959928878916, [(1, 2, 81703739369768)]) == 81703739369768\n\nLater they have:\n\nassert candidate(2, 1, 249959928878916, [(1, 2, 81703739369768)]) == 99999999999999\n\nTwo contradictory expectations for same input. That's weird. Let's examine the test suite: The first block includes several asserts, then after those they have:\n\nassert candidate(2, 1, 249959928878916, [(1, 2, 81703739369768)]) == 99999999999999\n\nThis seems contradictory; maybe they purposely added a contradictory test to check something else? Let's parse the entire test block.\n\nThe tests are:\n\n```\ndef check(candidate):\n    # Sample test cases\n    assert candidate(5, 6, 328, ...) == 33\n    assert candidate(6, 5, 998244353, ...) == 325437688\n    assert candidate(8, 28, 936294041850197, ...) == 11360716373\n    \n    # Additional test cases\n    assert candidate(2, 1, 249959928878916, [(1, 2, 81703739369768)]) == 81703739369768\n    assert candidate(2, 