dp[3]*inv2 = inv2^2. So dp[17] gets dp[10]*inv2 = inv2^3 = 125000001? Let's compute: dp[17] initially inv2 (from 17 alone). After processing 7, dp[17] gets dp[10]*inv2 = inv2^2 * inv2 = inv2^3. So dp[17] = inv2 + inv2^3.\n\nAlso dp[17] may get dp[...], maybe from 2+... Not needed.\n\nNow dp[17] = inv2 + inv2^3 = 500000004 + 125000001 = 625000005. ans = dp[17] * 16 mod M = 16*625000005 mod M = 10000000080 mod M? 10000000080 - 10*1000000007=10000000080 - 10000000070 = 10. Wait 10? Let's compute: 625000005 * 16 = 10,000,000,080. Subtract 9*1000000007 = 9,000,000,063 => remainder = 1,000,000,017? Wait 9*1000000007 = 9,000,000,063. Subtract: 10,000,000,080 - 9,000,000,063 = 1,000,000,017. Subtract one more 1,000,000,007 = 10. So ans = 10. But expected 8. Hmm our DP gave 10, not 8. Something off. Let's recalc.\n\nWait maybe we mis-calc dp contributions. Let's compute manually the sum of powers for [3,17,2,7] with k=17.\n\nOriginal array length n=4. All subsequences T that sum to 17:\n\n- [17] (len=1)\n- [3,2,7,5]? Wait 3+2+7=12, not 17. 3+2+? Actually there is no 5. So maybe [3,2,7,?] No. Let's list possible combos:\n\nNumbers: 3, 17, 2, 7.\n\nFind subsequences sum to 17:\n\n- [17] alone.\n- [3,2,7,5]? No 5.\n- [3,2,7,?] Actually 3+2+7=12. Need 5 more, not present.\n- [3,?] 3+? = 17 => need 14, not present.\n- [2,?] need 15, not present.\n- [7,?] need 10, not present.\n- [3, 2, ?] need 12 => 5, not present.\n- [3,7] = 10, need 7 more, not present.\n- [2,7] = 9, need 8, not present.\n- [3,2,7] = 12, need 5, not present.\nThus only [17] is possible.\n\nThus there is exactly one subsequence T of length 1. Contribution = 2^(4-1) = 8. So answer = 8. Our DP gave 10. So something wrong with DP.\n\nOur DP counted something else: dp[17] = inv2 (from 17 alone) + inv2^3 (from 3+2+7?). But 3+2+7 sum to 12, not 17. Wait dp[10] (sum 10) from 3+7 = 10, not 12. Then dp[10] contributed to dp[17] via adding 7? Actually we process 7 after 2. Let's step through carefully.\n\nWe'll simulate D