dding one element (exponent0) for i=5. However, we didn't finish generating all bits for i=5 (the second bit exponent2). Because we broke after exceeding max_index. That is okay because we don't need that element for the current max_index (which was 5). But later when we need to extend to index7, we need to generate the remaining bits for i=5 (exponent2) and then continue with i=6 etc.\n\nOur algorithm currently: In _extend_to, while len(_prefix)-1 <= max_index:\n    i = _next_i\n    x = i\n    while x:\n        low = x & -x\n        exp = low.bit_length() - 1\n        _prefix.append(_prefix[-1] + exp)\n        if len(_prefix)-1 > max_index:\n            break\n        x ^= low\n    _next_i += 1\n\nThus after break inner loop, we exit inner loop (we don't continue processing remaining bits for that i). But then we increment _next_i and continue outer while loop. However, we have not processed the remaining bits of i=5. Those bits are lost. But the outer loop will only generate bits for i=6, 7, etc. The missing bits (exponent2) for i=5 (the second element for i=5) is not generated. That means the sequence will be missing that element. That's a bug.\n\nThus we need to handle scenario where we break early due to reaching max_index. In that case, we must keep track of the remaining bits for the current i and generate them later when needed. Or we need to avoid breaking early and just generate all bits for i, but we can stop after we exceed max_index (i.e., after generating enough elements). However, we can't break out of the inner loop prematurely because we need to generate the remaining bits later for future calls (if we need more indices). The naive approach of breaking inner loop loses those bits.\n\nThus we need a different approach: We can generate all bits for each i, but we can also stop adding to prefix after we exceed max_index (but we still need to store the remaining bits for later). Actually we can still generate them but not add to prefix because we don't 