at we haven't added. That's okay because we didn't need them for now. But later when we need more elements, we will need to generate those remaining bits. But our algorithm will start from the next i, not from the current i. So we will lose those bits again.\n\nThus we need a way to remember where we left off within the bits of current i.\n\nSimplify: Instead of generating bits per i, we can generate the sequence element by element using a simple loop that increments a counter and computes exponent for each element in order. But we need a way to generate the sequence in order without missing bits.\n\nOne approach: Use a while loop that tracks current i and current bit position within i (like iterate through set bits). We can have variables:\n\n- current_i = 1\n- bits_queue = list of exponents for current_i sorted ascending.\n\nWe can generate bits for i on demand: When bits_queue is empty, we set bits_queue = list of exponents of next i (populated by extracting set bits). Then we pop the first element from bits_queue (pop(0) maybe). That yields the next element of big_nums_exponents.\n\nThus we can generate the sequence lazily, one element at a time, without missing bits. This way we can stop after we have enough elements, and later we can continue from where we left off (bits_queue may have remaining bits for current_i). However, storing bits_queue for current_i is trivial.\n\nThus we can implement _extend_to that generates up to required length using this method.\n\nImplementation:\n\nglobal _prefix, _next_i, _bits_queue\n\nInitialize:\n\n_prefix = array('Q', [0])\n_next_i = 1\n_bits_queue = []  # list of pending exponents for current i\n\ndef _extend_to(max_index):\n    global _prefix, _next_i, _bits_queue\n    while len(_prefix)-1 <= max_index:\n        if not _bits_queue:\n            # fill bits_queue with exponents of _next_i\n            x = _next_i\n            # generate exponents ascending\n            # using while x:\n            #   low = x & -x\n     