ed to compute prefix exponent sum up to index p inclusive. So if p >=0:\n\ni = find_i(p)\nbits_before = S_pop(i-1) # total bits before i\noffset = p - bits_before  # offset within block i (0-indexed)\nsum_full = S_pos(i-1)  # exponent sum for all previous blocks\nbits_of_i = get_set_bit_positions(i) sorted ascending\nsum_partial = sum(bits_of_i[:offset+1])  # include offset index\n\nreturn sum_full + sum_partial\n\nNow we need to handle large N for S_pos(i-1) etc. Use same function.\n\nNow we need to implement get_set_bit_positions(i):\n    pos = []\n    k = 0\n    while i > 0:\n        if i & 1:\n            pos.append(k)\n        i >>= 1\n        k += 1\n    return pos\n\nWorks.\n\nNow compute answer for query:\n\nif from_i == 0:\n    prefix_before = 0\nelse:\n    prefix_before = prefix_sum(from_i - 1)\nprefix_R = prefix_sum(to_i)\nE = prefix_R - prefix_before\nans = pow(2, E, mod_i)\n\nNow we need to ensure that mod_i may be up to 1e5, pow(2, E, mod) works.\n\nNow test with given examples.\n\nBefore implementing, let's test logic manually on small sample.\n\nbig_nums: i=1: bits [0] -> value 1\ni=2: bits [1] -> value 2\ni=3: bits [0,1] -> values 1,2\ni=4: bits [2] -> value 4\ni=5: bits [0,2] -> values 1,4\ni=6: bits [1,2] -> values 2,4\ni=7: bits [0,1,2] -> values 1,2,4\ni=8: bits [3] -> 8\nSequence: indices and values:\n0:1 (i=1)\n1:2 (i=2)\n2:1 (i=3, first bit)\n3:2 (i=3, second)\n4:4 (i=4)\n5:1 (i=5)\n6:4 (i=5)\n7:2 (i=6)\n8:4 (i=6)\n9:1 (i=7)\n10:2 (i=7)\n11:4 (i=7)\n12:8 (i=8)\n...\n\nCheck query [1,3,7] from example: indices 1..3 values [2,1,2] product = 4 mod 7 = 4. Good.\n\nNow our code: compute prefix up to 3:\np=3:\nFind i such that S_pop(i)>3.\nCompute S_pop(1)=1, S_pop(2)=2 (since popcount(2)=1), S_pop(3)=4 (popcount(3)=2). So i=3, offset = p - S_pop(2) = 3-2=1. Bits of i=3 are [0,1]; offset 1 includes bits[0:2] => sum bits =0+1=1. sum_full = S_pos(2) = sum_{i=1,2} bit_position_sum(i). bit_position_sum(1)=0, bit_position_sum(2)=1 => sum_full=1. So pref