h k as a list of chars: we have previous substring of length k-1 (maybe we stored it as a list?), but we can also store the bits in mask and then reconstruct using mask bits and new char.\n\nGiven mask (k-1 bits) representing characters positions i-(k-1) ... i-1 in order older to newer, we can extract bits in order: for pos = k-2 down to 0? Actually we need to know mapping. Let's define that bit (k-2) is the most recent (i-1), and bit 0 is the oldest (i-(k-1)). Then we can shift left and add new char at LSB? No, if we shift left and add new char at LSB, then the LSB will be new char (most recent), and older bits shift left (increase significance). That means bit 0 becomes 0 after shift (lost). That's not correct. We want to drop the oldest (bit 0) and shift bits towards older? Let's think.\n\nOption 1: Represent mask where bit 0 is the most recent (i-1), and bit (k-2) is the oldest (i-(k-1)). Then shift right by 1 to drop the most recent? Actually we want to drop the oldest when we add new char. If bit 0 is most recent, then the oldest is bit (k-2). When we shift left, bits move towards higher index: bit (k-2) (oldest) goes to bit (k-1) (out of range) and is dropped if we mask. But that would drop the newest? Wait, if bit 0 = most recent, then bit (k-2) = oldest. To drop oldest, we need to shift left? Let's check: Suppose mask bits (k-2...0). The oldest is at highest index (k-2). If we shift left, the bits move to higher index: bit (k-2) goes to index (k-1) (out of mask), thus gets dropped. Meanwhile bits shift left, and we can set bit 0 to new char (most recent). But shifting left moves bits towards older positions? Actually think of binary representation: e.g., mask = b_{k-2} ... b_1 b_0, where b_0 is LSB. If we shift left (mask << 1), we get b_{k-2} ... b_1 b_0 0. Then mask << 1 & ((1 << (k-1)) - 1) discards the bit that overflowed beyond k-1 bits. So the new mask bits after shift left are: bits positions 0 to k-2 (LSB to MSB) are: new bit 0 = 0 (since we inserte