ence of length L that is arithmetic if the differences between consecutive elements are equal. For arithmetic progression of original values, an arithmetic subsequence must have constant index step (since value differences are proportional to index step). So DP counts each arithmetic progression by its last two indices (j,i) and diff = (i-j)*D. It will extend previous sequences with same diff. So it will correctly count all arithmetic progressions.\n\nNow we can test for n=5 AP [1,2,3,4,5]. The number of length 3 arithmetic subsequences is sum_{s=1}^{2} (5-2*s) = s=1: 3, s=2: 1 => total 4. Let's compute DP manually? Should get ans[3]=4. Let's test with DP.\n\nIndices pairs:\n(0,1) diff=1\n(0,2) diff=2\n(0,3) diff=3\n(0,4) diff=4\n(1,2) diff=1\n(1,3) diff=2\n(1,4) diff=3\n(2,3) diff=1\n(2,4) diff=2\n(3,4) diff=1\n\nNow DP will extend sequences accordingly. For diff=1: dp[1][1][2]=1; dp[2][1][2]=1; dp[3][1][2]=1; dp[4][1][2]=1. Then extensions for length 3: for i=2 (j=1 diff=1) extend dp[1][1][2] -> dp[2][1][3]=1; for i=3 (j=2 diff=1) extend dp[2][1][2] -> dp[3][1][3]=1; also j=1 diff=2 not same; for i=4 (j=3 diff=1) extend dp[3][1][2] -> dp[4][1][3]=1; also j=2 diff=2 not same; also j=1 diff=3 not same; also j=0 diff=4 not same. So diff=1 yields 3 length-3 sequences (starting at indices (0,1,2), (1,2,3), (2,3,4)). diff=2: pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (2,4). dp[2][2][2]=1, dp[3][2][2]=1, dp[4][2][2]=1. For length-3: i=3, j=1 diff=2 extends dp[1][2]? dp[1][2] doesn't exist (since diff=2 not present at index 1). Actually dp[1][2] would be empty because diff=2 from (0,2) not involving index1. So no extension. i=4, j=2 diff=2 extends dp[2][2][2]=1 -> dp[4][2][3]=1. So diff=2 yields 1 length-3 sequence: (0,2,4). So total length-3 sequences = 3+1 =4, matches.\n\nThus DP works.\n\nNow test for length 4: The count for AP n=5 should be sum_{s=1}^{floor((n-1)/3)} (n - 3*s). For s=1: n-3=2 => two sequences: (0,1,2,3) and (1,2,3,4). s=?? floor(4/3)=1. So total 2. DP should produce ans[4]