e steps between consecutive selected indices are equal). Because the values differ by D times index differences. For the values to have equal differences, the index differences must be equal. So triples that are not equally spaced produce differences (j-i)D vs (k-j)D which are different unless j-i = k-j.\n\nThus not all triples are arithmetic; only those with equal gaps. So for length >2, we need indices that form arithmetic progression.\n\nThus the problem counts subsequences where the values themselves are arithmetic, not just any subsequence of arithmetic progression. So indeed, for arithmetic progression sequence, the number of arithmetic subsequences of length k is the number of ways to choose k positions that are equally spaced, i.e., choose a starting index i and a step d>0 such that i + (k-1)*d <= n-1. So count = sum_{d=1}^{?} (n - (k-1)*d). For each step d, there are n - (k-1)*d possible starting indices. So total count = sum_{d=1}^{floor((n-1)/(k-1))} (n - (k-1)*d). Compute that. For n=80, k=3: sum_{d=1}^{floor(79/2)=39} (80 - 2*d) = sum_{d=1}^{39} (80 - 2d) = 39*80 - 2*sum_{d=1}^{39} d = 3120 - 2*(39*40/2) = 3120 - 2*780 = 3120 - 1560 = 1560. Indeed matches answer. Good.\n\nThus the DP approach must count only subsequences where consecutive differences are equal. Our DP algorithm does that; it counts subsequences where the difference between values is constant. For arithmetic progression, diff between consecutive values is D * step. For triples, we need diff between first and second = diff between second and third => D*step1 = D*step2 => step1 = step2. So DP ensures that.\n\nThus DP algorithm is correct.\n\nNow test for repeated values (diff=0). For repeated values, diff between any two positions is 0, so any subsequence has diff 0, which is constant. Thus all subsequences are arithmetic. So DP should produce binomial.\n\nNow test for other random case: The large random list. DP should compute counts. The answer list for that random case is [80, 3160, 1, 