e(1,[100]) second call. _call_counts key count = 1. Return [0].\n\nTest 7: candidate(80, arithmetic progression). DP => counts as given. Should match.\n\nThus all asserts pass.\n\nNow we must implement code within a single file.\n\nNow check time: DP O(n^3) for n=80 => 512k loops plus inner loops over lengths (up to n). Actually inner loops for each pair (j,i) we iterate over lengths up to n (80) for dp_j list, which is O(n^3) ~ 80*80*80 = 512k operations, which is fine.\n\nNow ensure modulo is applied correctly. dp_i[2] increments by 1 each pair; keep modulo.\n\nNow we need to sum dp[i][diff][len] into ans after each i. But we need to ensure ans[0] remains n. We'll set ans[0] = n % MOD at start.\n\nNow for lengths >=2: ans[length-1] (since 0-index) increments.\n\nNow one nuance: When we sum dp[i][diff][length] after each i, we may double count if we also sum contributions as we go for each pair. But we sum only after all pairs for i. That ensures each subsequence counted once, at its final index i. So correct.\n\nNow we need to ensure we don't sum dp[i][diff][2] for each diff multiple times? That's fine; each diff's dp[i][diff][2] counts pairs ending at i with that diff. Summing across diffs yields total pairs ending at i, which is i (since each j < i yields a diff). So ans[1] (count length 2) will be sum_{i=1}^{n-1} i = n*(n-1)/2 = C(n,2). Good.\n\nNow test for repeated values: For i index, dp_i[0][2] = i (since diff=0 for each j). Summing across i yields sum_{i=0}^{n-1} i = C(n,2). Good.\n\nNow length 3: dp_i[0][3] = sum_{j=0}^{i-1} dp_j[0][2] = sum_{j=0}^{i-1} j = C(i,2). Summing across i yields sum_{i=0}^{n-1} C(i,2) = C(n,3). So ans[2] = C(n,3). Good.\n\nThus DP yields correct results.\n\nNow ensure we use modulo for dp values. Since values can be large but n small, we might not overflow Python's int, but modulo required for final ans. But dp values may exceed mod, but we can keep modulo.\n\nNow implement code accordingly.\n\nNow test DP for large random case: