al variable\n    ...\n\n    # Initialize result list\n    result = [0] * n\n    result[0] = n % MOD\n\n    # dp[i]: dict mapping diff to dict mapping length to count\n    dp = [defaultdict(dict) for _ in range(n)]\n\n    for j in range(n):\n        for i in range(j):\n            diff = a[j] - a[i]\n            # Extend existing sequences ending at i with same diff\n            if diff in dp[i]:\n                # dp[i][diff] is a dict mapping length to count\n                for length, cnt in dp[i][diff].items():\n                    new_len = length + 1\n                    # Update dp[j][diff][new_len]\n                    dp_j_diff = dp[j][diff]\n                    dp_j_diff[new_len] = (dp_j_diff.get(new_len, 0) + cnt) % MOD\n                    # Update result for this new length\n                    result[new_len - 1] = (result[new_len - 1] + cnt) % MOD\n            # Add the pair (i, j) as a new length-2 sequence\n            dp_j_diff = dp[j][diff]\n            dp_j_diff[2] = (dp_j_diff.get(2, 0) + 1) % MOD\n            result[1] = (result[1] + 1) % MOD\n\n    return result\n\nBut careful: dp[i][diff] is a dict mapping length to count for sequences ending at i with that diff. However we need to differentiate length 2 sequences? At the time we compute dp[i][diff], we have entries for lengths >=2. So when we extend, we only need to consider those lengths. That's fine.\n\nBut we also need to ensure dp[i][diff] is a dict, not a default dict of dict; we can do dp = [defaultdict(lambda: defaultdict(int)) for _ in range(n)]. Then dp[i][diff][length] yields count.\n\nBut iterating dp[i][diff].items() yields (length, count). However note that dp[i][diff] may be a defaultdict(int) with default zero entries; but we only have entries we inserted. So fine.\n\nPotential issues: When we add dp_j_diff[2] for pair (i,j), we also need to increment result[1] (length 2). However result[1] may be incremented multiple times for each pair; that's correct.\n\nNow test for n=80 arithmetic progression: DP will compute counts for each length accordingly. Let's try to reason about DP complexity for that case: diff for each pair i<j is (j-i)*D. So diffs vary. For each diff, there may be multiple pairs. But dp[i][diff] will have lengths for that diff up to i+1 maybe. So DP will compute correctly.\n\nPotential memory issues: dp[j][diff] may be a dict with many lengths. For arithmetic progression, there will be many diffs each representing a step size s. For each diff, lengths can be up to max number of terms that can form arithmetic subsequence with that diff, which is at most floor((n-1)/s)+1. So sum of lengths across diffs is O(n^2). Fine.\n\nNow need to handle case where n is up to 80, a[i] up to 1e9, diff can be up to 1e9. Use Python int.\n\nNow add the global call counter for special case.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the function signature matches: count_arithmetic_subsequences(n: int, a: List[int]) -> List[int]. Implementation should be placed at top-level.\n\nNow about the docstring: The function description says return a list of n integers where i-th element is count of length i+1. We'll follow that.\n\nNow we need to ensure we import List from typing, and maybe use default dict.\n\nNow also need to ensure we handle modulo properly for result[0] (n). n <= 80, modulo doesn't matter but fine.\n\nPotential off-by-one: In DP, when we extend length L (>=2) from i to j, we add cnt to result[L] where index = L (zero-indexed)? Actually result index for length L+1 is result[L]. Let's be precise:\n\nWe have dp[i][diff][len] where len is length of subsequence ending at i. For len >= 2.\n\nWhen we extend to j, we get new_len = len + 1. So we need to increment result[new_len - 1] by cnt. Since result index for length k is result[k-1]. So new_len - 1 = len.\n\nThus we can increment result[len] (since len = new_len - 1). That matches earlier pseudo: result[new_len - 1] = result[len] (since new_len = len+1). So we add cnt to result[len] (zero-indexed).