ip)\n- (some maybe missing) Let's systematically parse original list for items with salt <=77:\n\nOriginal list again:\n\n1. (30,10) ✓\n2. (10,1) ✓\n3. (42,4249) ✗\n4. (569,17) ✓ (sweet=569, salt=17)\n5. (15,9) ✓\n6. (133,5786) ✗\n7. (644,6054) ✗\n8. (207,231) ✗\n9. (6,194) ✗\n10. (5218,71) ✓\n11. (461,63) ✓\n12. (35,216) ✗\n13. (32,88) ✗\n14. (2048,455) ✗\n15. (5,3188) ✗\n16. (4,2924) ✗\n17. (1,42) ✓\n18. (154,350) ✗\n19. (5279,24) ✓\n20. (50,5605) ✗\n21. (1587,3) ✓\n22. (6574,276) ✗\n23. (162,3173) ✗\n24. (129,17) ✓\n25. (79,1075) ✗\n26. (1,62) ✓\n27. (11,19) ✓\n28. (1373,1904) ✗\n29. (19,197) ✗\n30. (1193,5) ✓\n31. (1515,49) ✓\n32. (2007,3743) ✗\n33. (15,1) ✓\n34. (160,233) ✗\n35. (120,12) ✓\n36. (1374,2804) ✗\n37. (23,627) ✗\n38. (25,995) ✗\n39. (54,1) ✓\n40. (5,180) ✗\n41. (1754,506) ✗\n42. (5,19) ✓\n43. (3,1494) ✗\n44. (452,1478) ✗\n45. (17,9424) ✗\n46. (36,4397) ✗\n47. (2,3581) ✗\n48. (5066,29) ✓\n49. (13,7296) ✗\n50. (4,199) ✗\n51. (496,1) ✓\n52. (122,2817) ✗\n53. (1,17) ✓\n54. (1,261) ✗\n55. (1,5) ✓\n56. (29,15) ✓\n57. (46,1917) ✗\n58. (86,8412) ✗\n59. (422,590) ✗\n60. (2,12) ✓\n61. (205,511) ✗\n62. (573,5) ✓\n63. (363,366) ✗\n64. (713,46) ✓\n65. (74,1302) ✗\n66. (1,9) ✓\n67. (6,3) ✓\n68. (6128,2) ✓\n69. (198,427) ✗\n70. (37,5) ✓\n71. (3,937) ✗\n72. (164,874) ✗\n73. (143,2493) ✗\n74. (28,33) ✓\n75. (6425,2066) ✗\n76. (21,8) ✓\n77. (339,1586) ✗\n78. (1,8) ✓\n79. (1273,3) ✓\n80. (68,18) ✓\n\nThus total count of \"feasible items\" (both constraints) is number of ✓ marks. Let's count: items with ✓:\n\n1,2,4,5,10,11,17,19,21,24,26,27,30,31,33,35,39,42,48,51,53,55,56,60,62,64,66,67,68,70,74,76,78,79,80. That's 35 items (maybe). Let's count precisely: \n\nIndices (1-indexed) with ✓: \n1,2,4,5,10,11,17,19,21,24,26,27,30,31,33,35,39,42,48,51,53,55,56,60,62,64,66,67,68,70,74,76,78,79,80. That's 35 items.\n\nNow we need to select a subset of these items maximizing count while sum sweet <=6376, sum salt <=77.\n\nGiven that each has low salt, we need to keep sum salt <=77, which is a very restrictive constraint. So we can only pick items whose total salt <=77. Many items have salt values up to 71. Let's list each with sweet and salt:\n\n1: (30,10)\n2: (10,1)\n4: (569,17)\n5: (15,9)\n10: (5218,71)\n11: (461,63)\n17: (1,42)\n19: (5279,24)\n21: (1587,3)\n24: (129,17)\n26: (1,62)\n27: (11,19)\n30: (1193,5)\n31: (1515,49)\n33: (15,1)\n35: (120,12)\n39: (54,1)\n42: (5,19)\n48: (5066,29)\n51: (496,1)\n53: (1,17)\n55: (1,5)\n56: (29,15)\n60: (2,12)\n62: (573,5)\n64: (713,46)\n66: (1,9)\n67: (6,3)\n68: (6128,2)\n70: (37,5)\n74: (28,33)\n76: (21,8)\n78: (1,8)\n79: (1273,3)\n80: (68,18)\n\nNow we need to pick subset with sum sweet <=6376 and sum salt <=77.\n\nWe can suspect that max_feasible = 16. Let's see if we can pick 16 items meeting constraints.\n\nBecause the salt constraint is tight, we need to pick items with small salts. Let's sort by salt:\n\n- salt=1: items: (10,1), (15,1), (54,1), (496,1) -> 4 items\n- salt=2: (6128,2)\n- salt=3: (1587,3), (6,3), (1273,3)\n- salt=4? none\n- salt=5: (1193,5), (573,5), (37,5)\n- salt=6? none\n- salt=7? none\n- salt=8: (21,8), (1,8)\n- salt=9: (30,10? actually salt=10), (15,9), (1,9) \n- salt=10: (30,10)\n- salt=12: (120,12), (2,12)\n- salt=15: (29,15)\n- salt=17: (569,17), (129,17), (1,17)\n- salt=18: (68,18)\n- salt=19: (11,19), (5,19)\n- salt=24: (5279,24)\n- salt=29: (5066,29)\n- salt=33: (28,33)\n- salt=42: (1,42)\n- salt=46: (713,46)\n- salt=49: (1515,49)\n- salt=63: (461,63)\n- salt=71: (5218,71)\n- salt=62: (1,62)\n- salt=24? we have (5279,24)\n- salt=17? many.\n\nNow we need to keep total salt <=77. So we can pick many low-salt items. Let's try to maximize count: pick all items with salt 1 (4 items). Then we can pick items with salt 2 (1 item), salt 3 (3 items) = total salt = 1*4 + 2 + 3*3 = 4 + 2 + 9 = 15. Then we can add salt 5 items (3 items) => +15 => total salt 30. Then salt 8 items (2 items) => +16 => total salt 46. Then salt 9 items (maybe 2 items: (15,9) and (1,9)?) Actually we hav