79 >77, exceed salt constraint. So cannot add.\n\nWhat about (29,15) sweet=29, salt=15: new salt = 74+15=89 >77. Not allowed.\n\nWhat about (30,10) sweet=30, salt=10: new salt=84 >77.\n\nWhat about (68,18): salt=18, new salt=92 >77.\n\nWhat about (1,42) salt=42, new salt=116 >77.\n\nThus we cannot add any more items without exceeding salt. So max_feasible maybe 15? But expected answer is 17, which would require max_feasible = 16. Let's see if we can find a subset of size 16 that satisfies constraints. Maybe we can include a different combination to increase count.\n\nLet's try to consider using (1,5) (sweet=1, salt=5) instead of some other items. Actually we used (1193,5),(573,5),(37,5) - three items with salt 5. Could replace (1193,5) with (1,5) and maybe add another low-salt item (like (30,10) or (569,17)). Let's try to find a combination of 16 items.\n\nWe need total salt <=77. Let's try to sum salt contributions for 16 items: average salt per item <=77/16 ≈ 4.8. So many items must have salt <=5. We have a limited number of items with salt <=5: let's count them:\n\nItems with salt <=5:\n\n- (10,1) [s=10]\n- (15,1) [s=15]\n- (54,1) [s=54]\n- (496,1) [s=496]\n- (1,5) [s=1]\n- (1193,5) [s=1193]\n- (573,5) [s=573]\n- (37,5) [s=37]\n- (30,10) is salt=10 >5, not included.\n\nAlso (2,12) not <=5.\n\nAlso (1,8) >5.\n\n(1,9) >5.\n\n(1,9) not.\n\nThus we have 8 items with salt <=5 (including the four with salt=1 and four with salt=5). Actually we have 4 with salt=1 and 4 with salt=5: total 8.\n\nWe need 16 items, so we must also include items with higher salt but keep total <=77.\n\nLet's compute total salt of these 8 low-salt items: salt sum = 1+1+1+1 + 5+5+5+5 = 24. Sweet sum = 10+15+54+496+1+1193+573+37 = 2379? Let's compute: 10+15=25, +54=79, +496=575, +1=576, +1193=1769, +573=2342, +37=2379. So we have 8 items with total sweet 2379, total salt 24.\n\nNow we need to add 8 more items (to reach 16) with total salt <=77-24 = 53. So average salt per added item <=53/8 ≈ 6.6