pawns, and current position (the knight's location). The location could be any coordinate (0-49,0-49) but we can restrict to either the start or any pawn location, because after each capture, the knight ends at that pawn's location. So the location will always be either the starting position (if mask=0) or one of the pawn positions in the mask (the last captured). However we need to know which location we are at given mask. We can store DP[mask][i] where i is index of the last captured pawn (or special index for start). That yields at most (2^n)*(n+1) states, which is fine for n=15: ~32768*16 = 524k states.\n\nTransitions: For each state (mask, last), it's the turn of player = (popcount(mask) % 2 == 0 ? Alice's turn (since Alice starts with 0 captured) else Bob's turn). For each pawn j not in mask, we compute distance d = dist[last_position][pawn_j_position]; new_mask = mask | (1<<j). Then we compute value = d + DP[new_mask][j] (since after moving, we add the cost d and continue). If it's Alice's turn, she will choose the pawn j that maximizes total sum; if Bob's turn, he chooses the pawn j that minimizes total sum. So DP[mask][last] = max/min over j of d + DP[new_mask][j].\n\nBase case: mask == all captured (full mask). DP[full][i] = 0 for any i (no more moves). Starting state: mask = 0, last = start (special index). Answer = DP[0][start].\n\nThus we need to compute distances between all pairs of relevant points (including start). We can precompute knight distances on 50x50 board using BFS from each point (or compute distances on the fly via BFS for each pair). Since number of points is at most 16 (start + up to 15 pawns), we can compute BFS from each point to all squares and extract distances to other points. BFS for a 2500 grid is cheap (2500 nodes, each with up to 8 moves). So 16 BFS = 40k nodes visited total, trivial.\n\nThus we can implement DP with memoization (dictionary) or iterative DP from full mask backward. Use bitmask DP with recursion and memo.\n\nCom