sk\n            while pm:\n                lsb = pm & -pm\n                v = (lsb.bit_length() - 1)\n                new_mask |= 1 << (v | val)\n                pm ^= lsb\n            dp_prefix[i][c] = without | new_mask\n        # for c > i, dp_prefix[i][c] remains 0\n\n    # dp_suffix[i][c] for suffix starting at i\n    dp_suffix = [[0] * (k+1) for _ in range(n+1)]\n    dp_suffix[n][0] = 1 << 0\n    for i in range(n-1, -1, -1):\n        val = nums[i]\n        dp_suffix[i][0] = dp_suffix[i+1][0]\n        # number of elements remaining = n-i\n        maxc = min(k, n-i)\n        for c in range(1, maxc + 1):\n            without = dp_suffix[i+1][c]\n            prev_mask = dp_suffix[i+1][c-1]\n            new_mask = 0\n            pm = prev_mask\n            while pm:\n                lsb = pm & -pm\n                v = (lsb.bit_length() - 1)\n                new_mask |= 1 << (v | val)\n                pm ^= lsb\n            dp_suffix[i][c] = without | new_mask\n\n    ans = 0\n    # split point i: prefix length i\n    for i in range(k, n - k + 1):\n        mask1 = dp_prefix[i][k]\n        mask2 = dp_suffix[i][k]\n        if mask1 == 0 or mask2 == 0:\n            continue\n        # iterate over bits of mask1\n        pm1 = mask1\n        while pm1:\n            lsb1 = pm1 & -pm1\n            x = (lsb1.bit_length() - 1)\n            # compute max XOR with any y in mask2\n            # Instead of looping all y, we can precompute best y for each x? But small.\n            pm2 = mask2\n            while pm2:\n                lsb2 = pm2 & -pm2\n                y = (lsb2.bit_length() - 1)\n                val = x ^ y\n                if val > ans:\n                    ans = val\n                pm2 ^= lsb2\n            pm1 ^= lsb1\n    return ans\n\nTest for k=0: early return.\n\nNow test with given examples.\n\nPotential issue: dp_prefix[i][c] for c > i may be left 0. That's fine.\n\nNow test manually with some examples.\n\nLet's write quick mental test for [2,6,7], k=1.\n\ndp_prefix as above.\n\ndp_suffix: n=3.\n\ndp_suffix[3][0]=1.\n\ni=2 (index2 val=7):\ndp_suffix[2][0]=1\nc=1: without = dp_suffix[3][1]=0, prev_mask = dp_suffix[3][0]=1 => new_mask bit (0|7)=7 => 1<<7=128 => dp_suffix[2][1]=128.\n\ni=1 (val=6):\ndp_suffix[1][0]=1\nc=1: without = dp_suffix[2][1]=128; prev_mask = dp_suffix[2][0]=1 => new_mask = 1<<6=64 => dp_suffix[1][1]=128|64=192 bits {6,7}.\nc=2: maxc = min(k=1, n-i=2) => c only up to 1. So ignore.\n\ni=0 (val=2):\ndp_suffix[0][0]=1\nc=1: without = dp_suffix[1][1]=192; prev_mask = dp_suffix[1][0]=1 => new_mask = 1<<2=4 => dp_suffix[0][1]=192|4=196 bits {2,6,7}.\n\nNow compute ans for i from 1 to 2 inclusive.\n\ni=1: mask1 = dp_prefix[1][1] bits {2}. mask2 = dp_suffix[1][1] bits {6,7}. Loop: x=2, y=6 => 4, y=7 =>5 => ans=5.\n\ni=2: mask1 bits {2,6}. mask2 = dp_suffix[2][1] bits {7}. Loop: x=2 => 5, x=6 => 1 => ans stays 5.\n\nReturn ans=5. Good.\n\nNow test [4,2,5,6,7], k=2.\n\nWe'll trust DP.\n\nNow test with large arrays: we need to ensure DP loops correct.\n\nPotential optimization: In dp_prefix loops, we used range(1, min(k,i)+1). That's good.\n\nIn dp_suffix loops, maxc = min(k, n-i). Good.\n\nNow test with k=0 early return.\n\nNow test with [127]*400, k=200.\n\ndp_prefix will compute many possibilities but OR always 127 for any non-empty selection; dp_prefix[i][200] bits only {127} if i >=200 else 0. dp_suffix similar. ans=0.\n\nNow test with [1]*50, k=12 -> OR=1 always, ans=0.\n\nNow test with pattern array; DP should compute correct values.\n\nPotential performance: loops nested within loops: For each split i (max ~400), we iterate over bits of mask1 (max 128) and bits of mask2 (max 128). That's up to 400*128*128 = 6.5 million, fine.\n\nNow check for edge case where mask1 or mask2 might have many bits (dense). That's fine.\n\nNow check for small k like 1: dp_prefix and dp_suffix loops fine.\n\nPotential bug: In dp_prefix, dp_prefix[i][0] = dp_prefix[i-1][0] = 1<<0 always. That's fine.\n\nPotential bug: dp