t (1,3,1,['##.']) not present.\n\nNow test (1,3,4,['##.']) expecting 0. empties = only one cell at idx2, K=4 > 0 => 0.\n\nTest (1,3,1) with grid '#..' we already did.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..' yields 2.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..' also appears as candidate(1,3,1,['#..']) expecting 2.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..' appears only once.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..' passes.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..' passes.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..' passes.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..' passes.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..' passes.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..' passes.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..' passes.\n\nOk.\n\nNow test (1,3,1) with grid '#..', not contradictory.\n\nNow test (1,3,8,['..#']) expecting 0. empties=2, K=8>1 => 0.\n\nNow test (1,3,8,['.#.']) expecting 0.\n\nNow test (1,10,11,['.....#....']) expecting 0. empties maybe 9? Actually grid length 10 with one # at position 5. empties=9. K=11>8 => 0.\n\nNow test (10,1,11,10 rows each '.' ) expecting 0. empties=10, K=11 >9 => 0.\n\nNow test (10,3,11 with many blocked) expecting 0. empties maybe less than 12, K=11 maybe impossible. But our algorithm will compute correctly.\n\nNow test (6,6,2) expecting 56. Let's try to compute manually? Not needed.\n\nNow test (6,6,10) with checkerboard expecting 0. Our adjacency for each empty cell is none, so neighbor_masks empty => dfs returns 0 for any start with steps>0 => total_paths=0.\n\nNow test (7,7,7) with one blocked cell at (5,3). The grid is all '.' except one '#'. So empties = 48? Actually 7x7=49 cells, one blocked => 48 empties. K=7 => path length 8. Our algorithm will count all simple walks of length 7 moves in near-full grid with one obstacle. The answer is 26916. Should be correct.\n\nNow test (9,8,11) expecting 951004. Good.\n\nNow test (9,10,9) expecting 422108. Good.\n\nNow test (9,10,10) expecting 12420. Good.\n\nNow test (8,9,11) expecting 32568. Good.\n\nNow test (8,10,7) expecting 4666. Good.\n\nNow