s. Let's try brute enumeration: Starting at each cell.\n\nWe'll write small script mentally? Let's try systematic enumeration.\n\nWe can treat path as sequence of 5 distinct cells.\n\nLet's enumerate using backtracking mentally.\n\nStart (0,0):\n- step1 to (1,0)\n  - visited {0,0,1,0}\n  - step2 from (1,0) can go to (2,0) or (1,1) (can't go back to (0,0))\n    * Option A: to (2,0)\n        visited {0,0,1,0,2,0}\n        step3 from (2,0): neighbors (1,0) visited, (2,1) not visited -> go to (2,1)\n        visited {0,0,1,0,2,0,2,1}\n        step4 from (2,1): neighbors (2,0) visited, (1,1) not visited -> go to (1,1)\n        visited {0,0,1,0,2,0,2,1,1,1}\n        steps done (4 moves) => count 1.\n    * Option B: to (1,1)\n        visited {0,0,1,0,1,1}\n        step3 from (1,1): neighbors (1,0) visited? Actually (1,0) visited, (2,1) not visited -> go to (2,1)\n        visited {0,0,1,0,1,1,2,1}\n        step4 from (2,1): neighbors (2,0) not visited, (1,1) visited -> go to (2,0)\n        visited {0,0,1,0,1,1,2,1,2,0}\n        steps done => count 1.\nThus start (0,0) yields 2 paths.\n\nStart (1,0):\nWe need to find all simple walks of length 4 from (1,0). Let's enumerate. Starting at (1,0) visited {1,0}.\n\nWe need to generate 4 moves.\n\nWe can think of possible sequences. Let's do systematic.\n\nAt step1, possible moves: (0,0), (2,0), (1,1). Let's consider each.\n\nCase 1: step1 to (0,0).\nVisited {1,0,0,0}\nStep2 from (0,0): neighbor only (1,0) visited, so dead-end => no path of length 4. So this branch yields 0.\n\nCase 2: step1 to (2,0).\nVisited {1,0,2,0}\nStep2 from (2,0): neighbors (1,0) visited, (2,1) not visited -> go to (2,1)\nVisited {1,0,2,0,2,1}\nStep3 from (2,1): neighbors (2,0) visited, (1,1) not visited -> go to (1,1)\nVisited {1,0,2,0,2,1,1,1}\nStep4 from (1,1): neighbors (1,0) visited, (2,1) visited -> dead, no moves left? Actually we need exactly 4 moves; after step3 we have taken 3 moves: (1,0)->(2,0) (1), (2,0)->(2,1) (2), (2,1)->(1,1) (3). We need one