6,7,8,9,10,15,17,19,24,29,34,40,42,43,45,46,49,53,56,57,59,61,62,63,64,72,75,76.\n\nWe also have slash at index? Wait earlier we counted slash at index 1? No, that's before sub.\n\nNow let's compute prefix ones count up to each slash.\n\nWe need prefix ones up to each slash (excluding slash). Let's compute prefix ones as we traverse from index5 to 76.\n\nWe'll compute a running count of ones as we go through indices 5 to 76. Also keep track of total twos after each slash.\n\nBetter to compute prefix ones array and suffix twos.\n\nWe can compute prefix ones at each position i (original index). Let's compute cumulative counts of ones and twos from start of sub.\n\nWe can create a table: for each original index from 5 to 76, we record char, prefix_one count up to that index (including char?), but we need count before slash. So prefix_one_before_i = count of ones in indices [5, i-1] (i exclusive). So for slash at index i, we need prefix_one[i] (where prefix_one[i] counts ones up to i-1). We'll compute.\n\nWe'll also compute suffix_twos after i: count of twos in indices [i+1,76].\n\nThus we need prefix ones and suffix twos.\n\nLet's produce a table:\n\nWe'll list index, char, prefix_ones (count of ones up to previous index), suffix_twos (to be computed later). We'll compute prefix_ones first.\n\nInitialize count = 0.\n\nWe'll iterate index from 5 to 76.\n\n- idx5: char '1' => prefix_ones before idx5 = 0; after processing, count becomes 1.\n- idx6: '/' => prefix_ones before slash = 1 (ones in indices 5). So prefix_ones for slash at idx6 = 1. After slash, count unchanged (still 1).\n- idx7: '/' => prefix_ones = 1.\n- idx8: '/' => prefix_ones = 1.\n- idx9: '/' => prefix_ones = 1.\n- idx10: '/' => prefix_ones = 1.\n- idx11: '2' => prefix_ones before idx11 = 1; char not '1', count stays 1.\n- idx12: '1' => prefix_ones before idx12 = 1; after, count becomes 2.\n- idx13: '1' => prefix_ones before idx13 = 2; after, count 3.\n- idx14: '2' => prefix_ones before idx14 = 3; count st