ax length=3. So answer 3 matches expected.\n\nTest candidate(s,10,25) == 5. Let's compute substring positions 10-25 inclusive: pos10 is char at index9? Let's get string again: positions:\n\n1: '/'\n2: '2'\n3: '/'\n4: '/'\n5: '2'\n6: '/'\n7: '2'\n8: '/'\n9: '2'\n10: '2'\n11: '2'\n12: '/'\n13: '/'\n14: '1'\n15: '/'\n16: '/'\n17: '1'\n18: '1'\n19: '2'\n20: '/'\n21: '/'\n22: '2'\n23: '2'\n24: '1'\n25: '/'\n\nThus substring from 10 to 25 includes characters at positions 10..25: positions:\n\n10: '2'\n11: '2'\n12: '/'\n13: '/'\n14: '1'\n15: '/'\n16: '/'\n17: '1'\n18: '1'\n19: '2'\n20: '/'\n21: '/'\n22: '2'\n23: '2'\n24: '1'\n25: '/'\n\nSo substring: \"22//1//112//221/\". Let's compute best.\n\nWe need slash with enough 1's before and 2's after.\n\nLet's compute prefix ones and suffix twos.\n\nIndices (0-indexed in substring):\n0:'2'\n1:'2'\n2:'/'\n3:'/'\n4:'1'\n5:'/'\n6:'/'\n7:'1'\n8:'1'\n9:'2'\n10:'/'\n11:'/'\n12:'2'\n13:'2'\n14:'1'\n15:'/'\n\nLength 16.\n\nprefix_ones: start 0.\ni0 char '2' => prefix[1]=0.\ni1 char '2' => prefix[2]=0.\ni2 '/' => prefix[3]=0.\ni3 '/' => prefix[4]=0.\ni4 '1' => prefix[5]=1.\ni5 '/' => prefix[6]=1.\ni6 '/' => prefix[7]=1.\ni7 '1' => prefix[8]=2.\ni8 '1' => prefix[9]=3.\ni9 '2' => prefix[10]=3.\ni10 '/' => prefix[11]=3.\ni11 '/' => prefix[12]=3.\ni12 '2' => prefix[13]=3.\ni13 '2' => prefix[14]=3.\ni14 '1' => prefix[15]=4.\ni15 '/' => prefix[16]=4.\n\nsuffix_twos: compute from right.\nsuffix[16]=0.\ni15 '/' => suffix[15]=0.\ni14 '1' => suffix[14]=0.\ni13 '2' => suffix[13]=1.\ni12 '2' => suffix[12]=2.\ni11 '/' => suffix[11]=2.\ni10 '/' => suffix[10]=2.\ni9 '2' => suffix[9]=3.\ni8 '1' => suffix[8]=3.\ni7 '1' => suffix[7]=3.\ni6 '/' => suffix[6]=3.\ni5 '/' => suffix[5]=3.\ni4 '1' => suffix[4]=3.\ni3 '/' => suffix[3]=3.\ni2 '/' => suffix[2]=3.\ni1 '2' => suffix[1]=4.\ni0 '2' => suffix[0]=5.\n\nNow evaluate each slash position:\n\nSlash at i=2: prefix_ones[2] = 0 (ones before index2), suffix_twos[3]=3 => k=0 => length 1.\ni=3: prefix_ones[3]=0, suffix_twos[4]=3 => length1.\ni=5: prefix_ones[5]=0? Actually prefix_ones[5] after index4 (char '1')? Wait prefix_ones[5] corresponds to count after processing char at index4? Let's compute: prefix[0]=0. After i0 '2' => prefix[1]=0. i1 '2' => prefix[2]=0. i2 '/' => prefix[3]=0. i3 '/' => prefix[4]=0. i4 '1' => prefix[5]=1. So before slash at i=5 (char at index5 '/'), prefix_ones[5] = 1. Wait careful: At i=5 char '/', prefix before slash = prefix[5] = 1. suffix after slash = suffix[6] = 3. k = min(1,3) =1 => length=3.\n\nSlash at i=6 (char '/'): prefix_ones[6] = after i5 '/', still 1. suffix[7] =3 => k=1 => length=3.\n\nSlash at i=10 (char '/'): prefix_ones[10] = after i9 '2', which is still 3 (since we had 3 ones up to i8). Actually prefix[10] = 3. suffix[11] =2 => k=2 => length=5.\n\nSlash at i=11 (char '/'): prefix_ones[11] = after i10 '/', still 3. suffix[12] =2 => k=2 => length=5.\n\nSlash at i=15 (char '/'): prefix_ones[15] = after i14 '1' => 4. suffix[16] =0 => k=0 => length=1.\n\nThus max length = 5. matches expected.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nNow we need to test candidate(s,3,5) ==1. Substring positions 3-5: positions: 3:'/',4:'/',5:'2' => substring \"//2\". Slashes present. No '1's before any slash. So answer = 1. Good.\n\nCandidate(s,17,17) ==0. Position 17 is char at index16? Let's check original string: position 17 is '1' (since we have '/2//2/2/222//1//112//221/', position 14 is '1', 15:'/',16:'/',17:'1',? Actually need to check again.\n\nBut anyway, substring is single character '1', no slash => answer 0. Good.\n\nThus algorithm should pass all except duplicate.\n\nNow verify candidate(s,44,56) actual answer based on algorithm: Let's compute substring for that long string.\n\nFirst we need to parse the long string and compute answer for that substring. Let's compute manually to see if answer is 5 or maybe algorithm yields 5 (matching first expected) or 2 (matching second). If algorithm yields 5, then the duplicate test will cause second call expecting