air indices between). So max_len = 2. Good.\n\nTest 6: candidate(19, [13,3,3,3,7,8,2,13,1,4,13,18,17,10,15,16,19,7,14]) expected 2.\n\nLet's locate pairs:\n\nIndices:\n\n0:13 !=3\n\n1:3 ==3? Actually a[1]=3, a[2]=3 => pair at i=1 val=3.\n\n2: i=2: a[2]=3, a[3]=3 => pair at i=2 val=3? Wait a[2]=3, a[3]=3 => yes pair at i=2 as well. But note these pairs overlap: i=1 pair uses indices 1 and 2; i=2 pair uses indices 2 and 3; they share index 2. But our algorithm iterates i step 2 for parity. Let's see parity: i=1 is odd parity; i=2 is even parity. So we will treat them in separate parity loops. However, overlapping pairs cannot be part of a valid subarray because pairs must be non-overlapping. So we need to ensure we only consider non-overlapping pairs. Our algorithm only considers pairs with start indices stepping by 2. That means we only consider pairs that start at positions of a given parity. That ensures that we never consider overlapping pairs because overlapping pairs would have start indices differing by 1, not by 2. Thus our algorithm will not consider both i=1 and i=2 together. It will treat each parity separately.\n\nThus pairs:\n\n- parity 1: i=1 val=3, i=3? a[3]=3, a[4]=7 => not pair. So only pair at i=1.\n\n- parity 0: i=2 val=3, i=4? a[4]=7, a[5]=8 not pair. So only pair at i=2.\n\nThus each parity window will have max length 2. So answer 2.\n\nCheck for any other pairs:\n\nIndices 3: a[3]=3, a[4]=7 not pair.\n\n4:7!=8\n\n5:8!=2\n\n6:2!=13\n\n7:13!=1\n\n8:1!=4\n\n9:4!=13\n\n10:13!=18\n\n11:18!=17\n\n12:17!=10\n\n13:10!=15\n\n14:15!=16\n\n15:16!=19\n\n16:19!=7\n\n17:7!=14\n\nThus only those two overlapping pairs. So answer 2.\n\nOur algorithm will find max_len=2.\n\nTest 7: candidate(18, [7,18,10,15,3,13,11,8,10,6,7,6,2,9,16,3,3,5]) expects 2.\n\nFind pairs:\n\ni=0:7!=18\n\ni=1:18!=10\n\ni=2:10!=15\n\ni=3:15!=3\n\ni=4:3!=13\n\ni=5:13!=11\n\ni=6:11!=8\n\ni=7:8!=10\n\ni=8:10!=6\n\ni=9:6!=7\n\ni=10:7!=6\n\ni=11:6!=2\n\ni=12:2!=9\n\ni=13:9!=16\n\ni=14:16!=3\n\