um_le_i. For a given y, these counts depend on a_i and B prefix.\n\nWe could precompute for each a_i a data structure that can answer count of B <= a_i for any prefix y. This is essentially a 2D counting problem: we have points (j, b_j). For each query (y, threshold a_i), we need count of points with index < y and value <= a_i. This is a typical offline query solved by BIT or segment tree over values, processing by index j.\n\nWe can treat queries as offline: For each query (x,y), we need to compute sum_{i<x} f(i,y) where f(i,y) = a_i * (2*cnt(i,y) - y) - 2*sum_le(i,y). So we need to compute sum_i a_i * cnt(i,y) and sum_i sum_le(i,y) over i<x.\n\nLet's define:\n\n- For each i, cnt(i,y) = number of b_j <= a_i for j < y.\n\n- sum_le(i,y) = sum of b_j for j < y and b_j <= a_i.\n\nThus sum_{i<x} a_i * cnt(i,y) = sum_{j<y} ( sum_{i<x, a_i >= b_j} a_i ). Because each b_j contributes to cnt(i,y) for each i where a_i >= b_j.\n\nSimilarly, sum_{i<x} sum_le(i,y) = sum_{j<y} ( sum_{i<x, a_i >= b_j} b_j ), because for each b_j, it contributes b_j to sum_le for each a_i >= b_j.\n\nThus we can rewrite total sum as:\n\nLet total_B_sum_y = sum_{j<y} b_j.\n\nDefine S1 = sum_{i<x} a_i * (2*cnt(i,y) - y) = 2 * sum_{i<x} a_i * cnt(i,y) - y * sum_{i<x} a_i.\n\nDefine S2 = -2 * sum_{i<x} sum_le(i,y).\n\nThus overall sum = x * total_B_sum_y + S1 + S2 = x * total_B_sum_y + 2 * sum_{i<x} a_i * cnt(i,y) - y * sum_{i<x} a_i - 2 * sum_{i<x} sum_le(i,y).\n\nNow plug in expressions for sum_{i<x} a_i * cnt(i,y) and sum_{i<x} sum_le(i,y).\n\nDefine prefix sums for A: prefix_sum_A[x] = sum_{i<x} a_i.\n\nNow define for each b_j (j<y) we need to compute:\n\n- sum_{i<x, a_i >= b_j} a_i = sum of a_i among i<x with a_i >= b_j.\n\n- count_{i<x, a_i >= b_j} = number of a_i among i<x with a_i >= b_j.\n\nThus:\n\nsum_{i<x} a_i * cnt(i,y) = sum_{j<y} sum_{i<x, a_i >= b_j} a_i.\n\nSimilarly sum_{i<x} sum_le(i,y) = sum_{j<y} b_j * count_{i<x, a_i >= b_j}.\n\nThus overall sum = x * total_B_sum_y + 2 * sum_{j<y}