INF > k (like k+1). When we compute min(cur) > k early exit, we can return INF.\n\nBut we need to ensure that we treat INF for out-of-range accesses.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the algorithm returns exactly the edit distance (or INF if >k). So final distance is prev[m - low_prev] if m within range else INF.\n\nNow test with some examples.\n\nLet's implement a test harness locally (simulate mental). But we need to ensure correct.\n\nEdge Cases:\n\n- When i=1, low may be 0, high may be min(m,i+k). For j=0, cur[0]=i.\n\n- For j>0, compute sub, ins, del.\n\n- For sub, need to get prev[j-1] if j-1 is within prev range (low_prev <= j-1 <= high_prev). Since prev corresponds to previous i-1 row. That row's low_prev = max(0, (i-1)-k) = max(0,i-1-k) = low_prev. So j-1 can be out of range if outside low_prev..high_prev.\n\n- For delete, need prev[j] if j in range.\n\n- For insertion, need cur[idx-1] if idx >0 else INF.\n\nNow careful about INF: we can set INF = k+1.\n\nNow early prune: After computing cur row, if min(cur) > k, we can return INF. Because all distances >k, no need further.\n\nNow final distance: if m within current low_prev..high_prev (i=n row), return prev[m - low_prev] else INF.\n\nNow test on small examples.\n\nTest 1: s='abc', t='awtf', k=3. Already did.\n\nTest 2: s='abc', t='awtf', k=2. We computed final distance 3 >2 => return False.\n\nTest 3: s='cspdhl', t='eppnz', k=19. Let's compute quickly to see if algorithm returns 5. We'll trust.\n\nNow test candidate(5,'m','ouwajo') with k=5. Diff=5. Let's compute algorithm to see if it returns 6. It should.\n\nSimulate: n=1, m=6, k=5, diff=5 <=k, so we compute DP.\n\nInitialize prev for i=0: low_prev=0, high_prev=min(m,k)=5 (since k=5, m=6). Actually min(m,k) = 5 (since m=6 >5). So prev = [0,1,2,3,4,5] representing DP[0][j]=j for j=0..5. Note j=6 not included (j=m). So we have only j up to 5. Because we limited to k band, we only compute up to j=5 for i=0. That's okay because distances beyond that would be >k