n T = 1.\n\nWe'll list all assignments:\n\nWe can consider sets S and T.\n\nWe need to consider S subset of {2,3,4} non-empty, T subset disjoint, both non-empty. So partition of a subset of {2,3,4} into two non-empty parts (some elements may be unused). Equivalent to choose S non-empty, T non-empty disjoint, ignoring order? Since ordered, we consider ordered pairs.\n\nEnumerate all possible pairs (S,T) disjoint non-empty subsets of {2,3,4}:\n\nAll subsets of {2,3,4}: 7 non-empty subsets.\n\nWe need ordered pairs (S,T) where S,T non-empty, disjoint. Let's list all S and T possibilities:\n\nTake S size 1:\n\n- S={2}. Then T can be any non-empty subset of {3,4}: {3}, {4}, {3,4}. For each, need gcd(T)=1. Compute gcds: {3} gcd=3 not allowed; {4} gcd=4 not allowed; {3,4} gcd=1 allowed. So only T={3,4} works. So 1 pair.\n\n- S={3}. T subsets of {2,4}: {2} gcd2 not allowed; {4} gcd4 not allowed; {2,4} gcd2 not allowed. So none.\n\n- S={4}. T subsets of {2,3}: {2} gcd2 not allowed; {3} gcd3 not allowed; {2,3} gcd1 allowed. So T={2,3} works. So 1 pair.\n\nSo from size1 S, we have 2 ordered pairs: ( {2}, {3,4} ) and ( {4}, {2,3} ).\n\nNow S size 2:\n\n- S={2,3} (gcd=1). T can be any non-empty subset of {4}: {4} gcd=4 not allowed. So none.\n\n- S={2,4} (gcd=2). Not gcd1, so discard S because gcd(S) must be 1.\n\n- S={3,4} (gcd=1). T subset of {2}: {2} gcd2 not allowed. So none.\n\nS size 3: S={2,3,4} gcd=1, T must be non-empty subset of empty set, impossible. So none.\n\nThus only those two ordered pairs.\n\nNow also consider S size 1, T size 1 (both from {2,3,4}) disjoint, but we already considered T subsets of size >1? Let's verify we didn't miss S={2}, T={3}? T={3} gcd3 not allowed, so no.\n\nThus only 2 ordered pairs.\n\nThus case C contributes 2 assignments.\n\nNow total assignments = case A + case B + case C = 9 + 9 + 2 = 20.\n\nThus answer = 20? But test expects either 10 or 15. So maybe we double counted something? Wait our DP approach counting assignments of each eleme