ll other inputs, which is needed for large arrays. DP O(n * 201^2) = 200*40k=8 million per test. For large test with length maybe 70? Actually the large test arrays have length maybe around 100? The first large test: length maybe 70? Let's count quickly: The array in first large test appears long, maybe 70. 8 million * 70 = 560 million operations? Wait DP runs per element, so for n=70, O(70*40k) = 2.8 million, fine. For n=200, O(200*40k) = 8 million, also fine. So DP should be okay.\n\nWe must implement modulo operations carefully.\n\nNow implement DP. Use Python lists of size max_g+1 = 201 (including 0). We'll use a list of lists. But we need to update dp for each element. We can use two 2D arrays or one and update in place with careful iteration. Since transitions only from previous dp to new dp, we need a fresh array each iteration.\n\nThus:\n\nMOD = 10**9+7\nmax_val = max(nums) maybe up to 200, but we need gcd up to max(nums). But gcd of any subset can be any divisor of any number up to 200. So we can use range(0,201).\n\nInitialize dp = [[0]*201 for _ in range(201)].\ndp[0][0] = 1\n\nFor each x in nums:\n    dp_next = [[0]*201 for _ in range(201)]\n    for g1 in range(201):\n        row = dp[g1]\n        for g2 in range(201):\n            val = row[g2]\n            if val == 0: continue\n            # assign to none\n            dp_next[g1][g2] = (dp_next[g1][g2] + val) % MOD\n            # assign to S\n            new_g1 = x if g1 == 0 else math.gcd(g1, x)\n            dp_next[new_g1][g2] = (dp_next[new_g1][g2] + val) % MOD\n            # assign to T\n            new_g2 = x if g2 == 0 else math.gcd(g2, x)\n            dp_next[g1][new_g2] = (dp_next[g1][new_g2] + val) % MOD\n    dp = dp_next\n\nAfter processing all elements, answer = sum(dp[g][g] for g in range(1, 201)) % MOD\n\nNote: dp includes states where S or T empty (g=0). At the end, we only sum where g>0 (non-empty). So answer is correct.\n\nLet's test this DP for small examples.\n\nTest [1,1,1,1] manua