ach element can only be assigned to one of the three categories: none, A, or B. So we need to compute new dp from old dp: new_dp[g1][g2] = dp[g1][g2] (assign none) + dp[prev_g1][prev_g2] contributions for assign to A and assign to B. Since assign none just retains dp.\n\nThus we can copy dp to new_dp initially (i.e., new_dp = [row[:] for row in dp]) then for each (g1,g2) where dp[g1][g2] > 0, we compute:\n\n# assign to A:\nnew_g1 = gcd_table[g1][val] if g1 != 0 else val (but gcd_table handles 0)\nnew_dp[new_g1][g2] = (new_dp[new_g1][g2] + dp[g1][g2]) % MOD\n\n# assign to B:\nnew_g2 = gcd_table[g2][val]\nnew_dp[g1][new_g2] = (new_dp[g1][new_g2] + dp[g1][g2]) % MOD\n\nBut careful: When we copy dp to new_dp, we have already accounted for assign none. Then adding contributions for assign to A and B.\n\nPotential double counting: Since we use dp (old) to compute contributions, it's fine.\n\nThus after processing all elements, dp contains counts of assignments for all possible (g1,g2). Then answer = sum_{g=1..200} dp[g][g] (both non-empty). But dp includes assignments where A empty (g1=0) and B empty (g2=0). So we ignore those.\n\nNow test for [1,1,1,1] yields dp[1][1] = 50. Good.\n\nNow test for [1,2,3,4] yields dp[1][1] = 10. Good.\n\nNow test for [7,7] yields dp[7][7] = 2.\n\nNow test for other cases. Let's test quickly with mental approximations: [10,20,30] expected 2. Let's compute using DP manually: Values [10,20,30] each divisible by 10? Actually gcds: 10,20,30. Let's compute all assignments. But DP will produce something; let's verify quickly.\n\nSubsets with gcd values: \n- {10}: gcd=10\n- {20}: gcd=20\n- {30}: gcd=30\n- {10,20}: gcd=10\n- {10,30}: gcd=10\n- {20,30}: gcd=10 (gcd(20,30)=10)\n- {10,20,30}: gcd=10\n\nThus subsets with gcd10: masks 1 (10), 3 (10,20), 5 (10,30), 6 (20,30), 7 (all). Subsets with gcd20: mask2 (20). Subsets with gcd30: mask4 (30). So only pairs where both have gcd10 are valid. Count ordered pairs of disjoint subsets with gcd10.\n\nList s