nce we have 1 flip left). But we could also flip a zero to 1 to split further? Actually we have 1 flip left after flipping both ends to 0. That flip can split the zero run into 2 pieces => total pieces = 2 (since flipping a zero yields a single 1, splitting zeros into two runs). So total pieces = 2, not good.\n\nAlternatively, we could flip only one of the ends, say flip leading 1 to 0, merging with zeros to length 999, then flip 2 zeros inside to split into 3 pieces: total pieces = 3? Actually after merging, we have zeros length 999. Flipping two zeros to 1 splits into three pieces. So total pieces = 3, max piece length = ceil(999/3) = 333. Not 249.\n\nThus answer 249 is not reachable with my reasoning. Let's compute expected answer maybe from test: \"1\"+long_zeros[1:-1]+\"1\" means string: leading 1, then zeros from index 1 to -2 (so length = 998 zeros), then trailing 1. Yes length 1000. k=3. According to test expects answer 249.\n\nMaybe the DP solution will produce answer 249. Let's try to reason if it's possible.\n\nWe need to produce final string t with max run length <= 249 using at most 3 flips. Original has zeros run length 998, plus two ones at ends.\n\nWe can flip up to 3 characters. Let's think of a target t that has max run length 249. That means any consecutive same bits must be at most 249.\n\nWe need to modify the string such that we break the zeros into runs of length <= 249. With zeros run length 998, we need at least ceil(998/249) = 4 pieces of zeros. That means we need at least 3 break points (i.e., flips of zeros to 1) to create 4 pieces. However we also have two ones at ends that can serve as break points: the ones at both ends are already ones, but they separate zeros? Actually zeros are sandwiched between ones, so there are already break points at both ends (the ones). So zeros run is bounded by ones at both sides. To split zeros into 4 pieces, we need 3 flips of zeros to 1. That's exactly k=3. So we can flip three zeros to 1, creating patte