r v_i or ~v_i. After that, we want to minimize sum over columns of min(count of 1s in that column, H - count). Equivalent to: for each column j, we want the count of 1s to be as close to 0 or H as possible (i.e., either all 0 or all 1), whichever yields lower min, but we can flip column later to make it 0. Actually after row flips, we can also flip columns to minimize sum. So effectively for each column j, after row flips, we can decide to flip column if cnt_j > H/2, making the number of 1s become H - cnt_j (which would be <= H/2). So the final number of 1s in column j after both row and column flips is min(cnt_j, H - cnt_j). So we want to minimize sum_j min(cnt_j, H - cnt_j). This is essentially the sum of the minority bits per column after row flips.\n\nThus, the row flips should be chosen to make each column as \"biased\" as possible (i.e., have as many bits the same as possible), so that the minority count is minimal. That is, we want for each column j, to have either many 0s or many 1s, whichever yields less minority.\n\nThus, we want to maximize for each column the absolute difference between count of ones and zeros. Equivalent to maximizing sum_j |cnt_j - (H - cnt_j)| = sum_j |2*cnt_j - H|. Since H is fixed, maximizing sum_j |2*cnt_j - H| is the same as maximizing sum_j |cnt_j - H/2|.\n\nNow, each row i contributes to each column j as either bit_i_j (if we don't flip) or 1 - bit_i_j (if we flip). So flipping row i toggles its contributions in all columns.\n\nThus, we have a vector of contributions for each row: original bits b_i = (b_i1, ..., b_iW). Flipping row i yields contributions (1 - b_i1, ..., 1 - b_iW). So we can think of each row i as having two possible contribution vectors: v_i = b_i, and ~v_i = 1 - b_i. Choose one for each row to maximize sum_j |2*cnt_j - H|. Hard.\n\nBut W small, so we could treat each column independently if we enumerate all possible row flip patterns? Since H can be large, we can't enumerate all subsets. However, perhaps we can