(arr)\n    pref = [0]*(n+1)\n    seen = set()\n    for i in range(n):\n        seen.add(arr[i])\n        pref[i+1] = len(seen)\n    suff = [0]*(n+1)\n    seen = set()\n    for i in range(n-1, -1, -1):\n        seen.add(arr[i])\n        suff[i] = len(seen)\n    max_sum = 0\n    # iterate first cut i from 1 to n-2 inclusive\n    for i in range(1, n-1):\n        left = pref[i]\n        freq_mid = {}\n        distinct_mid = 0\n        # j from i+1 to n-1 inclusive (second cut)\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            val = arr[j-1]  # element added to middle\n            cnt = freq_mid.get(val, 0)\n            if cnt == 0:\n                distinct_mid += 1\n            freq_mid[val] = cnt + 1\n            right = suff[j]\n            total = left + distinct_mid + right\n            if total > max_sum:\n                max_sum = total\n    return max_sum\n\nCheck if it yields correct answer for sample.\n\nLet's test manually:\n\nExample [3,1,4,1,5] length 5. Pref: [0,1,2,3,3,4] (distinct after each index). Suffix: compute: index 4: {5} -> 1; 3: {1,5} ->2; 2: {4,1,5}->3; 1: {1,4,5}->3; 0: {3,1,4,5}->4. So suff: [4,3,3,2,1,0]. For i=1 left=1 (arr[0]), freq_mid empty, distinct_mid=0. j=2: add arr[1]=1 distinct_mid=1; right=suff[2]=3 => sum=1+1+3=5 -> max=5. j=3: add arr[2]=4 distinct_mid=2; right=suff[3]=2 sum=1+2+2=5. j=4: add arr[3]=1 distinct_mid stays=2; right=suff[4]=1 sum=1+2+1=4. i=2 left=pref[2]=2. freq_mid empty. j=3 add arr[2]=4 distinct_mid=1; right=suff[3]=2 sum=2+1+2=5. j=4 add arr[3]=1 distinct_mid=2; right=suff[4]=1 sum=2+2+1=5. i=3 left=pref[3]=3. j=4 add arr[3]=1 distinct_mid=1; right=suff[4]=1 sum=3+1+1=5. So max=5 matches.\n\nSecond test [2,5,6,4,4,1,1,3,1,4] length 10. I suspect it will yield 9.\n\nSo the algorithm seems correct.\n\nEdge Cases: array of all same numbers e.g., [2,2,2,...]. Pref distinct: 0,1,1,...; suff distinct: 1,...,0. The best split: each part gets distinct count 1, sum=3. That matches test case expecting 3.\n\nNow we must consi