luded.\n\ni=3 (first part arr[:3] distinct=3)\nmid_seen empty.\nj=4: add arr[3]=1 -> mid_seen={1}. total=3+1+suffix[4]=1=>5\nj=5 not included.\n\nThus max=5. Good.\n\nNow test [2,5,6,4,4,1,1,3,1,4] length 10. Let's manually compute maybe.\n\nImplementation seems correct.\n\nNow note that we need to return int.\n\nNow we should also consider potential integer overflow? Not relevant.\n\nNow we need to deliver code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The function uses List[int] from typing. Should import typing.\n\nNow maybe there is a faster approach: Actually O(n^2) should pass. Let's see if any test has array length > 2000? The largest test appears to have about 200 numbers. So O(n^2) is fine.\n\nNow double-check suffix[?] for j = n (i.e., j = n). In loops, j goes up to n-1 inclusive. For j = n-1, suffix[j] = distinct count of arr[n-1:] = last element only. That's fine.\n\nNow ensure prefix[i] is correct for i from 1 to n-2 inclusive. We compute prefix[0] = 0 (unused). prefix[i] for i = length of first part.\n\nNow code.\n\nPotential issues: Using set inside loops can cause overhead but fine.\n\nNow, we need to include docstring same as original? Could leave.\n\nNow write final answer.\n\nBut note: The tests include many cases with longer arrays, maybe up to 200+ length. O(n^2) = 40k, fine.\n\nNow ensure we treat empty suffix correctly. In loops, j always <= n-1, so suffix[j] is defined. No need for suffix[n] = 0.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Compute n.\n2. Compute prefix distinct counts: prefix = [0] * (n+1). Use a set. For i in range(1, n+1): add arr[i-1] to set, prefix[i] = len(set).\n3. Compute suffix distinct counts: suffix = [0] * (n+1). Use set. For i in reversed(range(n)): add arr[i] to set, suffix[i] = len(set). suffix[n] = 0 (not used but set).\n4. Initialize max_sum = 0.\n5. For i in range(1, n-1):\n    mid_seen = set()\n    for j in range(i+1, n):\n        mid_seen.add(arr[j-1])\n        total = prefix[i] + len(mid_seen) + suffix[j]\n        if total > max_sum: max_sum = total\n6. Return max_sum.\n\nNow consider potential bug: prefix[i] includes arr[:i] distinct count, but i is length. That's correct. suffix[j] includes arr[j:] distinct count, correct.\n\nEdge Cases: n=3. prefix[1] = distinct arr[0], suffix[2] = distinct arr[2] => works.\n\nNow test some cases manually.\n\nCase [2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2] length 10. All elements same.\n\nPrefix distinct:\ni=1 -> 1, i>=2 ->1.\nSuffix distinct:\nj=9 ->1, j<=8->1, suffix[0]=1.\nNow loops:\ni=1: mid_seen empty. j=2: add arr[1]=2, mid_seen={2} len=1. total = prefix[1]=1 + 1 + suffix[2]=1 =>3. max=3.\nAny other i yields same total? For i=2: prefix[2]=1, j=3: mid_seen add arr[2]=2 => len=1, suffix[3]=1 => total=3.\nThus max=3. Expected output for that test is 3. Good.\n\nNow test [3,2,1] length 3. prefix[1]=1 (distinct of [3]), prefix[2]=2 (distinct of [3,2]), suffix[2]=1 (distinct of [1]), suffix[1]=2 (distinct of [2,1]), suffix[0]=3.\n\nLoop:\ni=1 (first part [3] distinct=1). mid_seen empty. j=2: add arr[1]=2 -> mid_seen={2} len=1. total = prefix[1]=1 + 1 + suffix[2]=1 =>3. max=3.\ni=2 (first part [3,2] distinct=2). j=3 not allowed (range i+1,n = 3, but range(3,3) empty). So only i=1 yields max=3. Expected output is 3. Good.\n\nCase [4,5,6,3,2,2] length 6. Let's compute expected 6.\n\nWe can test manually after code.\n\nNow check more complex case: [1,1,2,2] length 4. Expected 4.\n\nCompute prefix: i=1: {1}=1, i=2:{1}=1, i=3:{1,2}=2, i=4:{1,2}=2.\nSuffix: j=3 (index 3): arr[3]=2 => distinct=1, j=2 arr[2:]=[2,2] => distinct=1, j=1 arr[1:]=[1,2,2] distinct=2, j=0 arr[0:]=[1,1,2,2] distinct=2.\n\nNow loops:\ni=1 (first part arr[:1]=[1] distinct=1). mid_seen empty.\nj=2: add arr[1]=1 -> mid_seen={1}. total=1+1+suffix[2]=1 =>3.\nj=3: add arr[2]=2 -> mid_seen={1,2}. total=1+2+suffix[3]=1 =>4 =>max=4.\ni=2 (first part arr[:2]=[1,1] distinct=1). mid_seen empty.\nj=3: add arr[2]=2 -> mid_seen={2} len=1. total=1+1+suffix[3]=1 =>3.\ni=3 (first part arr[:3]=[1,1,2] distinct=2). mid_seen empty. j=4