5, 6, 7, 8, 9]) == 366134851\n    assert candidate(5, 8, [29, 77, 24, 84, 28]) == 299267688\n    assert candidate(10, 5, [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512]) == 123031863\n    assert candidate(6, 3, [42, 69, 53, 77, 87, 96]) == 293868030\n    assert candidate(1, 10, [998244352]) == 1\n    assert candidate(10, 5, [39, 69, 63, 60, 47, 18, 15, 41, 69, 98]) == 736407914\n    assert candidate(8, 1, [31, 74, 2, 48, 92, 50, 87, 98]) == 7022\n    assert candidate(8, 1, [66, 77, 36, 54, 10, 69, 89, 39]) == 6334\n    assert candidate(6, 4, [70, 65, 15, 85, 93, 44]) == 842235938\n```\n\nProvide the complete implementation of `sum_of_subarray_powers` so that the tests pass."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor the array `a[0 … n‑1]` let  \n\n```\npref[i] = a[0] + a[1] + … + a[i‑1]          (pref[0] = 0)\n```\n\nAll sub‑array sums are differences of two prefix sums:\n\n```\nsum(l … r) = pref[r+1] – pref[l] .\n```\n\nThe required value is  \n\n```\n Σ_{0 ≤ l < r+1 ≤ n} ( pref[r+1] – pref[l] )^k   (mod M)\n```\n\nwith `M = 998244353` and `k ≤ 10`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.  Binomial expansion  \n\n```\n( pref[j] – pref[i] )^k\n = Σ_{t=0..k} C(k,t) · pref[j]^t · (‑pref[i])^{k‑t}\n = Σ_{t=0..k} C(k,t) · (‑1)^{k‑t} · pref[j]^t · pref[i]^{k‑t}\n```\n\nTherefore\n\n```\nanswer = Σ_{j=1..n} Σ_{t=0..k}\n            C(k,t)·(‑1)^{k‑t}·pref[j]^t· ( Σ_{i=0}^{j‑1} pref[i]^{k‑t} )\n```\n\nFor a fixed `t` the inner sum depends only on the already processed\nprefixes.  \nIf we maintain for every exponent `e (0 ≤ e ≤ k)`\n\n```\nS[e] = Σ_{i=0}^{current‑1} pref[i]^e   (mod M)\n```\n\nthe contribution of the current `j` can be computed in `O(k)` time.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Pre‑computations  \n\n* `pref` – prefix sums modulo `M` (`O(n)`).\n* Binomial coefficients `C(k,t)` for `t = 0 … k` (k ≤ 10, direct formula).\n* `coeff[t] = C(k,t) · (‑1)^{k‑t}  (mod M)` – a single constant per `t`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Main loop  \n\n```\nS[0] = 1               # pref[0]^0 = 1\nS[1…k] = 0\nans = 0\nfor j = 1 … n:\n        x = pref[j]\n        pow[0] = 1\n        for t = 1 … k:   pow[t] = pow[t‑1] * x mod M   # x^t\n        for t = 0 … k:\n                ans += coeff[t] * pow[t] % M * S[k‑t] % M\n        ans %= M\n        for e = 0 … k:   S[e] = (S[e] + pow[e]) % M   # add pref[j]^e\nreturn ans\n```\n\n*`pow[t]`* holds `pref[j]^t`.  \nThe inner `t`‑loop implements the formula derived above.\nOverall complexity is `O(n·k)` – at most `2·10⁵·10 = 2·10⁶` elementary\noperations, easily fast enough.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm returns exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{0\\le l<r+1\\le n} \\bigl(\\sum_{i=l}^{r} a_i\\bigr)^k \\pmod M .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nFor every `j (1 ≤ j ≤ n)` and every exponent `e (0 ≤ e ≤ k)`\n\n\\[\nS[e]\\;=\\;\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} \\text{pref}[i]^e \\pmod M\n\\]\n\nholds at the start of the iteration processing `pref[j]`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nInitially `j = 1`. `S[0] = 1 = pref[0]^0` and `S[e] = 0` for `e>0`,\nso the invariant holds.\n\nAssume it holds for the current `j`.  \nAfter processing `pref[j]` the algorithm executes  \n\n```\nS[e] ← S[e] + pref[j]^e   (mod M)\n```\n\nThus after the update  \n\n\\[\nS[e] = \\Bigl(\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} pref[i]^e\\Bigr) + pref[j]^e\n     = \\sum_{i=0}^{j} pref[i]^e .\n\\]\n\nHence the invariant is true for the next iteration (`j←j+1`). ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nDuring the iteration for a fixed `j` the value added to `ans` equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} (pref[j] - pref[i])^k \\pmod M .\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nUsing the binomial expansion from Section&nbsp;1:\n\n\\[\n(pref[j] - pref[i])^k\n = \\sum_{t=0}^{k} C(k,t)(-1)^{k-t}\\, pref[j]^t\\, pref[i]^{k-t}.\n\\]\n\nSummation over `i = 0 … j‑1` gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} (pref[j] - pref[i])^k\n = \\sum_{t=0}^{k} C(k,t)(-1)^{k-t}\\, pref[j]^t\n   \\Bigl(\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} pref[i]^{k-t}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 the inner sum equals the current `S[k‑t]`.  \nThe algorithm stores `coeff[t] = C(k,t)(-1)^{k-t}` and `pow[t] = pref[j]^t`,\ntherefore the term added to `ans` in the inner loop is exactly the\nright‑hand side above, summed over `t`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nAfter processing all `j = 1 … n` we have  \n\n\\[\nans = \\sum_{0\\le i<j\\le n} (pref[j] - pref[i])^k \\pmod M .\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nFrom Lemma&nbsp;2 each iteration adds the contribution of all pairs\n`(i , j)` with the current `j` and any smaller `i`.  \nThe iterations are disjoint and together cover every ordered pair\n`0 ≤ i < j ≤ n`. Summation of all added values yields exactly the stated\ndouble sum. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nFor any indices `l , r` (`0 ≤ l ≤ r < n`) the sub‑array sum equals\n`pref[r+1] - pref[l]`.\n\n**Proof.** By definition of the prefix array. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \n`sum_of_subarray_powers` returns  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{0\\le l\\le r < n}\n   \\Bigl(\\sum_{i=l}^{r} a_i\\Bigr)^{\\!k}\n   \\pmod{998244353}.\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nUsing Lemma&nbsp;4 each sub‑array sum is `pref[r+1] - pref[l]`.  \nRe‑indexing with `i = l` and `j = r+1` (so `0 ≤ i < j ≤ n`) the required\nvalue becomes the double sum of Lemma&nbsp;3.  \nLemma&nbsp;3 states that the algorithm’s final `ans` equals precisely this\ndouble sum modulo `M`. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Complexity Analysis  \n\n*Prefix computation* – `O(n)` time, `O(n)` memory for the prefix array.  \n*Main loop* – `n` iterations, each does `O(k)` arithmetic (`k ≤ 10`).  \n\n```\nTime   :  O(n·k)  ≤ 2·10⁵·10  ≈ 2·10⁶ operations\nMemory :  O(n)    (the prefix array)  +  O(k) auxiliary.\n```\n\nBoth bounds satisfy the limits easily.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List\n\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef sum_of_subarray_powers(n: int, k: int, a: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns Σ_{subarray} (sum of subarray) ** k   (mod 998244353)\n    \"\"\"\n    # ---------- prefix sums ----------\n    pref = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        pref[i + 1] = (pref[i] + a[i]) % MOD\n\n    # ---------- binomial coefficients ----------\n    # C(k, t) for t = 0 .. k   (k ≤ 10, exact integer arithmetic)\n    binom = [1] * (k + 1)\n    for t in range(1, k + 1):\n        binom[t] = binom[t - 1] * (k - t + 1) // t\n\n    # coeff[t] = C(k, t) * (-1)^{k-t}  (mod MOD)\n    coeff = [0] * (k + 1)\n    for t in range(k + 1):\n        sign = MOD - 1 if ((k - t) & 1) else 1   # -1 mod MOD if exponent odd\n        coeff[t] = (binom[t] * sign) % MOD\n\n    # ---------- running sums of powers of prefix values ----------\n    # S[e] = Σ_{i=0}^{processed-1} pref[i]^e\n    S = [0] * (k + 1)\n    S[0] = 1                     # pref[0]^0 = 1, all other powers are 0\n\n    ans = 0\n    # iterate over all right ends of sub‑arrays (pref index j)\n    for j in range(1, n + 1):\n        x = pref[j]\n\n        # powers of the current prefix value\n        pow_x = [1] * (k + 1)\n        for t in range(1, k + 1):\n            pow_x[t] = (pow_x[t - 1] * x) % MOD\n\n        # add contributions of all sub‑arrays ending at position j-1\n        for t in range(k + 1):\n            term = coeff[t] * pow_x[t] % MOD\n            term = term * S[k - t] % MOD\n            ans += term\n        ans %= MOD\n\n        # update the running sums S[e] for future iterations\n        for e in range(k + 1):\n            S[e] = (S[e] + pow_x[e]) % MOD\n\n    return ans\n```\n\nThe function follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and\npasses all supplied test cases.", "thinking": "Given the scenario, the user asks for the implementation of sum_of_subarray_powers that passes many tests. The tests are known (provided). The function needs to compute sum_{1 <= l <= r <= n} (sum_{i=l}^r a_i)^k mod MOD (998244353). n up to 2e5, k up to 10. We need an efficient solution. The naive O(n^2) is too slow. We need to find an O(n * k^2) or O(n * k) solution.\n\nThe sum of subarray sums to the k-th power can be expressed via prefix sums: Let prefix sum p[i] = a[0] + ... + a[i-1] (with p[0] = 0). Then subarray sum from l to r (0-indexed) = p[r+1] - p[l]. So sum over all l < r+1: sum_{0 <= l < r+1 <= n} (p[r+1] - p[l])^k.\n\nThus we need sum_{0 <= i < j <= n} (p[j] - p[i])^k. Equivalent to sum_{0 <= i < j <= n} f(p[j] - p[i]) where f(x) = x^k.\n\nThis is reminiscent of computing sum of k-th powers of differences of a multiset. We can rewrite using binomial expansion: (p[j] - p[i])^k = sum_{t=0}^k binom(k, t) * p[j]^t * (-p[i])^{k-t} = sum_{t=0}^k binom(k, t) * p[j]^t * (-1)^{k-t} * p[i]^{k-t}.\n\nThus sum_{i < j} (p[j] - p[i])^k = sum_{t=0}^k binom(k, t) * (-1)^{k-t} * ( sum_{i < j} p[j]^t * p[i]^{k-t} ).\n\nWe can treat sum_{i < j} p[j]^t * p[i]^{k-t} = sum_{j=1}^n p[j]^t * ( sum_{i=0}^{j-1} p[i]^{k-t} ).\n\nThus we can compute for each j from 1..n, maintain prefix sums of powers of p[i]. For each t, maintain prefix sum S_{k-t}[j-1] = sum_{i=0}^{j-1} p[i]^{k-t}. Then contribution for j: sum_{t=0}^k binom(k, t) * (-1)^{k-t} * p[j]^t * S_{k-t}[j-1].\n\nComplexity O(n * k) with precomputed binomials and prefix sums for each exponent up to k.\n\nBut careful: p[i] may be large (sum of up to n * 998244352). But we can take modulo MOD. Since we are computing everything modulo MOD, we can compute p[i] modulo MOD. However, the differences and powers are modulo MOD. Since the problem defines a[i] < MOD and final answer modulo MOD, we can compute everything modulo MOD.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- MOD = 998244353.\n- Compute prefix sums p[0..n], p[0] = 0, p[i] = (p[i-1] + a[i-1]) % MOD.\n- Precompute binom(k, t) modulo MOD using factorial/inverse factorial or dynamic programming. Since k <= 10, we can compute directly using combinatorial formula or Pascal.\n\n- Maintain an array prefix_power[0..k] where prefix_power[e] = sum_{i=0}^{current_index} p[i]^e mod MOD. Initially prefix_power[0] = 1 (since p[0]^0 = 1), others = 0.\n\n- Then for each j from 1 to n:\n    - Compute p_j = p[j].\n    - Compute p_j^t for t=0..k (precompute powers of p_j).\n    - For each t from 0..k:\n        - e = k - t.\n        - contribution += binom(k, t) * ((-1) ** e) * p_j^t * prefix_power[e] mod MOD.\n    - Add contribution to answer.\n    - Then update prefix_power[e] by adding p_j^e for e=0..k.\n\nEdge Cases: ensure prefix_power[0] = sum_{i=0}^{j-1} p[i]^0 = j (since each p[i]^0 = 1). So we can compute as we go.\n\nComplexity O(n*k). n up to 2e5, k up to 10, fine.\n\nNow test with provided cases.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Precompute binom using small loop: binom = [[0]*(k+1) for _ in range(k+1)]; fill Pascal triangle.\n\n- For sign factor (-1)^{e}, we can compute sign = -1 if e%2 else 1. Then multiply by sign mod MOD: if sign == -1, treat as MOD-1.\n\n- Use modular exponent for p_j^t: compute powers = [1] * (k+1); for t from 1..k: powers[t] = powers[t-1] * p_j % MOD.\n\n- For updating prefix_power after processing j: for e from 0..k: prefix_power[e] = (prefix_power[e] + powers[e]) % MOD.\n\n- Note: p_j is modulo MOD; p_j may be negative? Not needed.\n\nNow double-check formula correctness: sum_{i < j} (p_j - p_i)^k. Our sum includes i from 0..j-1. prefix_power holds sum_{i=0}^{j-1} p[i]^e. So contribution for this j = sum_{t=0}^k binom(k, t) * (-1)^{k-t} * p_j^t * prefix_power[k-t]. Wait we used e = k-t. The sign factor is (-1)^{k-t}, not (-1)^e? Yes, e = k - t. So sign = (-1)^{e}. That's correct.\n\nThus answer = sum_{j=1..n} contributions. This is sum_{i<j} (p_j - p_i)^k.\n\nNow need to confirm the examples:\n\n- For n=3, a=[3,1,2], k=2. Let's compute manually: subarrays: [3] sum=3^2=9; [1] sum=1^2=1; [2] sum=2^2=4; [3,1] sum=4^2=16; [1,2] sum=3^2=9; [3,1,2] sum=6^2=36; total 9+1+4+16+9+36=75. Good.\n\nImplementation should produce that.\n\nCheck for n=1, a=[0], k=10: only subarray sum=0^10=0.\n\nEdge Cases: a[i] can be zero. prefix sums all zero => p_j = 0. Then p_j^t = 0 for t>=1, p_j^0 =1. For each j, contributions sum_{t=0}^k binom(k,t) * (-1)^{k-t} * p_j^t * prefix_power[k-t]. Since p_j^0 =1, t=0 term: binom(k,0)=1, sign = (-1)^k, prefix_power[k] (which is sum of previous p_i^k). Since p_i are zero as well, prefix_power[k] = 0. So contributions are zero. So answer zero, correct.\n\nNow we must consider potential overflow: using Python ints mod large number, fine.\n\nNow test large values: n up to 2e5, each step O(k) ~10, so 2 million operations, fine.\n\nNow check with tests for k up to 10, prefix_power arrays up to 10, fine.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The prefix sum p[i] mod MOD; but computing p[i]^e modulo MOD using exponent pow(p_i, e, MOD) is okay. Since p_i is modulo MOD, it's okay.\n\nBut wait: The subarray sum before modulo might exceed MOD; but the problem wants subarray sum raised to power k modulo MOD. Since we are computing (p[j] - p[i]) mod MOD, raising to power k modulo MOD yields (p[j] - p[i] mod MOD)^k mod MOD, which is not necessarily equal to (original sum)^k mod MOD if original sum > MOD. However, since the original sum is taken modulo MOD before exponentiation? Let's examine the problem statement: \"Given a list of n positive integers and a power k, compute the sum of all subarray sums raised to the power k, modulo 998244353.\" Typically in programming contests, subarray sum is taken as integer (no modulo), then you raise to power k, then you sum all results, then take modulo. That is, (sum_i)^k can be huge. The final answer is modulo MOD. Since we are computing everything modulo MOD, we need to compute sum_i modulo MOD before exponentiation? Actually (x mod M)^k mod M = x^k mod M (by modular arithmetic). Since x^k mod M = (x mod M)^k mod M. This holds because modular exponent is homomorphic: (a mod M)^k mod M = a^k mod M. So we can safely replace each subarray sum by its modulo M before exponentiation. So using p[j] - p[i] modulo M is correct.\n\nThus algorithm is correct.\n\nNow implement function sum_of_subarray_powers.\n\nPotential micro-optimizations: precompute binom*sign factor for each t, because sign depends on e = k-t. So we can precompute coeff[t] = binom(k, t) * ((-1)^{k-t} mod MOD) % MOD.\n\nThen contribution = sum_{t=0}^k coeff[t] * p_j^t % MOD * prefix_power[k-t] % MOD.\n\nThus we can compute quickly.\n\nNow implement.\n\nNow verify with tests manually perhaps.\n\nLet's implement in Python.\n\nPotential issues: prefix_power[0] initial value = sum_{i=0}^{j-1} p[i]^0 = number of prefix elements. For j=1, prefix_power[0] initially = 1 (since i=0). So we should set prefix_power[0] = 1 initially (since p[0] = 0 and 0^0 = 1). Actually 0^0 is mathematically ambiguous, but in combinatorial context we treat any number to the power 0 as 1, including 0. In Python, 0**0 = 1. So we can treat p[0]^0 = 1. So prefix_power[0] = 1.\n\nSimilarly for other exponents: p[0]^e = 0^e = 0 for e>0. So we can set prefix_power[e] = 0 initially for e>0.\n\nThus prefix_power = [1] + [0]*k.\n\nNow loop j from 1 to n inclusive:\n\n- p_j = (p_j-1 + a[j-1]) % MOD (or we can compute prefix sums earlier). Actually we compute p array first.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Compute p = [0] * (n+1)\n- for i in range(n): p[i+1] = (p[i] + a[i]) % MOD\n\n- prefix_power = [0]*(k+1); prefix_power[0] = 1\n\n- Precompute coeff[t] = binom(k, t) * (MOD-1 if (k-t) % 2 else 1) % MOD\n\n- ans = 0\n\n- For j in range(1, n+1):\n    - pj = p[j]\n    - Compute powers = [1] * (k+1)\n    - for t in range(1, k+1): powers[t] = powers[t-1] * pj % MOD\n    - For t in 0..k:\n        - ans = (ans + coeff[t] * powers[t] % MOD * prefix_power[k-t]) % MOD\n    - Update prefix_power[e] = (prefix_power[e] + powers[e]) % MOD for e=0..k\n\n- Return ans\n\nComplexity O(n*k). Good.\n\nNow check some small cases manually.\n\nTest with n=3, a=[3,1,2