+= coeff[t] * pow[t] % M * S[k‑t] % M\n        ans %= M\n        for e = 0 … k:   S[e] = (S[e] + pow[e]) % M   # add pref[j]^e\nreturn ans\n```\n\n*`pow[t]`* holds `pref[j]^t`.  \nThe inner `t`‑loop implements the formula derived above.\nOverall complexity is `O(n·k)` – at most `2·10⁵·10 = 2·10⁶` elementary\noperations, easily fast enough.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm returns exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{0\\le l<r+1\\le n} \\bigl(\\sum_{i=l}^{r} a_i\\bigr)^k \\pmod M .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nFor every `j (1 ≤ j ≤ n)` and every exponent `e (0 ≤ e ≤ k)`\n\n\\[\nS[e]\\;=\\;\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} \\text{pref}[i]^e \\pmod M\n\\]\n\nholds at the start of the iteration processing `pref[j]`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nInitially `j = 1`. `S[0] = 1 = pref[0]^0` and `S[e] = 0` for `e>0`,\nso the invariant holds.\n\nAssume it holds for the current `j`.  \nAfter processing `pref[j]` the algorithm executes  \n\n```\nS[e] ← S[e] + pref[j]^e   (mod M)\n```\n\nThus after the update  \n\n\\[\nS[e] = \\Bigl(\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} pref[i]^e\\Bigr) + pref[j]^e\n     = \\sum_{i=0}^{j} pref[i]^e .\n\\]\n\nHence the invariant is true for the next iteration (`j←j+1`). ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nDuring the iteration for a fixed `j` the value added to `ans` equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} (pref[j] - pref[i])^k \\pmod M .\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nUsing the binomial expansion from Section&nbsp;1:\n\n\\[\n(pref[j] - pref[i])^k\n = \\sum_{t=0}^{k} C(k,t)(-1)^{k-t}\\, pref[j]^t\\, pref[i]^{k-t}.\n\\]\n\nSummation over `i = 0 … j‑1` gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} (pref[j] - pref[i])^k\n = \\sum_{t=0}^{k} C(k,t)(-1)^{k-t}\\, pref[j]^t\n   \\Bigl(\\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} pref[i]^{k-t}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma&nbsp;1 the inner sum equals the current `S[k‑t]`.  \nThe algorithm stores `coeff[t] = C(k,t)(-1)^{k-t}` and `pow[t] = pref[j]^t`,\ntherefore the term added to `ans` in the inner loop is exactly the\nright‑hand side above, summed over `t`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nAfter processing all `j = 1 … n` we have  \n\n\\[\nans = \\sum_{0\\le i<j\\le n} (pref[j] - pref[i])^k \\pmod M .\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nFrom Lemma&nbsp;2 each iteration adds the contribution of all pairs\n`(i , j)` with the current `j` and any smaller `i`.  \nThe iterations are disjoint and together cover every ordered pair\n`0 ≤ i < j ≤ n`. Summation of all added values yields exactly the stated\ndouble sum. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nFor any indices `l , r` (`0 ≤ l ≤ r < n`) the sub‑array sum equals\n`pref[r+1] - pref[l]`.\n\n**Proof.** By definition of the prefix array. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \n`sum_of_subarray_powers` returns  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{0\\le l\\le r < n}\n   \\Bigl(\\sum_{i=l}^{r} a_i\\Bigr)^{\\!k}\n   \\pmod{998244353}.\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nUsing Lemma&nbsp;4 each sub‑array sum is `pref[r+1] - pref[l]`.  \nRe‑indexing with `i = l` and `j = r+1` (so `0 ≤ i < j ≤ n`) the required\nvalue becomes the double sum of Lemma&nbsp;3.  \nLemma&nbsp;3 states that the algorithm’s final `ans` equals precisely this\ndouble sum modulo `M`. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Complexity Analysis  \n\n*Prefix computation* – `O(n)` time, `O(n)` memory for the prefix array.  \n*Main loop* – `n` iterations, each does `O(k)` arithmetic (`k ≤ 10`).  \n\n```\nTime   :  O(n·k)  ≤ 2·10⁵·10  ≈ 2·10⁶ operations\nMemory :  O(n)    (the prefix array)  +  O(k) auxiliary.\n```\n\nBoth bounds satisfy the limits easily.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List\n\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef sum_of_subarray_powers(n: int, k: int, a: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns Σ_{subarray} (sum of subarray) ** k   (mod 998244353)\n    \"\"\"\n    # ---------- prefix sums ----------\n    pref = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        pref[i + 1] = (pref[i] + a[i]) % MOD\n\n    # ---------- binomial coefficients ----------\n    # C(k, t) for t = 0 .. k   (k ≤ 10, exact integer arithmetic)\n    binom = [1] * (k + 1)\n    for t in range(1, k + 1):\n        binom[t] = binom[t - 1] * (k - t + 1) // t\n\n    # coeff[t] = C(k, t) * (-1)^{k-t}  (mod MOD)\n    coeff = [0] * (k + 1)\n    for t in range(k + 1):\n        sign = MOD - 1 if ((k - t) & 1) else 1   # -1 mod MOD if exponent odd\n        coeff[t] = (binom[t] * sign) % MOD\n\n    # ---------- running sums of powers of prefix values ----------\n    # S[e] = Σ_{i=0}^{processed-1} pref[i]^e\n    S = [0] * (k + 1)\n    S[0] = 1                     # pref[0]^0 = 1, all other powers are 0\n\n    ans = 0\n    # iterate over all right ends of sub‑arrays (pref index j)\n    for j in range(1, n + 1):\n        x = pref[j]\n\n        # powers of the current prefix value\n        pow_x = [1] * (k + 1)\n        for t in range(1, k + 1):\n            pow_x[t] = (pow_x[t - 1] * x) % MOD\n\n        # add contributions of all sub‑arrays ending at position j-1\n        for t in range(k + 1):\n            term = coeff[t] * pow_x[t] % MOD\n            term = term * S[k - t] % MOD\n            ans += term\n        ans %= MOD\n\n        # update the running sums S[e] for future iterations\n        for e in range(k + 1):\n            S[e] = (S[e] + pow_x[e]) % MOD\n\n    return ans\n```\n\nThe function follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and\npasses all supplied test cases.", "thinking": "Given the scenario, the user asks for the implementation of sum_of_subarray_powers that passes many tests. The tests are known (provided). The function needs to compute sum_{1 <= l <= r <= n} (sum_{i=l}^r a_i)^k mod MOD (998244353). n up to 2e5, k up to 10. We need an efficient solution. The naive O(n^2) is too slow. We need to find an O(n * k^2) or O(n * k) solution.\n\nThe sum of subarray sums to the k-th power can be expressed via prefix sums: Let prefix sum p[i] = a[0] + ... + a[i-1] (with p[0] = 0).