f x+y <= n, we have possible operations. However, we also need to consider if the pattern can be applied at any position (i.e., there must exist a substring matching pattern). If there is no such substring initially, can we create one via other operations? Possibly. For example, for x=2,y=1, pattern length=3, we can apply to any position where there is \"00\"+\"1\" or \"1\"+\"00\". This may require some arrangement. In general, we may be able to generate pattern positions by moving bits. But maybe there are constraints.\n\nWe need to derive full condition for reachability. This problem seems similar to a known Codeforces problem: \"Can you make the string equal?\" maybe.\n\nWe need to consider the group generated by swapping blocks of sizes x and y. The operations are like transpositions of blocks of zeros and ones. This is akin to a rewriting system. We need to decide if we can rearrange bits arbitrarily given constraints.\n\nWe can think of the process as moving zeros rightwards across ones in increments of y (via operation A) or moving zeros leftwards across ones in increments of x (via operation B). Actually operation A moves zeros rightwards (since zeros are at left of pattern \"0\"*x + \"1\"*y, after swap they become at right). So each zero moves right by y positions. Operation B moves zeros leftwards (since zeros are at right of pattern \"1\"*y + \"0\"*x, after swap they become at left), moving each zero left by y positions? Wait, B swaps \"1\"*y + \"0\"*x into \"0\"*x + \"1\"*y, so zeros move left by y positions? Actually zeros originally at positions i+y..i+y+x-1 (the right block). After swapping, zeros occupy positions i..i+x-1 (the left block). So each zero moves left by y positions. So we have ability to move zeros left by y positions and right by y positions? Wait, we need to compute precisely.\n\nOperation A: pattern \"0\"*x + \"1\"*y at positions [i..i+x+y-1]. After swapping, zeros at [i..i+x-1] move to positions [i+y..i+y+x-1]; i.e., each zero moves 